{{ar-adj|أَشْهَل|f=شَهْلَاء|cpl=شُهْل}}

# {{tlb|ar|obsolete|;|said of [[eye]]s; also used in reference to [[mountain]]s and [[wolf|wolves]] with the meaning "[[dust-coloured]] inclining to [[whiteness]]"<ref name="Lane">{{R:ar:Lane}}</ref>}} {{def-uncertain|ar}}
## [[glaucous]]<ref>{{cite-book|title=The Optics of Ibn al-Haytham: Books I-III: on Direct Vision|year=1989|author=Sabra, A. I.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=WrszAQAAIAAJ|section=part II|page=41|text=It was frequently used as equivalent to the Greek ''glaukos'', grey, blue-grey, blue-green, light blue, etc. (LSJ). In the ''Optics'' it is mentioned as a colour of the eye (or iris) distinguished from blue (''azraq''). [...] / Kamāl al-Dīn, in ''Tanqīḥ'', 1, p. 106, lists ''al-shuhla'' (from ''ashhal'') among four colours of the iris, the other three being dark blue or blue-black (''al-kaḥal''), blue (''azraq'') and ''al-shuʿla''; ''al-shuhla'', he says, is closer to ''al-kaḥal'' while ''al-shuʿla'' is closer to blue. This would seem to make ''ashhal'' something like blush grey, but Kamāl al-Dīn appears to be propounding a theory rather than a description of actual usage.}}</ref><ref name="Maxwell-Stuart, p. 174">{{cite-book|last=Maxwell-Stuart|first=P. G.|year=1981|title=Studies in Greek Colour Terminology: Charopos|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Q4seAAAAIAAJ|publisher=Brill|page=174}}</ref> {{non-gloss|or}} [[subglaucous]];<ref name="Maxwell-Stuart, p. 174"/>
## having "a mixture of colours" (usually blue or red with black),<ref name="Lane"/><ref>{{cite-book|last=Maxwell-Stuart|first=P. G.|year=1981|title=op. cit.|page=175}}</ref> [[variegated]]<ref>{{cite-book|chapter=Glaucoma in the Medieval Arab World|title=The History of Glaucoma|author=Leffler, Christopher T.; Samara, Wasim A.; Hadi, Tamer H.; Salman, Ali; Khan, Faraaz A.|editor=Leffler, Christopher T.|year=2020|publisher=Wayenborgh Publishing|page=56|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NHXlDwAAQBAJ}}</ref> {{non-gloss|(likely from a confusion with {{m|ar|أَشْكَل}} although {{w|Abu Mansur al-Azhari}} considered them to be synonyms)}}:<ref name="Lane"/><ref>{{cite-book|de|first=Wolfdietrich|last=Fischer|title=Farb- und Formbezeichnung in der Sprache der altarabischen Dichtung|chapter=Heller Farbbereich:Rot und Blau|year=1965|page=353|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=b13RAAAAMAAJ|text=Nach den arab. Lexika sind beide etwa synonym. Hinsichtlich der primären Bedeutung von ''aškal'' 'unbestimmt, verschiedenartig gefärbt' scheint dies ungefähr zutreffend zu sein und wird durch einen Lis. III 475 = 2. 635a, 14—15 zitierten V. bestätigt: <br>''mutawaḍḍiḥu l-aqrābi fīhi šuhlatun * šaniǧu l-yadaini taḫāluhū maškūlā''<br>'(ein Kamel ?) an den Flanken hell gefärbt, doch mit Mischfarbigkeit getrübt, an den Vorderbeinen runzelig, so daß man es für ein undeutlich (gefärbtes Tier) hält'.<br>[…] Es wird, wie die Belege zeigen, als Gegensatz zu ''adʿaǧ'' 'schwarzäugig gebraucht', so daß die arab. Lexikographen im unklaren sind, ob bräunliche (d. h. rötliche) oder bläuliche (d. h. schillernde) Augen gemeint sind.}}</ref>
### [[reddish]]-[[brown]], [[dark]]-[[brown]];<ref>{{cite-book|last=Khan|first=Geoffrey|title=Arabic Legal and Administrative Documents in the Cambridge Genizah collections|publisher=Cambridge University Press|year=1993|page=17}}</ref>
### [[bluish]]-[[black]], [[deep blue]];<ref>{{R:ar:Wehr-4}}</ref>
## of "lighter [[shade]]s of [[brown]]"<ref>{{cite-book|last=Maxwell-Stuart|first=P. G.|year=1981|title=op. cit.|page=71}}</ref> {{non-gloss|or}} [[amber]],<ref name="Maxwell-Stuart, p. 174"/> [[tawny]],<ref name="Maxwell-Stuart, p. 174"/> {{non-gloss|or}} [[fawn]]<ref>{{cite-book|last=Aigle|first=Denise|year=2014|title=The Mongol-Empire Between Myth and Reality|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=o44cBQAAQBAJ|publisher=Brill|page=127}}</ref> {{non-gloss|(likely from a confusion with {{m|ar|أَشْعَل}})}}.<ref name="Maxwell-Stuart, p. 174"/>
##* {{Q|ar|Muslim ibn al-Walid|Diwan|45|19|quote=كَسَاهَا الخَلَا الرَسْمِيُّ مِنْ كُلِّ جَانِبٍ ‖ طَرَائِقَ حَتَّى سُودُ حَوْزَاتِهَا '''شُهْلُ'''|t=The rain dresses the yard on every side<br>{{nbsp}}{{nbsp}}{{nbsp}}Paths until the black of nature is '''blue'''|transauthor=Arthur Wormhoudt<ref>{{cite-book|title=The Diwan of Muslim ibn al-Walid, called Sariʻ al Gawani, translated and commented on by Arthur Wormhoudt|year=1981|publisher=William Penn College|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eSMsAAAAMAAJ}}</ref>}}
##* {{Q|ar|Ibn al-Nadim|Al-Fihrist|quote=احد الاسباب فى ذلك ان المأمون رأى فى منامه كان رجلا ابيض اللون مشربا حمرة واسع الجبهة مقرون الحاجب اجلح الرأس '''اشهل''' العينين حسن الشمائل جالس على سريره|t=One of the reasons for that was that al-Ma'mun had a dream. He saw a man white in complexion but with a reddish tinge, a high forehead, close-together eyebrows, bald, with '''grey''' eyes and pleasant features, sitting on his throne.|transauthor={{w|Clifford Edmund Bosworth}}<ref>{{cite-book|author=Bosworth, C. E.|year=1963|title=Medieval Arabic Culture and Administration|page=xv|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BTdtAAAAMAAJ}}</ref>}}
##* {{Q|ar|Ibn Mutarrif al-Tarafi|The Stories of the Prophets|quote=‫ وكانت صفة أيوب صلی الله عليه وسلم أحمر '''أشهل''' ناتی الوجنتين غليظ الخلق||t=Job [peace be upon him] was a man of a reddish complexion, '''blue''' eyes, protuberant cheeks and bulky constitution.|transauthor=Roberto Tottoli<ref>{{cite-journal|title=The ''Qiṣaṣ al-anbiyāʾ'' of Ibn Muṭarrif al-Ṭarafī (d. 454/1062)|journal=Al-Qantara|volume=19|issue=1|first=Roberto|last=Tottoli|year=1998|url=http://al-qantara.revistas.csic.es/index.php/al-qantara/article/download/490/481|page=153}}</ref>}}
##* {{Q|ar|Avicenna|The Canon of Medicine|3|quote=و لهذا السبب تكون عيون الاطفال زرقاء، و '''شهلاء'''، و هذه زرقة تكون عن رطوبة بالغة|transauthor=Christopher T. Leffler, Wasim A. Samara, Tamer H. Hadi, Ali Salman, Faraaz A. Khan<ref>{{cite-book|author=Leffler, Christopher T.; Samara, Wasim A.; Hadi, Tamer H.; Salman, Ali; Khan, Faraaz A.|editor=Leffler, Christopher T.|year=2020|pages=55–56|title=op. cit.}}</ref>|t=In this manner, the eyes of children are glaucous (''zarqaa'') and '''variegated''' (''shahlaa''), and this type of glaucous hue (''zurqa'') is due to the humour completely.}}
##* {{Q|ar||Cairo Geniza|T-S Ar. 51.110|lines 2|thru=5|year=1126|notes=[http://cudl.lib.cam.ac.uk/view/MS-TS-AR-00051-00110/1 original in ''rasm'']|quote=هذا ما استاجر عمر بن موسى الزيات وهو يومئذ رجل معتدل القامة<br>اسمر اللون رقيق السمرة تعلوه حمرا واضح الجبهة صلتها ابلج ما بين الحاجبين <br>ازجهما ابخض '''اشهل''' اخثم الانف غليظه موجن مستدير اللحية<br>ابنة اسمعيل المعروف والدها بالكوهان اليهودية [...]|t=This is what ‘Umar ibn Mūsā, the dealer in oil, who on this day is a man of average stature,<br>pure reddy-brown in complexion, with a smooth broad forehead, with fine eyebrows separated in the middle,<br>with fleshy eye-lids, '''dark brown''' eyes, a wide, thick, nose, prominent cheekbones and a round beard, leased from Ya’’āsa (?)<br>ibnat ’Isma‘īl, whose father is known as al-Kohen, the Jewess. […]|transauthor={{w|Geoffrey Khan}}<ref>{{cite-book|last=Khan|first=Geoffrey|title=op. cit.|year=1993|page=169}}</ref>}}

