===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|ar|weekday}}
From {{der|ar|sem-pro|*ʔil-}}, otherwise {{m|ar|أَيْل}}, {{m|ar|إِيل}}, {{m|ar|إِلّ}}, the most important god in the paganic pantheon—equivalent to the Sun, after which in the Western world the Sunday is named, in others. Origin from “first” above is unlikely since all other pagan weekday names are named after planets and thus indirectly gods and the other {{cog|sem|-}} languages there employ {{m|sem-pro|*ʔaḥad-}}, which would have been retained. Lastly the {{w|Banu Bakr}} and {{w|Taghlib}} tribes actually worshiped a deity called {{m|ar|أَوَال}} or {{m|ar|أُوَال}}.

====Proper noun====
{{ar-proper noun|أَوْل|head2=أَوَّل|m}}

# {{tlb|ar|obsolete}} [[Sunday]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|أَوْل:di|head2=أَوَّل:di|state=ind-def}}

=====See also=====
* {{list:days of the week/ar|sublang=Prestandard}}

====References====
* {{R:ar:Fischer:1896|page=224}}
* {{R:Hommel-Chrest.|page=57}}
* {{R:ar:Rotter:1993|page=116–118}}
* {{R:ar:Wellhausen|page=64}}
* {{RQ:Yaqut|W1|page=395|instead=1}}

