Page 1534 أيها: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{affix|ar|أَيُّ|هَا}}

===Particle===
{{ar-particle|أَيُّهَا}} (''feminine'' {{m|ar|أَيَّتُهَا}})

# {{non-gloss definition|vocative particle preceding a noun used in direct address; only rarely omitted in such contexts; occasionally translated as [[O#Particle|O]] or [[hey]]}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|8|45
|passage=يَا '''أَيُّهَا''' ٱلَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِذَا لَقِيتُمْ فِئَةً فَاثْبُتُوا وَاذْكُرُوا ٱللّٰهَ كَثِيرًا لَعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ
|translation='''O''' you who have attained to faith, when you meet a host in battle, be firm, and remember God often; so that you might attain to a happy state!
|subst=فَاثْبُتُوا/فَ-ٱثْبُتُوا,وَاذْكُرُوا/وَ-ٱذْكُرُوا}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|12|70
|passage=فَلَمَّا جَهَّزَهُم بِجَهَازِهِمْ جَعَلَ ٱلسِّقَايَةَ فِي رَحْلِ أَخِيهِ ثُمَّ أَذَّنَ مُؤَذِّنٌ '''أَيَّتُهَا''' ٱلْعِيرُ إِنَّكُمْ لَسَارِقُونَ
|translation=And when he had provided them with their provisions, he placed the drinking-cup in his brother's camel-pack. And a herald called out: "'''O''' you people of the caravan! Verily, you are thieves!
|subst=فَلَمَّا/فَ-لَمَّا,لَسَارِقُونَ/لَ-سَارِقُونَ}}

====Usage notes====
* The subsequent noun stands in the nominative of the [[determinate state]] and must have the article {{m|ar|ال}} attached to it.
* {{m|ar|أَيُّهَا}} is often used in conjunction with a preceding {{m|ar|يَا}} (this usage is the norm in the {{w|Quran}}, see first usage example above).
* {{m|ar|أَيُّهَا}} is far less common than {{m|ar|يَا}} and reserved for elevated registers. Whereas {{m|ar|يَا}} is often left untranslated in English, {{m|ar|أَيُّهَا}} is used in similar situations to English {{m|en|O}}.
* Unlike {{m|ar|يَا}}, {{m|ar|أَيُّهَا}} inflects for gender. When addressing a female, a group of females, or else anything that is referred to by a grammatically feminine word (see second usage example above), the form {{m|ar|أَيَّتُهَا}} is used.
#: {{ux|ar|'''يَا أَيُّهَا''' ٱلرَّجُلُ / ٱلرَّجُلَانِ / ٱلرِّجَالُ|'''Oh you''' man / two men / men!}}
#: {{ux|ar|'''يَا أَيَّتُهَا''' ٱلْمَرْأَةُ / ٱلْمَرْأَتَانِ / ٱلنِّسَاءُ|'''Oh you''' woman / two women / women!}}

====See also====
* {{l|ar|يَا}}

===Pronoun===
{{ar-pron|أَيِّهَا|f}}

# which one of them.

===References===
* {{R:ar:Fischer|section=§ 157}}
-------- end text --------
Page 1535 أيهقان: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Eruca vesicaria (17112458442).jpg|frameless|right|{{lang|ar|أَيْهُقَان}}|upright=1.0]]
[[File:Eruca vesicaria kz03.jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|أَيْهُقَان}}|upright=0.95]]

===Etymology===
Apparently one of the distortions of the {{bor|ar|arc|-}} borrowing {{m|ar|يَرَقَان|t=mildew, blight}} referencing the pale yellow flowers, consider particularly the variant of that word {{m|ar|رَيْهُقَان}}; such a long, foreign-patterned word was easily mangled in Bedouin mouths, like {{m|ar|عُبَيْثَرَان}} and its variants. Compare also the {{bor|ar|arc|-}} borrowing {{m|ar|بَرْوَق|t=asphodel}} similarly called after that plant’s homologous flowers.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|أَيْهُقَان|أَيْهَقَان|qual2=less common}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|أَيْهُقَان|m|head2=أَيْهَقَان}}

# {{C|ar|Crucifers}} [[arugula]] ({{taxfmt|Eruca vesicaria|species}})
#: {{hypernyms|ar|جِرْجِير}}
#* {{quote-book|ar|year=a. 622|author=[[w:Labīd|{{lang|ar|لَبيد بن ربيعة}}]] [Labīd ibn Rabīʿa]|title=[[s:ar:معلقة لبيد بن ربيعة|{{lang|ar|معلقة}}]] [https://www.sacred-texts.com/isl/arp/arp155.htm Muʻallaqa]|section=[[s:ar:عفتِ الديارُ محلُّها فمُقامُهَا (معلقة)|line 5b]]|passage=عَفَتِ ٱلدِّيَارُ مَحَلُّهَا فَمُقَامُهَا / بِمِنًى تَأَبَّدَ غَوْلُهَا فَرِجَامُهَا<br/>فَمَدَافِعُ ٱلرَّيَّانِ عُرِّيَ رَسْمُهَا / خَلَقًا كَمَا ضَمِنَ ٱلْوُحِيَّ سِلَامُهَا<br/>دِمَنٌ تَجَرَّمَ بَعْدَ عَهْدِ أَنِيسِهَا / حِجَجٌ خَلَوْنَ حَلَالُهَا وَحَرَامُهَا<br/>رُزِقَتْ مَرَابِيعَ ٱلنُّجُومِ وَصَابَهَا / وَدْقُ ٱلرَّوَاعِدِ جَوْدُهَا فَرِهَامُهَا<br/>مِنْ كُلِّ سَارِيَةٍ وَغَادٍ مُدْجِنٍ / وَعَشِيَّةٍ مُتَجَاوِبٍ إِرْزَامُهَا<br/>فَعَلَا فُرُوعُ '''ٱلْأَيْهُقَانِ''' وَأطْفَلَتْ / بِٱلْجَلْهَتَيْنِ ظِبَاؤُهَا وَنَعَامُهَا|t=Effaced are her resting-places where she stayed and settled in Mina: deserted are her camps in Ḡawl and ar-Rijām<br/>and the torrents of ar-Rayyān: the traces thereof are laid bare and are torn, as inscribed rocks.<br/>Campsites have already passed after their period of use; years of war and peace overcome.<br/>Cast off there fell upon them the rains of spring signs, and there swept sated thunder clouds and drizzling rains<br/>from every cloud of night, every full one of morn, and those of eve, answering like a hoting squawk,<br/>and there sprung shoots of '''arugula''', and in the brinks of a valley the goats and antelopes have youngs.}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|أَيْهُقَان|head2=أَيْهَقَان}}
