Page 1665 إدريس: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{unc|ar}}, formally a borrowing, candidates suggested include:

* {{bor|ar|grc-koi|Ἀνδρέας}}
** one of the apostles of Jesus<ref>{{R:ar:Nöldeke-Idris}}</ref><ref>{{R:ar:Grimme:1912|page=164}}</ref>
** and also the immortalized cook of Alexander the Great (the latter being called {{m|ar|ذُو الْقَرْنَيْن}})<ref>{{R:ar:Hartmann:1910|pages=314–315}}</ref>
* {{bor|ar|grc|Ποιμάνδρης}}, {{w|Poimandres|one of the}} figures in the {{w|Corpus Hermeticum}}<ref>{{R:egy:Albright:1922|pages=197–198}}</ref>
* the {{bor|ar|grc-koi|-}} form {{m|grc-koi|Ἔσδρας}} of {{der|ar|hbo|עֶזְרָא|tr=ʿezrā|t=[[Ezra]]}}, which would make it a doublet of {{doublet|notext=1|ar|عَازِر|t1=Ezra|عُزَيْر|t2=Ezra|آزَر|t3=Terah, Abraham’s father}}<ref>{{R:ar:Casanova:1924}}</ref><ref>{{R:ar:Torrey|page=72|JFI}}</ref>
* {{bor|ar|hbo|דרש|דּוֹרֵשׁ|tr=dōrēš|t=teacher of the Torah}}<ref>{{R:ar:Erder:1990|page=342 seqq.}}</ref>

Islamic theologists took the name as from {{m|ar|أَدْرَسَ||[[teach|to teach]]}} from the root {{ar-root|د ر س|nocat=1}}, in spite of the pattern {{m|ar||إِفْعِيل}} having been unproductive even by the time of Islam, leading to the equation with the biblical [[Enoch]], whose name derives from {{bor|ar|hbo|חינוך|t=education}}. Enoch is generally depicted as a man of science, and is sometimes credited for inventing the art of writing and astronomy.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|إِدْرِيس}}
** {{a|ar|Moroccan}} {{ary-IPA|ʔidrīs|drīs}}
{{rfap|ar}}

===Proper noun===
{{ar-proper noun|إِدْرِيس|m}}

# {{lb|ar|Islam}} [[Idris]], [[Idrees]], a figure in the Islamic narrative.
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|19|56|passage=وَٱذۡكُرۡ فِی ٱلۡكِتَـٰبِ '''إِدۡرِیسَۚ''' إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ صِدِّیقࣰا نَّبِیࣰّا|tr=wa-ḏkur fī l-kitābi '''ʾidrīsa''' ʾinnahū kāna ṣiddīqan nabiyyan|t=And mention in the Book, '''Idrees'''. Indeed, he was a man of truth and a prophet.}}
# {{given name|ar|male}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|إِدْرِيس:di|state=ind-def}}

