Page 2009 إن: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|ar|sem-wes-pro|*him}}, from {{inh|ar|sem-pro|*šim}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|إِن}}

====Conjunction====
{{ar-con|إِن}}

# {{lb|ar|conditional}} [[if]] {{gloss|possible, not contrary to fact}}
#: {{ux|ar|'''إِنْ''' تَدْرُسْ تَنْجَحْ|t='''If''' you study you (will) succeed.}}
#: {{ux|ar|'''إِنْ''' لَا تَدْرُسْ لَا تَنْجَحْ|t='''If''' you do not study you (will) not succeed.}}
#: {{ux|ar|اَلْمَرْءُ مَقْتُولٌ بِمَا قَتَلَ بِهِ: إِنْ سَيْفًا فَسَيْفٌ، وَإِنْ خِنْجَرًا فَخِنْجَرٌ.|A person is killed by what they are killed with, whether it's a sword, it's a sword, and if it's a dagger, it's a dagger.}}
#: {{ux|ar|اَلنَّاسُ مَجْزِيُّونَ بِأَعَمَالِهِمْ: إِنْ خَيْرًا فَخَيْرٌ، وَإنْ شَرًّا فَشَرٌّ.|People are rewarded for their deeds, if it's good, it's good, and if it's evil, it's evil.}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|2|111
|passage=هَاتُوا بُرْهَانَكُمْ '''إِنْ''' كُنْتُمْ صَادِقِينَ
|translation=Give your proof, '''if''' you are truthful.}}

=====Usage notes=====
Normally for conditions that are capable of being fulfilled. For contrary-to-fact conditions, use {{m|ar|لَوْ}}. Used with the past tense or the jussive, in both cases with a present-tense meaning.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|ar|إِذْمَا}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|ar|إِمَّا}}

===Etymology 2===
Cognate to {{cog|he|הִנֵּה||lo, behold}}.<ref>{{R:sem:Lipinski|page=482|passage=One of the oldest and most important presentatives is *''han'', attested in Palaeosyrian and in Old Akkadian ''en-ma'', later ''umma'' by assimilation. It is found in Ugaritic (''hn''), in Old Canaanite (''a-nu'', ''a-nu-ú'', ''an-nu'', ''an-nu-ú''), in Hebrew (''hinnē''), in Arabic (''ʾinna''), In Ge'ez (''ʾən-ka''); e.g. Arabic ''ʾinna llāha ʾalā kulli šayʾin qadīrun'', "behold, God has power over everything". It should be identified with the West Semitic article ''han-'', but carefully distinguished from the conditional particle ''hn'' → ''ʾn''.}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|year=1997|first=Robert|last=Hetzron|title=The Semitic Languages|page=201}}: The [Arabic] particle ''ʾinna'', etymologically cognate to Hebrew ''hen'', ''hinne'': "behold", emphasizes that the speaker's utterance is true.</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|إِنَّ}}

====Particle====
{{ar-particle|إِنَّ}}

# [[indeed]], {{non-gloss definition|an emphasizing sentence particle, usually untranslated}}
#: {{ux|ar|'''إِنِّي''' فَقِيرٌ وَلَا أَجِدُ طَعَامًا أُطْعِمُ أَوْلَادِي وَعَائِلَتِي، فَسَاعِدْنِي.|'''Indeed''', I am poor and can't find food to feed my children and my family, so please help me.|subst=وَ/وَ-,فَسَ/فَ-سَ}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The subject of a clause containing {{m|ar|إِنَّ}} takes the [[accusative]] case, personal pronouns take [[enclitic]] forms.
* When {{m|ar|إِنَّ}} is followed by the first person singular enclitic {{m|ar|ي|ـِي|my, me}}, it produces forms {{m|ar|إِنِّي}} or {{m|ar|إِنَّنِي}}. When it is followed by the first person plural enclitic {{m|ar|نَا||our, us}}, it produces forms {{m|ar|إِنَّا}} or {{m|ar|إِنَّنَا}}.

=====Inflection=====
{{ar-prep-auto|إِنَّ||1s=[[إِنِّي]] / [[إِنَّنِي]]|1p=[[إِنَّنَا]] / [[إِنَّا]]}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|ar|إِنَّمَا}}

=====See also=====
* {{list:ʾinna and her sisters/ar}}

===Etymology 3===
Cognate to {{cog|he|אין|tr=ein}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|إِنْ}}

====Particle====
{{ar-particle|إِنْ}}

# {{lb|ar|archaic}} [[not]]
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|67:20
|passage='''إِنِ''' الْكَافِرُونَ إِلَّا فِي غُرُورٍ
|translation=the disbelievers are not but in delusion}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|4:62
|passage='''إِنْ''' أَرَدْنَا إِلَّا إِحْسَانًا وَتَوْفِيقًا
|translation=we intended nothing but good conduct and accommodation
|transliteration='''ʔin''' ʔaradnā ʔillā ʔiḥsānan wa-tawfīqan}}
#* {{quote-book|ar|author=w:Al-Jāḥiẓ|year=a. 869|title=البخلاء|chapter=طرف أهل خراسان|passage=لَا وَٱللّٰهِ، '''إِنْ''' أَهْلَكَ النَّاسَ وَلَا أَقْفَرَ بُيُوتَهُمْ وَلَا تَرَكَ دُورَهُمْ بَلَاقِعَ إِلَّا الْإِيمَانُ بِٱلْخَلَفِ}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|ar|مَا}}

===References===
<references/>
* {{R:ar:Lane|pages=103 seqq.}}
* {{R:ar:Wehr-4|page=37}}

