Page 2085 إيا: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
An innovation first attested in [[w:Old Arabic|Old Arabic]], derived from the root {{ar-root|ء|ي|ء}}, originally a vocative word used to call to someone, to direct their attention; see also {{m|ar|إِيَّاكَ}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|إِيَّا}}

===Particle===
{{ar-particle|إِيَّا}}

# ''particle attached to oblique pronoun forms when they are not attached to the verb or preposition governing them''
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|1|5
|passage='''إِيَّاكَ''' نَعْبُدُ وَ'''إِيَّاكَ''' نَسْتَعِينُ
|translation='''Thee''' do we worship; and unto '''Thee''' do we turn for aid.
|subst=وَ'''إِيَّاكَ'''/وَ-'''إِيَّاكَ'''}}

====Usage notes====
Arabic personal pronouns are suffixes, not complete words in their own right, except in the subject form. Because of this, Arabic grammar does not allow them to stand independently in a sentence; they must be attached to a "carrier" word. Normally, object pronouns immediately follow the verb, preposition, or particle governing them, and thus in the overwhelming majority of the time, this governing verb, preposition, or particle is also the "carrier" of the pronoun suffix. However, these object pronouns may be separated from the governing word for any of a number of reasons, in which case {{m|ar|إِيَّا}} must be employed as a generic "carrier" word:
# to delay the verb until after the object pronoun is introduced, usually to emphasize the pronoun (similarly to {{cog|zh|把|tr=bă}})
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|1|5}}
#*: {{ux|ar|'''إِيَّاكَ''' نَعْبُدُ وَ'''إِيَّاكَ''' نَسْتَعِينُ|'''Thee''' do we worship; and unto '''Thee''' do we turn for aid.|subst=وَ'''إِيَّاكَ'''/وَ-'''إِيَّاكَ'''}}
#*: Without {{m|ar|إِيَّا}}, the pronouns would have to follow the verbs, and it would simply say {{m|ar||نَعْبُدُكَ وَنَسْتَعِينُكَ|tr=naʕbuduka wa-nastaʕīnuka|gloss=We worship Thee and turn to Thee for aid}}.
# to introduce a second object pronoun, when the other object pronoun is already attached to the verb, preposition, or particle
#* {{quote-book|ar|author=|title=''The Quran''|edition=|year=7th century {{CE}}|url=https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/القرآن_الكريم/سورة_التوبة|passage=وَمَا كَانَ ٱسْتِغْفَارُ إِبْرَاهِيمَ لِأَبِيهِ إِلَّا عَنْ مَوْعِدَةٍ وَعَدَهَا '''إِيَّاهُ'''|translation=And Abraham prayed for his father's forgiveness only because of a promise he had made '''to him'''.|subst=وَمَا/وَ-مَا,لِأَبِيهِ/لِ-أَبِيهِ}}
#*: Arabic grammar requires that the object of a relative clause be restated with a redundant pronoun, so the sentence literally reads "about a promise he made it '''(to) him'''"; the indirect object ("him", i.e. Abraham's father) therefore requires {{m|ar|إِيَّا}} to carry it, as {{m|ar|وَعَدَ}} is already carrying the relative pronoun referring back to the promise.
#* {{ux|ar|أَعْطِيتُهُ السَّيَّارَةَ|t=I gave him the car.}}
#* {{ux|ar|أَعْطِيتُهُ '''إِيَّاهَا'''|t=I gave him it.}}
# to state the object of a verb, preposition, or particle that is elided or understood from context
#* {{quote-book|ar|author=|title=''The Quran''|edition=|year=7th century {{CE}}|url=https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/القرآن_الكريم/سورة_يوسف|passage=أَمَرَ أَلَّا تَعْبُدُوٱ إِلَّا '''إِيَّاهُ'''|translation=He has ordained that you should worship nought but '''Him''' (''literally, "He has ordained that you should not worship except '''Him'''''").}}
#*: {{m|ar|إِلَّا|gloss=[[except]], [[but]]}} functions as a conjunction, not a preposition; the implied sentence is {{m|ar||أَمَرَ أَلَّا تَعْبُدُوٱ إِلَّا تَعْبُدُوهُ|gloss=He has ordained that you should not worship except [that you worship] '''Him'''}}, but the repeated verb {{m|ar|عَبَدَ|تَعْبُدُوٱ}} is elided the second time. The remaining pronoun requires {{m|ar|إِيَّا}} since the verb to which it would have been attached is gone.
#* {{quote-book|ar|author=|title=''The Quran''|edition=|year=7th century {{CE}}|url=https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/القرآن_الكريم/سورة_سبأ|passage=وَإِنَّا أَوْ '''إِيَّاكُمْ''' لَعَلَىٰ هُدًى أَوْ فِي ضَلَالٍ مُبِينٍ|translation=And, behold, either we or '''you''' are on the right path, or have clearly gone astray!|subst=وَإِنَّا/وَ-إِنَّا}}
#*: Because {{m|ar|أَوْ|gloss=[[or]]}} is a conjunction, it cannot carry an object pronoun. {{m|ar|إِيَّا}} provides a carrier for the second pronoun, as an alternative to repeating {{m|ar|إِنَّ|gloss=[[indeed]]; [[behold]]}} ({{m|ar||إِنَّا أَوْ إِنَّكُم}}).
Since {{m|ar|إِيَّا}} may not be used in the genitive, Arabic grammarians have traditionally thought of the inflections of {{m|ar|إِيَّا}} as {{w|Bound and free morphemes|free or unbound}} accusative pronouns, which, unlike their suffixal counterparts, may be moved around and may be placed before or after the verb.
# {{ux|ar|أَكَلْتُهَا.|t=I ate it.}}
# {{ux|ar|أَكَلْتُ إِيَّاهَا.|t=I ate that.}}
# {{ux|ar|إِيَّاهَا أَكَلْتُ.|t=That, I ate.}}

====Inflection====
{{ar-prep-auto|إِيَّا<!--
-->|1s=إِيَّايَ}}

