Page 3526 اللات: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|اَللَّٰت||classical spelling}}

===Etymology===
{{unc|ar}}. Possible etymologies:<ref>Fahd, T., "al-Lāt", ''{{w|Encyclopaedia of Islam}}'', vol. '''5''': Khe-Mahi, 2nd edition, ed. by C. E. Bosworth, E. van Donzel, B. Lewis and Ch. Pellat, Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1986, page 892</ref>
* From the root {{ar-root|ل|ت|ت}}, from which the verb {{m|ar|لَتَّ|gloss=to mix, or knead, barley-meal}} is derived. This is the explanation given in Arab lexicographical tradition and supported by epigraphic evidence; compare {{cog|sem-his|𐪁𐪉|tr=lt}}.
* A feminine form of {{m|ar|اللّٰه}} or {{m|ar|إله|الإلٰه}}. This is supported by the form given by Herodotus, who identifies {{m|grc|Ἀλιλᾱ́τ}} (which would correspond to {{m|ar||*اَلْإِلَات}}) with Aphrodite Urania,<ref>J.F. Healey, ''The Religion of the Nabataeans: A Conspectus''</ref> and is explained as having the same ending as some ossified vocative forms like {{m|ar||أَخَات}} for {{m|ar|أُخْت|t=sister}}.<ref>{{cite-journal|de|last=Bauer|first=Hans|authorlink=Hans Bauer (semitist)|journal=Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft|volume=69|year=1915|page=561|pageurl=http://menadoc.bibliothek.uni-halle.de/dmg/periodical/pageview/61327|title=Semitische Sprachprobleme. 5. Die Verwandtschaftsnamen und ''ilāh'' „Gott“ im Semitischen}}</ref> However, many gods and goddesses were referred to by the titles {{m|ar|إله|الإلٰه}} and {{m|ar|إِلَاهَة|الإِلٰهَة}}, and so it is not certain that the goddess referenced by Herodotus is the same.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|اللَّات}}

===Proper noun===
{{ar-proper noun|اللَّات|f}}

# {{lb|ar|Arabian god}} [[Allat]]
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|53|19-20|passage=أَفَرَأَيْتُمُ اللَّاتَ وَٱلْعُزَّى وَمَنَاةَ ٱلثَّالِثَةَ ٱلْأُخْرَى|t=So have you considered al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā? And Manāt, the third - the other one?}}

