Page 4023 بارود: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{unc|ar}}. Not a Classical Arabic word. Possible etymologies:
* From {{m|ar|بَرُود|t=a soothing collyrium ({{m|ar|كُحْل}}) used against inflammation of the eye; any powdery collyrium}}{{ar-root|ب|ر|د|notext=1}}, following common colloquial alteration between ''a'' and  ''ā''  in words of the form {{IPAfont|CaCūC}}.
* Via {{bor|ar|arc|-}} from {{der|ar|hy|վառոդ|վառօդ|t=gunpowder}}, composed of {{m|hy|վառ|t=burning}} + {{m|hy|օդ|t=air}}. However, Ačaṙyan regards this as a folk-etymology reshaping of {{m|hy|բարութ}}, which was ultimately borrowed from this very Arabic term.

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|بَارُود|m}} {{tlb|ar|uncountable}}

# [[saltpetre]]
# [[gunpowder]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|بَارُود}}

====Descendants====
* {{desctree|ota|باروت|tr=barut|bor=1}}
* {{desctree|fa|بارود|tr=bârud|bor=1}}

===References===
* {{R:ar:EI-2|author=G. S. Colin|entry=Bārūd|volume=1|pages=1055–1057}}
* {{R:trk:Budagov|vol=I|page=224a}}
* {{R:xcl:HAB|բարութ|vol=I|page=428b}}

{{C|ar|Explosives}}

