Page 4218 بر: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From the root {{ar-root|ب ر ر}}. Inherited from {{inh|ar|sem-pro|*barr-}}.

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|بَرّ|m}}

# {{senseid|ar|land}} [[land]], [[dry]] [[land]] {{qualifier|as opposed to [[sea]]}}
#: {{coordinate terms|ar|بَحْر|جَوّ}}
# {{senseid|ar|outside}} [[outside]], [[field]]
#: {{antonyms|ar|جَوّ}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|بَرّ}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|ar|بَرِّيّ|t=of the dry land}}
* {{l|ar|بَرَّانِيّ|t=exterior}}

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|I/i~a.vn:بِرّ}}

# {{senseid|ar|be-pious}} {{lb|ar|intransitive}} to be [[righteous]], to be [[pious]], to be [[godly]], to be  [[reverent]], to be [[religious]]
## {{lb|ar|intransitive}} to [[straighten up]], to [[reform]]
##: {{syn|ar|صَلَحَ|اِسْتَقَام|t1=to reform|t2=to straighten up}}
##: {{ant|ar|فَجَرَ|t=to be sinful}}
# {{lb|ar|intransitive}} to be [[kind]] or [[generous]] to {{+obj|ar|:بِ}}
#: {{syn|ar|أَحْسَنَ}}
# {{lb|ar|intransitive|transitive}} to [[respect]] or to be [[respectful]] {{+obj|ar|:بِ<to elders, one's parents or grandparents, old kinsmen, a deity, and so on>}}, to [[honor]], to [[revere]]; to [[obey]]
#: {{syn|ar|وَقَّرَ|t=to revere, to venerate}}
#: {{ux|ar|بَرَّ الصَّبِيُّ وَالِدَيْهِ وَرَبَّهُ|The boy obeyed his parents and his Lord.}}
# {{lb|ar|transitive}} to [[deem]] (an act) [[true]], [[sincere]], or [[pious]]; to [[accept]] or [[bless]] (a religious ritual)
#: {{syn|ar|قَبِلَ|بَارَك}}
#: {{ux|ar|بَرَّ اللهُ حَجَّهُ وَصَلَاتَهُ|Allah accepted his pilgrimage and his prayer.}}
## {{lb|ar|intransitive|of an act, especially religious rituals, such as pilgrimages}} to be [[true]], [[sincere]], or [[pious]]
##: {{ux|ar|بَرَّ حَجُّهُ وَصَلَاتُهُ|His pilgrimage and prayer were pious.}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|I/i~a.vn:بِرّ}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|بِرّ|m}}

# {{senseid|ar|piety}} {{ar-verbal noun of|بَرَّ|form=I}}
# [[faith]], [[godliness]], [[piety]]
# [[respectfulness]]
# [[kindness]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|بِرّ}}

====Adjective====
{{ar-adj|بَرّ|pl=أَبْرَار}}

# {{senseid|ar|pious}} [[pious]]
#: {{syn|ar|صَالِح|t=righteous; godly}}
#: {{ant|ar|فَاجِر|t=wicked; sinful, ungodly}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|82|13-14
|passage=إِنَّ '''الْأَبْرَارَ''' لَفِي نَعِيمٍ / وَإِنَّ الْفُجَّارَ لَفِي جَحِيمٍ
|translation=Indeed the '''pious''' will be in bliss, and lo, the wicked will be in the fire of hell.}}
## [[reverent]], [[religious]]
##: {{syn|ar|تَقِيّ|مُتَّقٍ}}
# [[kind]], [[benevolent]]
#: {{syn|ar|مُحْسِن}}
# [[respectful]]
# [[true]], [[sincere]]
#: {{syn|ar|مُخْلِص}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-adj|بَرّ|pl=أَبْرَار|f=بَرَّة}}

===Etymology 2===

====Verb====
{{ar-verb-form|بُرْ|I}}

# {{inflection of|ar|بَارَ||2|m|s|actv|impr}}

===Etymology 3===
[[File:Triticum turgidum L. Bajuwarenhof Kirchheim 2012-08-05.jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|بُرّ}}|upright=1.25]]
Probably one of the words Yemenite expatriates picked up in the {{w|Byzantine Empire}}, from {{bor|ar|grc|πυρός}}, similar to {{m|ar|فِرْسِك|t=peach}}, if it is correct that one has to posit for {{der|ar|ine-pro|*péh₂wr̥}} a meaning “spelt”. Another word for spelt or dinkel wheat is borrowed from Ancient Greek: {{m|ar|خندريس|خَنْدَرُوس}}.

The similar {{cog|he|בָּר|tr=bār|t=grain, corn}} may be instead from the same derivation as the etymon of {{m|ar|بَزْر|t=seed}}. The similarity to {{cog|sla-pro|*bъrъ|t=foxtail millet}} is also likely a coincidence.

====Noun====
{{ar-coll-noun|بُرّ|m|pl=أَبْرَار|sing=بُرَّة}}

# {{senseid|ar|wheat}} {{tlb|ar|now|_|South Ḥijāz|Yemen|Ẓufār}} [[wheat]]
#: {{syn|ar|حِنْطَة|قَمْح|طَعَام|غَلَّة}}
#* {{quote-book|ar|year=c. 950|author=ar:أبو دلف مسعر بن مهلهل<tr:ʔAbū Dulaf Misʕar Ibn Muhalhal>|title=De itinere Asiatico commentarium|editor=w:Kurd von Schlözer|year_published=1845 [https://www.worldcat.org/title/abu-dolef-misaris-ben-mohalhal-de-itinere-asiatico-commentarium/oclc/978639123 <sup>WC</sup>] [https://books.google.com/books?id=R7BCAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA39#v=onepage&q&f=false <sup>GB</sup>]|url=https://www.deutsche-digitale-bibliothek.de/item/DKVP45UPGMDNQ27ALWG5FXSJKYT7ZUVC|pages=18–19|pageurl=https://reader.digitale-sammlungen.de/de/fs1/object/display/bsb10219218_00026.html?zoom=1.25|passage=فخرجت إلى الساحل أربد بكلة وهذا أول الهند ومنتهى مسير المراكب لا يتهيّأ لها أن يتجاوزها وألا غرقت قال فلما وصلت إلى كلة  رأيته وهي عظيمة عالية السور كثيرة البساتين غزيرة الماء ووجدت بها معدن الرصاص القلعي لا يكون إلا في قلعتها في سائر الدنيا، وفي هذه القلعة تضرب السيوف القلعية، وهي الهندية العتيقة … وأهل هذه القلعة يمتنعون على ملكها إذا أرادوا يطيعونه إن أحبوا ورسمهم رسم الصين في ترك الذبائح وليس في جميع الدنيا معدن الرصاص القلعي إلا في هذه القلعة وبينهما وبين الصين ثلثمائة فرسخ وحولها مدن ورساتيق وقرى ولهم أحكام وحبوس وخبايات وأكلهم '''البر''' والتمور وبقولهم كلها تباع وزنا وأرغفة خبزهم تباع عددا ولا حمامات لهم بل عندهم عين جارية يغتسلون فيها ودرهمهم يزن ثلثي درهم ويعرف بالفهري ولهم فلوس يتعاملون بها ويلبسون كأهل الصين الإفرند الصيني المثمن وملكها دون ملك الصين ويخطب لملك الصين وقبلته إليه وبيت عبادته له.|t=And I went off to the dusty shore of Kala, which is the first and ultimate end of India for vessels, as nobody even dares to imagine to pass by it since he would drown. When I arrived at Kala, I conned it and it is large and has high walls, many gardens and plenty of water, and I found mines of tin not found in the rest of the world but in Qalʕa, and in this Qalʕa one forges the Qalʕaite swords, the excellent Indian ones, and the populace of this Qalʕa withstands its king when it wants and abides by him when it lists, and their tradition is like the tradition of China, refraining from slaughtering beasts, and there aren’t in all the world tin mines but in this Qalʕa, and between them two and China are three-hundred parasang, and there are towns, rural districts and villages, and they have jurisdiction, jails, and corbans, and their food is wheat and dates and all their vegetables they buy by scales, and their bread is bread-rolls they buy by count. They have no baths but a running spring in which they wash, and their dirham weighs as a third-dirham and is known as Fahrī, and they have fulūs with which they commerce, and they dress like the people of China in dear Chinese silks, and their king is subordinate to the King of China, reports to the King of China, his praying direction is to him, his place of sacrifice is towards him.}}
#* {{quote-book|ar|author={{lang|ar|{{w|lang=ar|علي بن برهان الدين الحلبي }}}}|chapter=[[s:ar:السيرة الحلبية/باب شهوده حلف الفضول|{{lang|ar|باب شهوده حلف الفضول}}]]|title=Sīra Ḥalabiyya|year=a. 1635|passage=فاتفق أن أمية بن أبي الصلت مر على بني عبد المدان، فرأى طعامهم لباب '''البر''' والشهد.|t=There has been agreement that Umayya ibn Abī s-Salt passed by the Banū ʕabd al-Madān and he saw that they ate pulp of wheat and honey.}}

