Page 4363 بريد: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|بَرِيد}}

===Etymology 1===
Already in the 6th century, from {{bor|ar|gkm|βέρηδος}}, {{m|grc|βέρεδος|t=post-horse}}, from {{der|ar|la|verēdus}}.

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|بَرِيد|m|pl=بُرُد}} (''in verse also'' {{l|ar|بُرْد}})

# [[post]], [[mail]], an [[institution]] to send [[message]]s, [[intelligence]]
# {{lb|ar|archaic}}{{C|ar|Occupations}} [[messenger]], [[courier]], [[express]], [[estafette]], a person employed in the institution of [[mail]] or [[intelligence]]
#: {{syn|ar|رَسُول|قَيْنَاب|قَانِب|فَيْج|فُرَانِق}}
# {{lb|ar|obsolete}}{{C|ar|Equids|Camelids}} the [[beast]] by which [[message]]s are sent by the mail institution, a [[posthorse|post-horse]], post-[[mule]] or post-[[camel]]
# {{lb|ar|obsolete}}{{C|ar|Units of measure}} a [[measure]] of [[distance]], a [[postal route]]
#* {{RQ:Yaqut|W1|page=37|line=9|passage=فأمّا الْبَرِيد ففيه خلاف وذهب قوم إلى أنه بالبادية اثنا عشر ميلا وبالشام وخراسان ستة أميال|t=About the postal route there is disagreement. Folks believed that in the desert it is twelve miles and in Syria and Khorasan six miles.}}
# {{lb|ar|obsolete|rare}} [[post office]], a [[stationed]] [[relay]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|بَرِيد|pl=بُرُد}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|ar|سَاعِي بَرِيدٍ|t=postman}}
* {{l|ar|بَرِيدِيّ|t=postal; postmaster; courier}}
* {{l|ar|أَبْرَدَ|t=to sent onto the way as a courier; to send a courier to; to send by way of post}}

====References====
{{box-top}}
{{col-top|2}}
* {{R:Iranica|author=Bernburg, Lutz Richter and EIr|url=http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/greece-xiv|title=Greece xiv. Greek Loanwords in Medieval New Persian|date=2012-02-23}}, embraces Zimmern’s etymology.
* {{R:xaa:ELA|II|page=122}}, recognizes the Latin origin but assumes a {{cog|syc||**ܒܪܝܕܐ|tr=**brīdā}} mediary which is denied by {{R:ar:Ullmann-barīd|page=8 footnote 19}} by information from {{w|lang=de|Rainer Degen}} that it is a dis legomenon transcription for all what is attested of the term in Aramaic, {{R:CAL|b)rwd%232|N}}, {{R:CAL|byryd|N}}.
* {{cite-journal|de|title=sursat – Eine Untersuchung zum persischen Steuerwesen in der Kadscharenzeit|author=[http://irfancolloquia.org/pdf/lights16_memoriam_kamran_ekbal.pdf Ekbal, Kamran]|volume=133|year=1983|url=https://menadoc.bibliothek.uni-halle.de/dmg/periodical/titleinfo/150148|work={{w|Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft}}|page=150|pageurl=https://menadoc.bibliothek.uni-halle.de/dmg/periodical/pageview/128559}}, repeats after {{w|Alois Sprenger}} 1864 who only [https://archive.org/details/diepostundreise00spregoog/page/n54/mode/2up relates the common story] the etymology from {{noncog|fa|بریده|tr=borida|t=docked}} and after Sourdel and {{w|Richard N. Frye|Frye}} the Assyrian one.
* {{R:ar:Lane|page=185}}, introduces another false etymology connecting the word to {{cog|he|פֶּרֶד|tr=péreḏ|t=mule, hinny}}.
* {{R:ar:Quatremère|MII2|pages=87–92 footnote 34}}, already recognized the Roman derivation and refuted Medieval folk-etymologies of which the most persisting is that the word would be related to {{noncog|fa|بریده|tr=burîda|t=docked, having the tail clipped}} because the post-horses were marked so by {{w|Darius the Great}} or similar – according to which legend some Persian king according to {{RQ:Yaqut|W1|page=37 line 19 seqq.|instead=1}} marked horses of couriers for them to be recognized and provided for by governors.
* {{R:ar:EI-2|pages=1045–1046|last=Sourdel|first=Dominique|authorlink=Dominique Sourdel|volume=1|entry=Barīd}}, confused as a mere compilator, derives the Arabic from Byzantine and Latin but that from perhaps Assyrian, apparently not knowing its origin in {{der|ar|cel-pro|*uɸorēdos|t=horse}}. In the glossary page 182 they suggest that the Assyrian word derives from Latin.
* {{R:ar:Steingass|page=121}}
* {{R:ar:Ullmann-barīd}}, tracks the history of the word by two hundred quotes.
* {{R:ar:Wehr-4|page=64}}
* {{R:akk:Zimmern|page=7}} knows the Roman derivation but following the topic of his book to list as many Akkadian cultural influences as possible throws out the idea of a borrowing from {{noncog|akk|𒉽𒄬|tr=PAP.ḪAL|ts=purīdu|t=leg; roaming one|pos=in the literal sense as well as in the sense of messenger}}, which comes from {{m|akk|𒄷𒄭|tr=MUD|ts=parādu|t=to be terrified, to be scared; to be disturbed, to not rest, to keep moving; to run at full speed, to hastily get away, to move urgently}}.
{{col-bottom}}
{{box-bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
Apparently from {{m|ar|بَرَدَ|t=to cut off}}.

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|بَرِيد|m}}

# {{C|ar|Breads}} [[crumb]], piece of bread eaten to a [[stew]] for [[volume]]
#* {{R:ar:FAH|6I|page=316 Nr. 468|passage=وقولون البَرِيدُ لخبز يلقى عليه الماء تَطْعَمُةُ النِّسَاءُ لِلسُّمْنَةِ. والصّوَاب الْمَبْرُودُ.|t=They call ''barīd'' the bread thrown into the water the women eat for volume. The right is ''mabrūd''.}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|بَرِيد|pl=بُرُد}}

