{{ar-decl-noun|بَشْر}}

===Etymology 2===
{{ar-rootbox|ب ش ر}}
Related to {{m|ar|بَشَر||skin}} as rejoicement manifests itself as a complexion of the skin.

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|I/i~a.ipass?.vn:بَشَر}}<br/>
{{ar-verb|I/a~i.ipass?.vn:بَشَر}}

# to [[rejoice]] at, to become [[glad]], to [[congratulate]], to [[rejoice]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|I/i~a.ipass?.vn:بَشَر}}
{{ar-conj|I/a~i.ipass?.vn:بَشَر}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|بَشَر|m}}

# {{ar-verbal noun of|بَشِرَ|form=I}}
# {{ar-verbal noun of|بَشَرَ|form=I}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|بَشَر}}

===Etymology 3===
Causative of {{m-self|ar|بَشِرَ|t=to [[rejoice]] at}}. Religious sense is a literal translation of {{semantic loan|ar|grc|εὐαγγελίζω|notext=1}}, from {{m|grc|εὐαγγέλιον|t=good news, gospel}}. Cognate with {{cog|he|בישר|בִּשֵׂר|t=to bring good news|tr=bisér, biśśēr}}.

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|II.pass}}

# to [[bring]] [[good news]] (to)
#: {{ant|ar|أَنْذَرَ}}
# {{lb|ar|Christianity}} to [[evangelize]], to preach the [[gospel]] (to)

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|II.pass}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|mt|baxar|inh=1}}

{{cln|ar|causative verbs|collective nouns|masculine and feminine nouns by sense}}
