Page 4508 بطيخ: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Watermelons.jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|بَطِّيخ}}]]

===Etymology===
Presumably borrowed from {{bor|ar|arc|-}}, attested in {{cog|jpa|בטיח|tr=*baṭṭīḥ}} and {{cog|syc|ܦܲܛܝܼܚܵܐ|tr=paṭṭīḥā}}, and a broken plural {{cog|he|אֲבַטִּיחַ|tr=ʾăḇaṭṭī́aḥ}}. While it has been theoretized to have been acquired in {{cog|sem|-}} via {{der|ar|egy|bddw-kꜣ|t=watermelon}} from sub-Saharan Africa, there is an explanation as an Iranian borrowing equivalent to {{cog|fa|بیدخت|tr=bêdoxt|t=Venus}}, which is the known {{cog|akk|𒂗|tr=bēlu|t=master; Bel, Baal}} + {{m+|fa|دخت|tr=doxt|t=daughter}}, literally “daughter of Baal”, a reinterpretation of the feminine form of the {{w|Bel (mythology)|Baʿal}} god found as {{m|syc|ܒܝܠܬܝ|tr=bēltī}}, {{m|syc|ܒܠܬܝ|tr=beltī|t=Venus}} in {{cog|syc|-}}, equalling {{doublet|ar|بَعْلَة|notext=1|t1=mistress}}. Like in general food items can be reused to denote body parts, especially those viewed sexually, and in Eastern languages specifically the names of fruits come to mean “vulva”, as well-known {{cog|ajp|تِينة|tr=tīne|t=fig}}, {{cog|hy|թուզ|t=fig}}, {{cog|he|רימון|רִמּוֹן|tr=rimmṓn|t=pomegrenate}} in {{w|Song of Solomon}} 7:12 and 8:2, here in turn the meaning of a melon would be secondarily transferred from {{w|Venus figurine|representations of fertility goddesses}}. This is corroborated by lexicographers mentioning a [[Himyaritic]] word {{m|ar|بَيْدَخَة|t=plump, chubby|pos=applied to women}}.

===Noun===
{{ar-coll-noun|بِطِّيخ|head2=بَطِّيخ|sing=بِطِّيخَة|sing2=بَطِّيخَة|pl=بَطَاطِيخ}}

# [[watermelon]], [[watermelons]]
# [[melon]], [[melons]]
#: {{syn|ar|شمام|tr=šammām|بَطِّيخ أَصْفَر|tr2=}}
<!-- Steingass also says pumpkin but not sure this is correct -->

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-coll-noun|بِطِّيخ|head2=بَطِّيخ|sing=بِطِّيخَة|sing2=بَطِّيخَة|pl=بَطَاطِيخ}}

====Descendants====
