Page 4661 بلوط: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Quercus infectoria ssp veneris kz4.jpg|thumb|right|بَلُّوط]]
{{wp|lang=ar}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|arc|בְּלוּטָא}} / {{m|syc|ܒܠܘܛܐ|tr=bəllūṭā, ballūṭā|t=oak; acorn}}, further
* according to Löw an Aramaic formation from {{m+|arc|בָלַט|tr=bālaṭ|t=to bulge out, to stand forth}}, because of the nut having a protruding appearance, the pattern KaLLūM being common in Aramaic for fruits.
* possibly related to {{cog|akk|𒋾|tr=balāṭu|t=to live, to stay or remain, to be enduring; to bring back to life, life-bringing trees or seeds}}.
* potentially also {{cog|akk|𒄑𒁁𒀖|tr=be-lit, baluṭ|t=a strong tree, bearing edible produce, sustainable in more northerly fringes of the Assyrian Empire}} found in the vicinity of [[Harran]] mentioned during the reign of {{w|Sargon II}}, the more typical word for oak being {{m|akk|𒀠𒆷𒀭|tr=ʾallānu, alyānu|t=oak; acorn}} from {{noncog|sux|𒄑𒀠𒆷𒈝|tr=allanum|t=oak; acorn, acorn-shaped}}. Compare {{noncog|arc|אִילָנָא}}, {{noncog|syc|ܐܺܝܠܳܢܳܐ|tr=ʾīlānā|t=tree}}, {{noncog|he|אֵלָה|tr=ʾelā|t=terebinth}}, {{noncog|he|אֵילִים|tr=ēlīm|t=mighty tree}}, {{noncog|he|אֵלֹון|tr=elōn|t=great tree}}, {{noncog|he|אַלּוֹן|tr=allōn|t=great tree; oak}}.
{{rel-top|further cognates}}
* comparable to {{cog|pal|blwt'|tr=balūt|t=acorn, oak-nut}}, {{cog|mzn|پلت|tr=palat||maple, poplar}}, {{cog|kmr|berrû}}, {{m|kmr|belû}}, dialectal {{cog|tr|bolut}}, {{m|tr|pelüt}}, {{m|tr|palıt}}, {{m|tr|pölüt}}, {{m|tr|pelit}}, {{m|tr|pilit}} etc., {{cog|az|palıd}}, dialectal {{m|az|palut}}, {{cog|uz|balut}}, {{cog|tr|palamut}} / {{m|ota|پلاموت}}, {{m|tr|pelit}} / {{m|ota|پلیت}}, {{m|tr|pelid}} / {{m|ota|پلید}}, {{cog|chg|tr=pälütʼʼ}}, all meaning the oak and/or its nut. The borrowing directions cannot be ascertained.
* whereas {{cog|vec|palamita}}, {{cog|sh|palàmīda}} / {{m|sh|пала̀мӣда}}, {{cog|gkm|παλαμύδα}} / {{m|grc|παλαμίδα}}, {{cog|el|παλαμύδι}} / {{m|grc|παλαμίδι}}, {{cog|ro|pălămidă}} mean the [[creeping thistle]] {{taxlink|Serratula arvensis|species}} <small>L.</small> which looks at its top similar to the acorn.
* to be distinguished from the fish-names {{noncog|el|παλαμύδα}}, {{m|el|παλαμίδα}} from {{noncog|grc|πᾱλᾰμῠ́ς}} / {{m|grc|πηλᾰμῠ́ς}} and its numerous descendants meaning {{taxfmt|Sarda sarda|species}} ({{l|en|Atlantic bonito}}).
{{rel-bottom}}

