Page 4683 بن: -------- begin text --------
{{wikipedia|lang=ar}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|ar|bin}}
Base form of {{m|ar|اِبْن}}. From {{inh|ar|sem-pro|*bin-}}. Compare {{cog|he|בֵּן}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|اِبْن}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|بْن|tr=bn|m}}

# {{alternative form of|ar|اِبْن}}

=====Usage notes=====
Used after another noun (which necessarily ends in a vowel if case endings are preserved).

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|بْن|state=con}}

===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|ar|coffee}}
Borrowed from {{bor|ar|syc|ܒܽܘܢܳܐ|tr=būnā}}, which, attested as “barley soaked in water”, underwent a meaning development in 14<sup>th</sup>-century Yemen due to brewages of sundry appetizing beverages, less likely in view of the pungent fish-sauce {{m|ar|بُنّ|id=backfrombynni}}, but from the picture of [[barley]] grains to the [[coffee bean]] in particular. Compare meaning transferrals in the spice-names {{m|ar|قِزْح}} and {{m|ar|بِسْبَاس}}, and {{m|ar|خَنْدَرِيس|t=spelt; gruel}} to “wine”, as {{m|ar|قَهْوَة|t=coffee}} originally meant “wine”. This specific meaning strain—it was only generalized in Ethiopia to mean the berry, powder and decoct of coffee too—is confirmed by {{der|ar|grc|βύνη|t=[[malt]]}}, a borrowing found in Late Antiquity’s {{w|Papyrus Graecus Holmiensis}} and {{w|Aëtius of Amida}}. The further origin can be a technical term restricted from the well-known {{der|ar|ira-mid|-}} word for “base, grounds” found as {{cog|fa-cls|بن|tr=bun}} and {{cog|xcl|բուն}}, also borrowed directly from Iranian in {{cog|acx|بون|tr=būn}}, compare the Iranianism reflected in {{cog|ar|دُرْدِيّ|t=lees, amurca}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|بُنّ}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|بُنّ|m|pl=أَبْنَان}} {{tlb|ar|usually|uncountable}}

# [[coffee bean]]s, [[coffee]]
# [[coffee]] tree
# {{lb|ar|obsolete}} a fine strong fragrance

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|بُنّ|pl=أَبْنَان}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|ar|بُنِّيّ|id=nisbacoffee}}
* {{l|ar|بَنَّة|t=odour}}

