Page 4688 بنة: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Apparently generalized from {{m|ar|بُنّ|id=coffee}} later glossed as “coffee-beans/plants; a fine strong fragrance” and explained by {{der|ar|syc|ܒܽܘܢܳܐ|tr=būnā|t=barley soaked in water}} and {{cog|grc|βύνη|t=malt}}, so originally developing an idea of something ''[[fermented]]'', or otherwise regarding the associated meanings of “staying” or “lingering” and our connection to {{der|ar|ira-pro|*buHnáh}}, {{der|ar|iir-pro|*bʰudʰnás|t=ground}} one can also construct a basic idea of ''ground'', ''[[base note|base (note)]]''. Similarity to {{cog|sla-pro|*voňь|*vòňa|t=stench}} is a coincidence.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|بَنَّة}}
* {{a|ar|Andalusi}} {{ar-IPA|بِنَّة}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|بَنَّة|f|pl=بِنَان}}

# {{lb|ar|classical}} [[aroma]], [[odour]] considered [[contranymous]]ly either [[pleasant]] or [[fetid]], from [[foodstuff]], [[clothing]], or in [[lodging]]-[[place]]s of [[cattle]]
#* {{R:ar:FAH|6II|page=253 Nr.80|passage=ويقولون هذا طعام ليس له بَنَّة أي طِيبٌ. والبَنَّةُ عند العرب الرِّيحُ. تقول أَجِدُ في هذا الثّوب بَنَّةً من تفّاح أو سفرجل أو غير ذلك. والبَنَّةُ أيضاً رِيحُ مَرَابِضِ البَقَرِ والغَنَمِ.|t=They say this food has no pleasant odour or scent. Odour is smell with the Arabs. You say I find in this garment smell of apples and quinces and other things. And odour is also smell of cow and sheep barns.}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|بَنَّة|pl=بِنَان}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ar|بِنّ|t=a place having a fetid odour; layer of fat|id=bannabin}}
* {{l|ar|بَنَّ|t=to stop over or have a stay|id=bannabin}}
* {{l|ar|بَنَّنَ|t=to tie to fatten; to give an odour to}}
* {{l|ar|أَبَنَّ|t=to stick, to stay, to remain}}
* {{l|ar|تَبَنَّنَ|t=to acquire an odour}}
* {{l|ar|بَنِين|t=prudent; constant; fat; savoury}}
* {{l|ar|بُنَانَة|t=a place rich in plants or otherwise of agreeable odours}}

===References===
* {{R:xaa:ELA|II|page=189}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=1|page=158b}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=1|page=166}}
* {{R:ar:Lane|page=258}}

