Page 4918 ت ر ج م: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
There has been noted the correspondence of this root in Arabic and the other Semitic languages to the {{noncog|hit|-}} and {{noncog|xlu|-}} verb {{m|hit|𒋻𒄣𒈠𒀀𒄿|tr=tarkummāi}} / {{m|xlu|𒋻𒄣𒈪𒅀|tr=tarkummiya}} of the meaning “to announce”, “to explain”, which can be explained by there not being a Proto-Semitic predecessor, but Arabic having borrowed the root via {{bor|ar|syc|ܬܰܪܓܡܳܢܳܐ|tr=targmānā|t=interpreter}}<ref>Arabic having borrowed {{m|ar|تَرْجُمَان}} is already suspected by {{R:ar:Fraenkel|page=280}}. But Fraenkel knew no Akkadian and the Anatolian languages were unknown in his time.</ref> and/or {{bor|ar|syc|ܬܰܪܓܶܡ|tr=targem|t=to speak in public; to {{l|en|interpret}}}}, and Aramaic from {{der|ar|akk|targumannum|𒅴𒁄|tr=targumannum|t=interpreter}}, possibly borrowed or alternatively derived natively from {{m|akk|ragāmum|𒅗𒂷𒂷|tr=ragāmum|t=to shout, to exclaim, to cast forth a prophecy; hence also, to speak for the gods, to speak for another, to interpret, and to {{l|en|rede}}}}. {{cog|he|-}} – {{m|he|תורגמן|תֻּרְגְּמָן|tr=turg'mán|t=translator}}, {{m|he|תִּרְגֵּם|tr=tirgém|t=to translate}} – would have loaned the root from Aramaic, and the well-developed {{cog|sem-eth|-}} root (see {{ncog|gez|ተርጐመ|tr=tärgʷämä}}) is also claimed<ref name="leslautrgm">{{R:gez:Leslau-Comp.|page=579}}</ref> to be of Aramaic origin.

For the thesis of a loanword in Semitic speaks:
* the variation in forms, especially in Arabic and Akkadian which are else not prone to variations of vowels
* a slight strain of specialization in meaning from Aramaic to Arabic and Ethiopic, as the meaning of “speaking in public” or “announcing” gets lost south-east of Aramaic
* the observation of a “wandering from West to East”<ref name="rabin">{{R:he:Rabin-Hit.|pages=134–136}}</ref>
* the identification of the ending of the Akkadian noun as not ''-ānu'', but ''-annu'' which only appears in loanwords in Akkadian<ref name="starke">{{cite-journal|author=[[w:de:Frank Starke|Frank Starke]]|title=Zur Herkunft von akkad. ta/urgumannu(m) „Dolmetscher“|year=1993|journal=Die Welt des Orients|volume=24|pages=20–38|doi=10.2307/25683454}}</ref>

But as for Hittite, there is no weighty Indo-European derivation for the Anatolian verb,<ref>Starke exercises the most in-depth try for an Indo-European reconstruction of eventual Anatolian forms, therein also comparing to {{m|hit|𒋻𒌑𒍣|tr=tarúzi|t=to dance in a circle}}, which he compares with {{noncog|la|torqueō|t=to twist}} and {{noncog|sa|तर्क|t=reasoning}}, while pointing to {{noncog|la|vertō}} and {{m|la|versō|t=to turn}} also having acquired the meanings of “to translate”, to which can be added {{noncog|en|turn}} with its meaning. Starke claims that “die formalen and semantischen Voraussetzungen sind gegeben”, however there is a lot of reconstruction with non-necessary relating to other languages.</ref> it shows features of being denominal, and there is no attested instance of it precisely meaning “to translate” or “to serve as language mediator for”, while it is striking that ''the occupational noun is not at all attested in Anatolian''.<ref name="starke"/> Also there is culturally no reason to assume that Akkadian has borrowed ''the word for this specific concept specifically from Anatolian'', as it was a long-established practice to converse diplomatically through interpreters.<ref name="starke"/><ref name="rubio">{{cite-journal|last=Rubio|first=Gonzalo|title=Šulgi and the Death of Sumerian|year=2006|editors=Piotr Michalowski, Niek Veldhuis|journal=Approaches to Sumerian Literature Studies in Honour of Stip (H.L.J. Vanstiphout)|location=Leiden|publisher=Brill|section=Cuneiform Monographs 35|page=167}}</ref>

The conclusion can be that Akkadian has borrowed from an {{unk|ar|nocap=1}} language, whereas what the Hittite forms are is in sum uncertain, and their value for Semitic has been that they have incited thoughts about the origins of the Semitic root ''t-r-g-m'' whilst being unrelated.

