-------- end text --------
Page 5002 تارة: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
Of a little-developed root. {{m|ar|أَتَارَ|t=to repeat|pos={{ar-form|IV|nocap=1}}}} is clearly denominal, and about {{m|ar|تَارَ|t=to run, to flow {{q|of blood}}}} even the Classical Arabic lexicographers have had little to say other than that it is obsolete, and as the examples of {{m|ar|آنَ}}, {{m|ar|حَانَ}}, {{m|ar|أَنَى}} illustrate, the base stem too tends to be secondary in relation to a time word. In spite of {{m|ar|طَوْر|t=time}} not directly related to it but probably a borrowing from {{bor|ar|arc|-}}, compare {{cog|arc-jla|תּוּר|tr=tōr|t=to go around (a border)}},<ref>{{R:CAL|t)r%232|V}}</ref> rather than cognate to Arabic {{ar-root|ث و ر|nocat=1}} or {{ar-root|د و ر|nocat=1}} (whence {{cog|ar|دَوْرَة|t=period}})<ref>Notwithstanding the difficulty of correspondence this is considered in {{R:xaa:ELA|II|page=227}}</ref> denominal from {{m|arc|תּוֹרָא|t=line, row; cord, band|tr=tōrā}}, which means this word is the same word as {{doublet|ar|تُرّ|t1=plumb line|notext=1}}, figurative uses from drawing a line forth and back as times pass—compare also {{m|ar|تَوّ|t=a knot; an instance}}. Exactly the meanings are found in {{cog|he|תּוֹר|tr=tōr|t=queue, line; turn; appointment; period}}.<ref>A comparison the 2017 ''Dictionnaire du faisceau dialectal arabe andalou'' also makes, without any origin conclusion, equally comparing {{cog|he|טוּר|tr=ṭūr|t=line, row; series}} and {{cog|he|דוֹר|tr=dōr|t=circle of time, period, generation}}.</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|تَارَة}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|تَارَة|f|pl=تَارَات}}

# {{lb|ar|usually|used adverbially}} a [[time]]
#: {{syn|ar|مَرَّة}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|تَارَة|pl=sfp}}

====References====
* {{R:ar:Almaany}}
* {{R:ar:Lane|page=322b–c}} where there are also suspicious vacillations about the original form of the word.
<references/>

