===Etymology 1===
Borrowed from {{bor|ar|arc|תַּנִּינָא}} / {{m|syc|ܬܲܢܝܼܢܵܐ|tr=tannīnā|t=sea serpent, monster}}, from {{der|ar|akk|𒆗𒉌𒈾|tr=danninu|t=[[netherworld]]; source of earthquakes; the inaccessible land where the dead remain}}, ultimately from {{der|ar|sem-pro|*dnn}}/{{m|sem-pro|*tnn|t=to be mighty, to be strong; to be fortified, to be long lasting, to stay at a place; to rumble, to earthquake, to shake with a booming noise}}. {{doublet|ar|دَنْدَن|t1=mythical monsterous fish that can swallow everything else in the sea, the biggest fish in the sea}}; possibly related as well to {{cog|egy|dnwn|ts=denwen|t=giant serpent whose body was made of fire defeated by the spirit of the dead pharaoh; symbolic of drought, chaos, and destructive natural forces}} attested in the {{w|Pyramid Texts}} of uncertain origin.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|تِنِّين}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|تِنِّين|m|pl=تَنَانِين}}

# [[eel]], [[whale]], any scaled animal {{lb|ar|obsolete}}
# sea [[monster]]
# [[dragon]]
# {{lb|ar|constellation}} (normally {{l|ar|اَلتِّنِّين}}) [[Draco]]
# {{lb|ar|weather}} [[waterspout]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|تِنِّين|pl=تَنَانِين}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|scn|biḍḍina|unc=1}}

===Etymology 2===

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|تَنِّين}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|تَنِّين|m}}

# [[tannin]], [[tannic acid]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|تَنِّين}}

===References===
* {{R:ar:Fraenkel|page=123}}

{{C|ar|Mythological creatures|Chemistry}}

