Page 6636 ثانية: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{ar-rootbox|ث ن ي}}
Compare {{m|ar|ثَنَى||to [[double]], to [[fold]], to [[bend]]}}, {{m|ar|اِثْنَان||[[two]]}}.

The terms {{m|ar|دَقِيقة|t=minute}} and {{m|ar||ثانِية|t=second}} are literally equivalent to {{cog|ML.|minūta}} and {{m|la|secunda}}, which is obviously not coincidental. The exact history of these terms is complicated, but the Arabo-Islamic influence is usually considered greater. {{w|Al-Biruni}} is credited with first having used the measures in the modern sense based on the Ancient Greek division of the circle.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|ثَانِيَة}}
* {{audio|ar|Ar-ثانية.ogg}}
** {{IPA|afb|/ˈθɑːnjə/|a=Kuwaiti}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|ثَانِيَة|f|pl=ثَوَانٍ}}

# [[second]] {{gloss|unit of time}} {{C|ar|Time}}
#* {{quote-book|ar|year=a. 905|author=Al-Yaʿqūbiyy|editor=Abd al-Amīr Muhannā|year_published=2010|title=Tārīkh al-Yaʿqūbī|volume=1|pages=180–181|edition=1st|publisher=ar:شركة الأعلمي للمطبوعات|location=Beirut, Lebanon|passage=إِنَّ مَسِيرَ الشَّمْسِ فِي كُلِّ يَوْمٍ يَكُونُ تِسْعًا وَخَمْسِينَ دَقِيقَةً، وَمَسِيرُ أَوْجِ الْقَمَرِ سَبْعُ دَقَائِقَ، وَمَسِيرُ رَأْسِ التِّنِّينِ، وَهُوَ الْجَوْزَهَرُ، ثَلَاثُ دَقَائِقَ، وَمَسِيرُ زُحَلَ دَقِيقَتَانِ، وَمَسِيرُ الْمُشْتَرِي خَمْسُ دَقَائِقَ، وَمَسِيرُ الْمِرِّيخِ إِحْدَى وَثَلَاثُونَ دَقِيقَةً، وَمَسِيرُ الزُّهَرَةِ دَرَجَةٌ وَسِتٌّ وَثَلَاثُونَ دَقِيقَةً، وَمَسِيرُ عُطَارِدٍ أَرْبَعُ دَرَجٍ وَخَمْسُ دَقَائِقَ، وَمَسِيرُ قَلْبِ الْأَسَدِ سِتُّ '''ثَوانٍ'''.|translation=The path [movement] of the Sun every day is 59 minutes, the path of the Moon’s apogee is 7 minutes, and the path of the Dragon’s Head, which is Jauzahar, is 3 minutes. The path of Saturn is 2 minutes, the path of Jupiter is 5 minutes, the path of Mars is 31 minutes, and the path of Venus is 36 minutes. The path of Mercury is 4 degrees and 5 minutes, and the path of Leo’s heart is 6 seconds.}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|ثَانِيَة|pl=ثَوَانٍ}}

