{{ar-rootbox|ج م ه ر}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ar-etym-iyya|جُمْهُور|[[crowd]], [[public]]}}, a {{clq|ar|la|rēs pūblica|nocap=1}} after the [[French Revolution]] 1789, first in {{bor|ar|ota|جمهوریت|tr={{m|tr|cumhuriyet}}}}, at the beginning used for the new ideology of [[republicanism]], while {{m|ar|جُمْهُور}} was used earlier already for states like the {{w|Republic of Venice}} – an invention of Turkish diplomacy: the [[Classical Arabic]] translation for {{cog|la|rēs pūblica}} and {{cog|grc|πολιτεία}} was {{m|ar|مَدِينَة}}, and the ideological parallel development was represented by the {{m|ar|خِلَافَة}} having an [[elective]] [[head of state]] subject to the [[rule of law]] – and this nisba meaning the form of government only became definite in the middle of the 19th century and broadly known with the rise of Pan-Arabism during World War I. An Arab ''Jumhuriya'' {{w|lang=fr|République de Tripolitaine|was}} proclaimed in Italian-ruled Tripoli in November 1919; however {{w|Azerbaijan Democratic Republic|Azerbaijan}} came before, in May 1918.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|جُمْهُورِيَّة}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|جُمْهُورِيَّة|f|pl=جُمْهُورِيَّات}}

# [[republicanism]]
# [[republic]]

