Page 7210 جن: -------- begin text --------
{{ar-rootbox|ج ن ن}}

===Etymology 1===
Compare {{cog|he|גָּנַן|tr=gānán|t=to defend, to cover}}.

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|I/a~u.pass.vn:جَنّ,جُنُون}}

# {{lb|ar|transitive}} to [[cover]], to [[hide]], to [[conceal]], to [[veil]]
# {{lb|ar|transitive}} to [[envelop]], to [[enshroud]], to [[cloak]], to [[screen]]
# {{lb|ar|transitive}} to [[descend]], to [[fall]], to become [[night]]
#* '''11 Century CE''', [[w:Wallada bint al-Mustakfi|Wallada bint al-Mustakfi]], <big>ترقب إذا جن الظلام زيارتي</big>
#*: {{quote|ar|تَرَقَّبْ إِذَا جَنَّ الظَّلَامُ زِيَارَتِي / فَإِنِّي رَأَيْتُ اللَّيْلَ أَكْتَمَ لِلسِّرِّ|subst=لِلسِّرِّ/لِ-سْ-سِرِّ,فَإِنِّي/فَ-إِنِّي|Await, when darkness falls, my visit. For I found the night to be a better keeper of secrets.}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|I/a~u.pass.vn:جَنّ,جُنُون}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|ar|جَنِين||[[embryo]], [[germ]]}}
* {{l|ar|مِجَنّ||[[shield]]}}

===Etymology 2===
Formally from the root {{ar-root|ج ن ن}}, though most likely a {{bf|ar|جِنِّي|nocap=1}}, derived from {{cog|syc|ܓܢܝ|tr=gnē|t=spirit, genie}}, or its emphatic form {{cog|arc|גניא|tr=ginnāyā}} or {{cog|syc|ܓܢܝܐ|tr=genyā}}, also meaning "a [[tutelary deity]]" or "[[Astaroth]] idols". Compare {{noncog|la|genius}}.

====Noun====
{{ar-coll-noun|جِنّ|m|sing=جَانّ|singg=mf|sing2=جِنِّيّ|sing2g=m|sing3=جِنِّيَّة|sing3g=f|pl=جِنّ|pl2=جِنَّة|pl3=جِنَّان|pl4=جَوَان}}

# {{lb|ar|collective}} [[spiritual]] or otherwise [[unseen]], [[undetectable]], [[mask]]ed, or [[morph]]ed beings that may be [[benevolent]] or [[helpful]] ([[agathodaemon]]s, [[eudaemon]]s), [[neutral]], or [[malevolent]] ([[cacodemon]]s); [[jinn]], [[genie]]s, [[genii]], [[demon]]s.
#: {{syn|ar|جِنَّة|جَوَان|جِنَّان}}
# {{lb|ar|collective}} any [[mythical]] beings in general (such as [[fairy|fairies]], [[satyr]]s, [[nymph]]s, [[elf|elves]], [[goblin]]s, and [[sprite]]s)
# {{lb|ar|informal|in the singular}} a [[genie]], a [[jann]], one of the [[jinn]](s).

=====Usage notes=====
In Arabian and Islamic mythology and demonology, the genies (alternatively referred to in English using the transliteration {{m|en|jinn}}), are conceptualized as a race that lives on earth alongside humans and beasts. Genies, like humans, are not deemed wholly evil or good, but they are said to possess powers that screen them from humankind, such as shapeshifting, which allows them to take up the form of animals (usually snakes and serpents) or even humans. Evil, godless, malicious, or otherwise harmful genies may influence the world indirectly (often through the works of human agents, such as sorcerers and witches) or directly (through the actions of the genies themselves). For example, {{w|Saʽd ibn ʽUbadah|Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah}}, one of the companions of Muhammad, is said to have been supposedly assassinated by a genie who shot him with an arrow while he was urinating alone in the desert, and the second Caliph {{w|Umar ibn al-Khattab|ʽUmar ibn al-Ḵaṭṭāb}}, as reported by {{w|Al-Munawi|Al-Munāwi}} in his [http://shiaonlinelibrary.com/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A8/2287_%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%B6-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%8A%D8%B1-%D8%B4%D8%B1%D8%AD-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%85%D8%B9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D8%BA%D9%8A%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%88%D9%8 Fayḍ al-Qadīr] ("The Flow of the Ablest"), struck dead a {{m|ar|غُول}} (a kind of {{m|ar|جِنّ}}) with his sword, describing the creature as being "with a form like a human but with legs like those of a donkey". The {{m|ar|جِنّ}} are therefore ''not'' to be confused with the race of otherworldly creatures trapped in oil lamps as popularized in English, which, in Arabic, would be only a subtype of {{m|ar|جِنّ}}, like the {{m|ar|قُطْرُب|t=lycanthrope}} and the {{m|ar|سِعْلَاء|t=succubus}} and the chiefly Egyptian {{m|arz|نَدَّاهَة|t=a kind of murderous enchanting river-nymphs or succubi}}. For more information, see the Wikipedia article on {{w|Jinn}}.

The word is also sometimes indiscriminately used when translating the non-Arabic names of other mythological beings (especially fairies and elves). This, however, often creates much confusion, and so various Arabicized forms of the original denominations may be used to avoid this.

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-coll-noun|جِنّ|m|head2=جِنَّة|g2=f|sing=جان|singm=mf|sing2=جِنِّيّ|sing2g=m|sing3=جِنِّيَّة|sing3g=f|pl=جِنّ|pl2=جِنَّة|pl3=جِنَّان|pl4=جَوَان}}

