Page 7253 جنيه: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
19th century, from {{bor|ar|arz|جنيه|tr=ginēh}}, classicized after the diminutive measure {{m|ar|فُعَيْل}}. The Egyptian word is from {{der|ar|en|guinea}}; historically, a guinea was worth one [[pound]] and one [[shilling]]. In view of the (former) importance of French learning in Egypt, the final stress may follow {{der|ar|fr|guinée}}, itself from the English

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|جُنَيْه}}
* {{a|ar|Egypt}} {{ar-IPA|tr=ginēh}}
* {{audio|ar|Ar-جنيه.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|جُنَيْه|m|pl=جُنَيْهَات}}

# [[pound]] {{gloss|unit of currency}}

====Usage notes====
* Most currencies called {{m|en|pound}} in English are known as {{m|ar|جنيه}} in Arabic, including the [[w:pound sterling|pound sterling]] and the [[w:Egyptian pound|Egyptian pound]]; however, the [[w:Lebanese pound|Lebanese pound]] and [[w:Syrian pound|Syrian pound]] are called {{m|ar|لِيرَة||[[lira]]}} in Arabic.

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|جُنَيْه|pl=جُنَيْهَات}}

{{C|ar|Currencies}}
-------- end text --------
