Page 7355 ح ب ن: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Superficially augmented from {{ar-root|ح ب ب|nocat=1}} in its grain meanings, which has perhaps supported this root. Corriente only has {{m|ar|حَبَن|t=oleander}} and suggests its origin in {{bor|ar|egy|ḥbn|t=to stab, to chef}} owing to other names of the plant referring to its deadliness: {{m|ar|قَاتِل الْحَمِير}}, {{m|ar|سَمّ الْحِمَار}}, {{m|ar|سَمّ الْبَهَائِم}}, {{m|ar|جَوْزَهْرَج}}. This seems even more verisimile if one wares how notorious {{taxfmt|Nerium oleander|species}} is for its repellent effect: The meanings of dropsy then derives from it, see the tree {{w|Zaqqum}} in Qurʾānic Hell, from the fruits of which the digestive tracts of its inhabitants boil and spit. {{m|ar|زَقُّوم}} served as another name for “oleander”, at least it is its most common name in {{cog|tr|-}}, {{m|tr|zakkum}}, beside {{m|tr|ağı ağacı|lit=poison tree}}; {{m|tr|zıkkım}} means “1. poison 2. harmful habit such as smoking 3. an annoyance, a vex”. In Greek mythology {{w|Daphne}} was a fatal {{l|en|nymph}}, from whose name the oleander’s other Arabic name {{m|ar|دِفْلَى}} derives. The chamaeleon has its name by its venomous colour, and the monkey from his protruding red rear end.

