Page 7562 حتى: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
* Traditionally, Arabic grammarians link it to the root {{ar-root|ح|ت|ت}} in the senses of "to scrape or rub off", "to remove or destroy", "to end something", "to drive back or repel"; hence the meaning of an end-limit, the ending barrier or the point that holds back. Such a derivation would be highly irregular.
* Some classical dialects attest the word as {{l|ar|عَتَّى}}, which is closer to the usual Semitic form found in {{cog|uga|𐎓𐎄}}, {{cog|he|עַד|tr=ʿaḏ}}, {{cog|arc|עַד|tr=ʿaḏ}}, and {{cog|akk|𒀀𒁲|tr=adi}}. If this is related, it could be connected with the root {{ar-root|ع|ت|ي}}, meaning "to be excessive", "to push the limits or be at the threshold", "to be extremely old or at the extreme end of life".
* Al-Jallad proposes an etymology from {{m|ar|حَدّ||limit}} + a conjunction *''tay'', as seen in {{m|ar|مَتَى|t=when|lit=what + *''tay''}} and {{cog|apc|تَـ|tr=ta-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|ar|Ar-حتى.ogg}}

===Preposition===
{{ar-prep|حَتَّى}}

# [[until]], [[as far as]], [[to]] [[the]] [[point]] [[of]], [[even]] [[up]] [[to]]
#: {{ux|ar|أَكَلْتُ ٱلسَّمَكَةَ حَتَّى رَأْسِهَا|tr=ʾakaltu s-samakata '''ḥattā''' raʾsihā|t=I ate the fish, '''until''' its head}}

===Conjunction===
{{ar-con|حَتَّى}}

# [[until]]
#: {{synonyms|ar|[[إِلَى]] [[أَنْ]]}}
# [[in order to]], [[so that]]
#: {{synonyms|ar|كَيْ|لِ|لِأَنْ|لِكَيْ|[[مِنْ أَجْلِ]] أَنْ|[[لِأَجْلِ]] أَنْ}}
# [[even]]
#: {{ux|ar|خَرَجَ النَّاسُ '''حَتَّى''' ٱلْأَطْفَالُ.|tr=|t=The people went forth, '''even''' the children}}
#: {{ux|ar|كُلُّ تَعْمِيمٍ بَاطِلٌ، '''حَتَّى''' هَذَا التَّعْمِيمُ.|translation=All generalizations are false, '''including''' this one.|subst=هَذَا/هَاذَا}}

====Usage notes====
* {{m-self|ar|حَتَّى}} is followed under most conveyed circumstances by the [[subjunctive mood]]; the details are complicated, see Wild 1980 for more.
* Unlike {{l|ar|إِلَى}} and {{l|ar|عَلَى}}, this preposition never has the pronominal suffixes added to it: {{l|ar||*حَتَّيَّ}}, {{l|ar||*حَتَّيْكَ}}, etc. do not exist.

===Adverb===
{{ar-adv|حَتَّى}}

# [[even]], [[even]] [[this]], [[including]], [[even]] [[if]]
# [[finally]], [[until]] [[finally]]
# {{lb|ar|rare|disputed}} [[unless]], [[only if]]

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ar|حَتَّامَ}}

===Descendants===
* {{desc|ary|حتى|tr=ḥatta}}
* {{desc|lad|ḥatta|bor=1}} {{qualifier|Haketia}}
* {{desc|mt|sa|unc=1|qq=possibly}}
* {{desctree|fa|حَتّی|tr=hattâ|bor=1}}
* {{desc|roa-opt|até|bor=1|unc=1|qq=possibly}}
** {{desc|pt|até}}
* {{desc|osp|fata|fasta|bor=1|unc=1|qq=possibly}}
** {{desc|es|hasta}}
* {{desc|so|xitaa|bor=1}}
* {{desc|sw|hata|bor=1}}
* {{desc|ug|ھەتتا|bor=1}}

===Trivia===
[[w:Al-Farra’|ʾAbū Zakariyyā al-Farrāʾ]] (761-822 C.E.), an influential grammarian, famously said: “ʾamūtu wafī nafsī min ḥattā šayʾun” – “I shall die, while in my soul there is something off about ''ḥatta''”, referring to his frustrations with its usage and grammatical complexities.

===References===
* {{R:ar:Lane|حت|pages=508-510}}
* {{R:ar:Lane|عتى|page=1951}}; specifically mentions the dialects of Huḏayl and Thaqīf.
* Al-Jallad, Ahmad (2018) "The Etymology of Ḥattā", in ''To the Madbar and Back Again: Studies in the languages, archaeology, and cultures of Arabia dedicated to Michael C.A. Macdonald'', eds. L. Nehmé and A. Al-Jallad. Leiden: Brill.
* {{R:uga:DUL-3|pages=142–143|ʿd}}
* {{cite-book|last=Sadan|first=Arik|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=dP4bIv8BEPgC&lpg=PA197&dq=hatta%20arabic%20subjunctive&pg=PA197#v=onepage&q&f=true|chapter=ḤATTĀ|year=2012|title=The Subjunctive Mood in Arabic Grammatical Thought|location=Leiden|publisher=Brill|pages=197–249}}
* {{cite-book|last=Wild|first=Stefan|chapter=Die Konjunktion ''ḥattā'' mit dem Indikativ Perfekt im klassischen Arabisch|title=Studien aus Arabistik und Semitistik. Anton Spitaler zum siebzigsten Geburtstag, von seinen Schülern überreicht|editors=Diem, Werner; Wild, Stefan|publisher=Otto Harrassowitz|location=Wiesbaden|year=1980|pages=204–223}}

