Page 7579 حجل: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
The absence of farther cognates suggests a borrowing of the name for the bird from {{bor|ar|syc|ܚܰܓܠܴܐ|tr=ḥaḡlā}}, {{m|syc|ܚܰܓܠܬܴܐ|tr=ḥaḡəlṯā|t=partridge}}, also appearing early, although by itself the term is unsuspicious and cognacy cannot be excluded. Although there is a potential semantic connection to the verb “to hop”, this would require a synonymous verb to exist in a common ancestor of Arabic and Syriac as the latter is, owing to its age, unlikely to be a borrowing from Arabic, so if the words are related, the verb would have to be denominal. In that case, the word for an anklet could also be denominal, naming a thing on the part of the body used to hop; however there is also a connection to {{ar-root|ح ج ر|nocat=1}} and its Semitic matches related to detaining and {{cog|arc|חֲגַל|tr=ḥăḡal}} / {{m|syc|ܚܓܰܠ|tr=ḥgal|t=to draw a circle about, to gird}}, possibly the source verb of the name of the partridge due to its going around or due to its bulbous body depending on the species, {{cog|gez|ሐገለ|t=to tie, to bind}}, also {{cog|gez|ሐግል|t=shackle, fetter}}, and the surrounding meaning is again found for the root ''ḥ-g-l'' in {{cog|sem-srb|-}} and {{cog|sqt|-}}, which implies together with the consideration of workmanship lacking in the [[Najd]] that instead, the word for an anklet or shackle was borrowed into Arabic from a more southern Semitic language, from which the word for hopping was then derived, whereas the word for the partridge is to be seen as borrowed from {{cog|arc|-}} to account for its etymology.

