Page 7628 حرباء: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Chamaeleo africanus at Hurghada desert by Hatem Moushir 75.JPG|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|حِرْبَاء}}|182px]]
{{slim-wikipedia|lang=ar}}

===Etymology===
* From {{l|ar|حَرْبَة|t=dart, javelin; weapon in a general sense}} from the root {{ar-root|ح|ر|ب}}; likely a reference to the projectile-like tongue of a {{l|en|chameleon}}. Cognate with {{cog|akk|𒁇𒁯𒁯𒉡|ts=ḫurbabillu|t=chameleon}} and {{cog|xeb|𒄯𒁀𒌝|tr=ḫur.ba.um|t=chameleon}}.
* Deriving from the root {{ar-root|ح|ر|ب}} in the sense of "becoming enraged or to grow agitated", as the changing of color is used idiomatically to denote changes in states or moods in Semitic Languages.
* Alternatively, philologist [[w:Georg Freytag|Georg Freytag]] lists it as from a Persian {{m|fa|خربا|tr=xorbâ}} (> {{m|fa|خوربان|tr=xwarbân|t=sun-keeper}}), giving the meaning “guardian of the sun”; perhaps related to {{noncog|fa|آفتاب‌پرست|tr=âftâb-parast|t=chameleon|lit=sun-worshiper}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|حِرْبَاء}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|حِرْبَاء|m|pl=حَرَابِيّ}}

# [[chameleon]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|حِرْبَاء|pl=حَرَابِيّ}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|ar|جَمَل اليَهُود|tr=|lit=Jewish camel}}

===References===
* {{R:Dehkhoda|خربا}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=1|page=361}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=1|pages=403}}
* {{R:ar:Lane|pages=540–541}}
* {{R:akk:CDA|page=121}}
* Leonid Kogan (2006), [https://books.google.com/books?id=RfCV3LAg0BcC&lpg=PA298&dq=nin.hur.ba.um&pg=PA298#v=onepage&q&f=true Animal Names: 4.3. Lizards] in ''Babel und Bibel 3: Annual of Ancient Near Eastern, Old Testament, and Semitic Studies'', Winona Lake, Indiana: Eisenbrauns in conjunction with the Russian State University for Humanities, page 298, note 177

{{C|ar|Lizards}}
