Mandaville observes that “there are a few unexpected absences in the old data [i. e. of classical Arabic plant name uses] […] We also look in vain for two important annuals with edible rootstock: [[ربحلة|rubaḥlah]] and ḥambizān”. Steiger argues that the forms for this sour root vegetable are actually the same word as {{m|ar|زَنْبُوع|t=pomelo}}, {{m|ar|زَنْبَاع|t=grapefruit}}, positing these as progressively sonorized from {{m|ar|زَنْبُوح}}, {{m|ar||*زَنْبَاح}}, in a chain from the now obsolete form {{m|ar|حُنْزُوب}} to {{m|ar||*زُنْحُوب}} to {{m|ar|زُنْبُوح}} to {{m|ar|زُنْبُوع}} and a chain {{m|ar|حِنْزَاب}} → {{m|ar||*زِنْبَاح}} → {{m|ar||زَنْبَاع}}, and also deeming the same term present in {{cog|ca|alambó}}, {{m|ca|alambor|t=bitter orange}}, ultimately explaining all as from the transparent term {{m|ar|حُمَّاض}}, vulgarly {{m|ar|حَمَّاض|t=sorrel}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|حَمْبِزَان}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|حَمْبِزَان|m}}

# {{taxfmt|Emex|genus}} gen. et spp. {{gloss|the [[taproot]] and [[petiole]]s, of {{taxlink|Emex spinosa|species}} at least, are eaten}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|حَمْبِزَان}}

===Further reading===
* {{pedia|lang=ar}}
* {{R:ar:Mandaville|B|pages=107, 154, 294, 336–337}}
* {{R:Steiger-alambor|pages=231–244 and from 241 about this word}}

{{C|ar|Buckwheat family plants|Vegetables}}
