#: {{syn|ar|نَصِيف|عِمَامَة}}

====Usage notes====
Although both {{m|ar|خِمَار}} and {{m|ar|حِجَاب}} are very common words that refer to religiously and/or socially prescribed [[veil]] or [[headgear]] worn by women, the former, which could originally be used in texts written in the Classical idiom to refer to any veil, is now often associated with looser and longer, and hence more proper, clothes and garments (such as a {{m|ar|جِلْبَاب|t=a robe}} or a {{m|ar|مِلْحَفَة|t=a typically thick and very long outer garment}}), while the latter word, which originally meant "[[barrier]]", has become a more general term referring to a cloth draped about the head of a woman and is thus often translated as "[[scarf]]" or "[[headscarf]]". For the face coverings, see {{m|ar|بُرْقُع}} and {{m|ar|نِقَاب}}.

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|خِمَار|pl=أَخْمِرَة|pl2=خُمُر}}

===Noun===
