===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|syc|ܕܘܪܩܝܢܐ|tr=dōraqqīnā}}, from {{der|ar|grc|δωράκινον}}, from {{der|ar|la|dūracinum}}, nominative neuter singular of {{m|la|dūracinus|t=hard-berried}}, from {{m|la|dūrus|t=hard}}, which is applied for peaches that are firm-fleshed and cling to the stone – [[clingstone]]s as distinguished from [[freestone]]s – and are apt to be conserved rather than to be eaten. Such peaches are called to this day in {{noncog|de|Härtling|Härtlinge|lit=hardlings}} from {{m|de|hart|t=hard}} as distinguished from {{m|de|Kerngeher|lit=kern-goers}}.

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|دُرَّاق|m}}

# [[peach]]
#: {{syn|ar|خَوْخ}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|دُرَّاق}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|kmr|teraqî|bor=1|alts=1}}
* {{desc|ota|دراقی|tr=durrakı|bor=1}}
** {{desc|tr|durakı}}

