# {{lb|ar|of soil}} [[richness]], [[fertility]]
#* {{quote-book|ar|year=1203|author=ar:عبد اللطيف بن يوسف البغدادي<t:{{w|Abd al-Latif al-Baghdadi|ʿAbd-al-Laṭīf Ibn Yūsuf al-Baġdādīy}}>|title=Compendium memorabilium Aegypti|editor={{w|Joseph White (orientalist)|Joseph White}}|year_published=1789 [https://books.google.com/books?id=pOPJpWszrAoC&pg=PA3#v=onepage&q&f=false <sup>GB</sup>] and 1800 [https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.358151/page/n53 <sup>Archive</sup>]|url=https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_g5jbaLFi5mIC/page/n3|section=[https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_g5jbaLFi5mIC/page/n14 page 3] 1789, [https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/white1800/0060 page 6] 1800|passage=إن أرض مصر رملية لا تصلح للزراعة، لكنه يأتيها طين أسود علك فيه '''دسومة''' كثيرة يسمى الإيليز؛ يأتيها من بلاد السودان مختلطًا بماء النيل عند مده، فيستقر الطين، وينضب الماء، فيحرث ويزرع. وكل سنة يأتيها طين جديد، ولهذا يزرع جميع أراضيها ولا يراح شيء منها، كما يفعل في العراق والشام.|t=The land of Egypt is sandy and unsuitable for agriculture, but to it comes a black viscose mud of much '''richness''' called ''ʾiblīz''; it comes from the lands of the Sudan mingled with Nile water at rising tide, and the mud having settled and the water having drained one can plough and sow. And every year new mud comes to it and most of the regions of Egypt are sown by this, and it wanes by no jot, unlike it does in Iraq or Syria.}}

