Page 8832 دقل: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ar|sem-wes-pro|*daqal-}}, in {{cog|akk|-}} only found in the toponymy of [[Chaldean]] territory, and likewise the Arabic is suspect to be influenced by {{sl|ar|arc|דִקְלָא|tr=diqlā|t=date palm; topgallant|nocap=1}} for the sense of a “mast”, compare also {{m|ar|سَارِيَة|t=post, mast}} from Aramaic, though the mast sense is also found in {{cog|gdq||dəqāl|sc=Latn|t=mast}}, {{cog|sqt||daqal|sc=Latn|t=pole}}. In {{cog|he|דֶּגֶל|tr=déḡel|t=a flag}}.

The sort of dates denoted by this word varies according to time. In antiquity, it denoted in particular a date palm that produced an abundant number of dates, however of an inferior quality, often characterized by being hard, small, or having a meager ratio of flesh compared to its pit. In modernity the meaning has switched to denote a supreme kind of date; the semantic shift seems to have been brought on by the popular variety {{l|ar|دَقْلَة النُور|tr=|t=heavenly or divine dates|lit=date-palm of light}}, so-named for producing a harvest as numerous as the ill-tasting variety, yet instead of being the expected unpleasant taste, they have a notoriously heavenly soft honey-like taste.

{{wp|lang=ar|دقلة النور}}

===Noun===
{{ar-coll-noun|دَقَل|sing=دَقَلَة}}

# a [[variety]] of [[date]]s {{gloss|fruit and tree}}
#* {{RQ:Sunna|muslim|55|43
|passage=أَلَسْتُمْ فِي طَعَامٍ وَشَرَابٍ مَا شِئْتُمْ – لَقَدْ رَأَيْتُ نَبِيَّكُمْ صَلَّى اللّٰهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَمَا يَجِدُ مِنَ الدَّقَلِ مَا يَمْلَأُ بِهِ بَطْنَهُ؟
|translation=Don’t you have food and drink as you will, whereas I have seen your prophet not even finding the worst kind of dates to fill his belly?
|subst=بِهِ/بِ-هِي,أَلَسْتُمْ/أَ-لَسْتُمْ,وَشَرَابٍ/وَ-شَرَابٍ,بَطْنَهُ/بَطْنَهُو,وَمَا/وَ-مَا}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-coll-noun|دَقَل}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|دَقَل|m|pl=دِقَال|pl2=أَدْقَال}}

