Page 8846 دلاع: -------- begin text --------
[[File:البطيخ الأحمر.JPG|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|دَلَّاع}}]]

===Etymology===
Considered sometimes a {{bor|ar|ber}} borrowing,<ref>{{cite-journal|journal=Annals of Botany|first=Harry S.|last=Paris|title=Origin and emergence of the sweet dessert watermelon, ''Citrullus lanatus''|year=2015|issue=116|doi=10.1093/aob/mcv077|url=http://aob.oxfordjournals.org/content/early/2015/07/03/aob.mcv077.full.pdf|page=145}}; a treatise which has been summarized by {{cite-web|first=Mark|last=Strauss|title=The 5,000-Year Secret History of the Watermelon|url=https://news.nationalgeographic.com/2015/08/150821-watermelon-fruit-history-agriculture/|date=2015-08-21|work=National Geographic}}.</ref> which would coincide with the {{w|Watermelon#History|spread of the watermelon}} in the Early Middle Ages from [[West Africa]], however first attested in {{cog|arc-imp|-}} as a plural {{m|arc|𐡃𐡋𐡏𐡍|t=gourds}} in the first quarter of the 5<sup>th</sup> century {{tooltip|before Common Era|BCE}},<ref>On which {{R:CAL|dl(t|N}}.</ref> and it occurs in {{cog|he|-}} from the [[Mishnaic]] period as {{m|he|דְּלַעַת|tr=dəlaʿáṯ|t=gourd}}. The Arabic root {{ar-root|د|ل|ع|nocat=1}} related to “sticking out, hanging out” is connected to the word<ref>Already {{R:he:Hiller:1725|volume=2|page=234}}.</ref> in view of the watermelon’s way of growth. That it is first attested in Aramaic and appears only more than half a millennium later in Hebrew, sometimes with an Aramaizing plural ending, and the measure which the Arabic exposes varies in the first vowel and is in both cases for this semantic field more common in Aramaic than Arabic, points to the word having passed into Arabic from {{bor|ar|arc|-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|دَلَّاع|دُلَّاع}}

===Noun===
