Page 9028 ديوث: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{ar-rootbox|د ي ث}}
The presence of [[homorganic]] radicals, especially at the first and third positions in what appears to be a [[quadriconsonantal]] root, suggests that the word's origin may not be authentically Arabic. This can be seen in words like {{m|ar|غُنْج}}, {{m|xaa|قَلَطِي|tr=qalaṭī}}, {{m|sem-pro|*waral-}}, and {{m|qfa-hur-pro|*kinnar}}, among others. Additionally, the suffix ''-ūṯ'' is not native to Arabic, but it does exist in {{cog|sem-ara|Aramaic}}, as seen in clear borrowings like {{m|ar|جَبَرُوت}}. Moreover, words such as {{m|ar|حُوت}}, {{m|ar|حَانُوت}}, {{m|ar|سَنُّوت}}, {{m|ar|تُوت}}, {{m|ar|تُوث}}, {{m|ar|طَرْثُوث}}, and {{m|ar|كُشُوث}} that relate to natural phenomena could also have Aramaic origins.

Apparently, the Aramaic words {{m|arc|דַּיְתָא|tr=dayyəṯā}} and {{m|arc|דַּיּוּתָא|tr=dayyūṯā|t=kite}} are equivalent to {{doublet|ar|حَدَأَة|notext=1|t1=kite}} in Arabic. It's possible that in ancient Near East culture, which is not fully captured in surviving sources, these words had an [[Appendix:Aramaic terms only attested in borrowings|additional meaning]] of 'cuckoo' or symbolized a [[cuckold]]. Intriguingly, bird names like 'kite' and 'cuckoo' in Indo-European languages often share [[onomatopoeia|onomatopoetic]] origins.

This could imply that male [[Aramaeans|Aramaean]] visitors, such as traders ({{m|ar|تاجر|تُجَّار}}), might have engaged in sexual liaisons with {{w|Women in pre-Islamic Arabia|pre-Islamic Arabic-speaking tribes, which were sometimes matriarchal}}. This cultural exchange could have influenced language and semantics, as exemplified in words like {{m|ar|فَتْوَى}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|دَيُّوث}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|دَيُّوث|m}}

