Page 9903 زعتر: -------- begin text --------
[[File:زعتر سبلة.jpg|frameless|right|{{lang|ar|زَعْتَر}}|upright=1.2]]
[[File:Green zaʿtar.jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|زَعْتَر}}|upright=1.15]]
{{wikipedia|lang=ar}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|صَعْتَر|سَعْتَر}}

===Etymology===
Assumed to be from {{bor|ar|arc|צתרא|tr=ṣaṯrā, ṣāṯrā}} / {{m|syc|ܨܬܪܐ|tr=ṣaṯrā, ṣāṯrā}}, the {{IPAchar|/a/}} + {{IPAchar|/ʕ/}} in Arabic a regular resolution of {{IPAchar|/aː/}} giving a word an Arabic shape, like in {{noncog|ar|لَعْل}} for {{noncog|fa|لال|tr=lâl}}, or the {{IPAchar|/ʕ/}} was previously present in Aramaic too. The form with the onset corresponding to the Aramaic, {{m|ar|صَعْتَر}}, is the most classical in Arabic.

The ultimate origin is {{unknown|ar|nocap=1|title=speculative}}; the word is also found in {{cog|he|צַתְרָה|tr=ṣátrā}}, {{cog|ku|-}} {{m|kmr|catirî}}, {{m|ckb|جاترە}}, {{cog|axm|ծոթրին}}, and {{cog|la|saturēia}}. In one old view all are ultimately from the Latin term, the origin of which is {{unknown|ar|nocap=1}}.

In Mandaville’s conception, there is an “old demonstrative ''s''” seen in {{cog|akk|𒊭|ts=ša}} “with the semantic effect of” {{cog|ar|ذُو}}  prefixed to {{m|ar|عِطْر|t=scent}}, hence the word means “that which has scent”. (Or is it the plant name {{m|ar|عِتْر}}? He writes ''ʿitr'' and glosses it “perfume”.)

However the oldest forms seem to be found in {{cog|akk|𒌑𒍝𒋼𒊒|tr=<sup>U<sub>2</sub></sup>.za-te-ru|ts=zateru, ṣateru|t=savory and the {{taxfmt|Origanum|genus}} family}} found in lexical lists connected to {{noncog|akk|𒌑𒈛𒈥𒌅|tr=<sup>U<sub>2</sub></sup>.LUḪ.MAR.TU|ts=šibburratu|t=an aromatic herb}} and {{noncog|akk|𒍝𒄠𒁍𒊒𒊬|tr=za-am-bu-ru<sup>SAR</sup>|ts=zambūru|t=thyme}}.

Of further curiosity is the possible attestation of the {{IPAchar|/ʕ/}} preserved in cuneiform; it is common for the zero reflex of {{IPAchar|/ʕ/}}, to only be detectable in Akkadian by the e-coloring of an adjacent *a. The distance between Arabic and Akkadian and the lack of its presence in their likely intermediaries, possibly suggests a form of further common origin, albeit with a metathesis of {{IPAchar|/ʕ/}} and {{IPAchar|/t/}}, and a retention of that form in Arabic, but not so in other Semitic tongues.

