-------- end text --------
Page 10077 س ج ن: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
The root derives from the noun {{m|ar|سِجْن}}, which is borrowed from {{bor|ar|gkm|σίγνον}}, from {{bor|ar|la|sīgnum}}, so called because the Roman field standard ({{m|la|sīgnum}}), as well as the [[war chest]]s and soldiers’ savings, were kept in the {{m|la|aedēs}}, in the center of the headquarters ({{m|la|principium|principia}}). The {{w|Aquila (Roman)|eagle token}} being central to Roman ideology and religion, the warden of these monies was called {{m|la|sīgnifer}} and {{m|la|aquilifer}} and anything deposited there was brought ''ad signum''. As this cellared building was well guarded, arrestees where brought thither – in Constantinople the {{m|la|praetōrium}} of the {{w|eparch of the city}} –, so  the colloquial Greek attested from the 4th to 7th century used the phrase {{m|grc||ἐν τοῖς σίγνοις}} for “into prison”.

Accordingly the Arabic {{m|ar|سِجْن}} is first attested by an (albeit like many possibly forged) verse of {{w|Umaiya ibn Abī s-Salt|lang=de|Umayya ibn Abī ṣ-Ṣalt}} {{l|ar||أَيُّمَا شَاطِنٍ عَصَاهُ عَكَاهُ … ثُمَّ يُلْقَى فِي ٱلسِّجْنِ وَٱلْأَْكْبَالِ}}, then in the Umayyad era by {{w|Su'luk|brigand-poets}}. That is because the nomadic Arabs, obviously, had no prisons, which were only known in the periphery settled by Byzantines, instead the outer punishment was exclusion from the tribal community which was likely to entail death. The other words {{m|ar|دِيمَاس}} and {{m|ar|مَطْمُورَة}} meaning places to lock up miscreants were {{bor|ar|arc|-}} loanwords too.

As the occurrences of the word and derivatives in the Qurʾān are all from the {{w|Yusuf (surah)|Sūra Yūsuf}} and in [https://quran.com/26/29 26:29] again about the Pharaoh threating with imprisonment, the term has been repeatedly suggested as mediated via {{cog|cop|-}}, where the word also occurs, however it is just that the Pharaonic Egypt was imagined like the recent Greco-Roman Egypt, while Coptic was limited to Egypt, unlikely to lend any loanwords to Arabic before its Arab colonization, unlike {{bor|ar|arc|-}}, where the Greek plural was borrowed, possibly heard by Arabs as its [[emphatic state]] to be clipped into the state {{m|ar||سِجْن}}. But since Arabs were in Byzantine military service and its command language was Latin, till the 7th century ending bilingualism, it could well have been borrowed from either Greek or Latin directly.

===Root===
{{ar-root}}

# related to [[gaol]]ing or [[jail]]ing

====Derived terms====
; Verbs
{{ar-verb forms|a|I=to throw into gaol|I-vn-head=سَجْن|II=+}}

