# ''has meanings related to withdrawing, dragging, pulling out''

====Derived terms====
; Verbs:
{{ar-verb forms|I=+|VII=+}}
; Nouns
* {{l|ar|سَحَاب}}
-------- end text --------
Page 10079 س ح ر: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
The “sorcery” significations according to Barth correspond to {{cog|arc|-}} {{m|syc|ח־ר־שׁ}}, {{syc-root-entry|ܚ|ܪ|ܫ|t=related to magic}}, {{cog|he|ח־ר־שׁ}}, {{cog|gez|ሐ ረ ሰ|tr=ḥ-r-s}}, whereas Fraenkels points out that the Arabic might instead be related to {{m|he|שׁ־ח־ר|t=blackness}} (present by borrowing in Arabic as {{ar-root|ش ح ر|nocat=1}}), and Nöldeke thought the Geʿez is borrowed from Aramaic, which Leslau reproaches with {{cog|sgw||araši|t=one who has the power of casting the evil eye}}, {{cog|bej|hariš|t=magic}}. Von Soden sees a connection with {{cog|akk|𒄩𒊏𒋗|ts=ḫarāšu|t=to bind, to tie}} because of magical knot imagery. However the word can well be related to {{cog|akk|𒊓𒄩𒊒|tr=sa-ḫa-ru|ts=saḫāru|t=to turn; to circumambulate; to circle, to curve; to encircle with magic; to repel etc.}} allowing for reconstruction of {{inh|ar|sem-pro|*sax̣ar-}}. In any case the “dawn” root constitutes a separate reconstruction {{der|ar|sem-pro|*šaḥ(a)r-}} and the Arabic root formula is polyphyletic.

===Root===
{{ar-root}}

