Page 10210 سالوس: -------- begin text --------
{{ar-rootbox|س ل س}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|شَالُوس}}

===Etymology===
In view of the variation between {{m|ar||س}} and {{m|ar||ش}} and the pattern which is not part of Arabic grammar, but {{bor|ar|arc|-}} grammar and loans, we may be completely wrong to associate with {{m|ar|سَلِس|t=pliant}} and the word will then by neither Arabic nor Iranian nor Greek. However the {{m|ar|لُغَةُ السِّينِ}} of the Sāsānī Gypsies is known for being “a mixed language that takes the form of embedding a substitutive vocabulary into the grammatical
structure of other languages” without independent grammar, a “para-language” with intentionally opaque patches (Richardson 2017 pp. 122, 138, 151). See also the obscure Greek {{m|grc|σόλος|t=a lump of iron used in throwing exercises}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|سَالُوس}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|سَالُوس|m}}

# {{tlb|ar|obsolete|criminal slang}} [[slick]] [[word]]s, [[blandishment]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|سَالُوس}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ar|شَوْلَسَ|t=to pull a confidence trick}}

====Descendants====
* {{→}} Late {{desc|grc-koi|σαλός|t=fool}}
* {{desc|fa-cls|سالوس|tr=sālūs|bor=1|t=deceit; deceiver; deceitful}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:ar:MIU|II|page=231|passage=Dr. D.N. MacKenzie tells me that this word has no known Iranian etymology, and may accordingly be a loanword in Persian.}}
* {{R:ar:Richardson:2017}}
* {{R:ar:Shahîd|V|page=268 fn. 173}}, where the Greek is declared to be possibly from Persian, obviously since the Arabic is found in no normal place

-------- end text --------
