{{ar-rootbox|س ن ن}}
Compare {{m|ar|سَنَّ||to [[establish]] {{qualifier|a [[law]], [[custom]], etc.}}}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|سُنَّة}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|سُنَّة|f|pl=سُنَن}}

# {{lb|ar|countable}} a [[usual]], [[recurrent]], [[continual]], [[determinable]], or [[constant]] [[thing]]
## {{lb|ar|countable}} a [[common]], [[habitual]], [[popularized]], or [[enforced]] [[practice]]; a [[custom]], [[convention]], or [[ritual]]; a [[social]] [[norm]] or [[standard]]
##: {{syn|ar|عُرْف|عَادَة|تَقْلِيد}}
##* {{quote-book|ar|author=Ibn Jinnī|year=a. 1002|title=الخصائص|passage=وهذا عادة للعرب مألوفة و'''سنة''' مسلوكة: إذا أعطوا شيئًا من شيء حكمًا ما قابلوا ذلك بأن يعطوا المأخوذ منه حكمًا من أحكام صاحبه عمارة لبينهما وتتميمًا للشبه الجامع لهما. وعليه باب ما لا ينصرف ألا تراهم لما شبهوا الاسم بالفعل فلم يصرفوه؛ كذلك شبهوا الفعل بالاسم فأعربوه|t=This is a familiar Arab custom and a recurrent '''practice'''. For when they judged a part of some thing to be such-and-such, they also qualified the thing itself from which a part has been taken as having some attribute of the part so as to balance the whole and the part and to formulate their commonly shared similarities. And on this, nunation (or the lack thereof) was modeled. Do you not see how they sometimes likened the noun to the verb and thus did not nunate it and other times likened the verb to the noun and thus suffixed it in the manner of nouns?}}
## {{lb|ar|uncountable}} the [[conduct]], [[habits]], [[behavior]], or [[mannerisms]] of a [[person]] {{gloss|viewed collectively}}
## {{lb|ar|countable}} a [[determinate]] or [[predetermined]] [[universal]] [[law]] (either [[normative]] or [[historical]])
##: {{ux|ar|'''سُنَنُ''' الْكَوْنِ وَٱلتَّارِيخِ|the [determinative] '''laws''' of history and the universe}}
##* {{RQ:Qur'an|33
|passage=لَئِن لَّمْ يَنتَهِ الْمُنَافِقُونَ وَالَّذِينَ فِي قُلُوبِهِم مَّرَضٌ وَالْمُرْجِفُونَ فِي الْمَدِينَةِ لَنُغْرِيَنَّكَ بِهِمْ ثُمَّ لا يُجَاوِرُونَكَ فِيهَا إِلاَّ قَلِيلا / مَلْعُونِينَ أَيْنَمَا ثُقِفُوا أُخِذُوا وَقُتِّلُوا تَقْتِيلا / '''سُنَّةَ''' اللَّهِ فِي الَّذِينَ خَلَوْا مِن قَبْلُ وَلَن تَجِدَ لِسُنَّةِ اللَّهِ تَبْدِيلا
|translation=If the dissimulators, those whose hearts are diseased, and the [demoralizing] scaremongers in Medina do not stop, We shall [first] incite you against them, and then they shall never neighbor you in it but [very] rarely. / Cursed [are they] wherever they are espied—they shall be captured and slaughtered! / [Such was] the '''Law''' of Allah for those who perished in the past, and you shall never find any alteration in the Law of Allah.}}
# {{lb|ar|uncountable|Islam}} the [[body]] of [[narrative]]s [[attribute]]d to [[Islamic]] [[religious]] [[figure]]s {{gloss|viewed collectively}}
# {{lb|ar|countable|Islam}} a [[religious]]ly [[canonize]]d [[tradition]] or [[practice]]
#: {{ant|ar|بِدْعَة|زَنْدَقَة|t1=an uncanonical or heterodox innovation; a heterodoxy; an newly introduced deviation or degeneration|t2=a heresy}}
## {{lb|ar|uncountable|Islam|see usage notes}} the [[set]] of [[canonical]] [[traditions]] whence [[orthodoxy]] and [[orthopraxy]] are [[derived]] {{gloss|viewed collectively}}
##: {{hyper|ar|شَرِيعَة|t=the set of religious ordinances}}
## {{lb|ar|uncountable|Islam|approving|see usage notes}} [[adherence]] to the [[religious]] [[traditions]], [[traditionalism]]; [[orthodoxy]] and [[orthopraxy]]
##: {{ant|ar|بِدْعَة|زَنْدَقَة|t1=heterodoxy; heteropraxy|t2=misbelief, heresy}}
## {{lb|ar|countable|Islam}} a [[traditional]] [[religious]] [[practice]] for which there is a [[divine]] [[reward]] but for whose [[omission]] there is no [[punishment]], a [[commendable]] [[supererogatory]] [[act]] often [[done]] as an [[expression]] of [[faith]], a [[religious]] [[work]] of [[supererogation]]
##: {{cot|ar|فَرْض}}
##:
# {{lb|ar|Islam|see usage notes}} the [[Sunni]] [[sect]] or the [[adherent]]s thereof {{gloss|viewed collectively}}; [[Sunnism]]
#: {{ux|ar|مَا أَبْرَزُ الِاخْتِلَافَاتِ الْفِقْهِيَّةِ بَيْنَ '''السُّنَّةِ''' وَالشِّيعَة؟|What are the major jurisprudential differences between '''Sunnites''' and Shiites?|subst=الِاخْتِلَافَاتِ/الِٱخْتِلَافَاتِ}}
# {{lb|ar|countable|obsolete}} an [[image]], a [[form]], an [[appearance]], a [[look]]
#* {{RQ:Awwam|7|54|volume=1|pages=403–404|passage=أما العليق فمعروف وأما الورد الجبلي وورد الكلب وهو المعروف عند أهل الطب بالنسرين قال أبو حنيفة الورد الجبلي يشبه الورد ويشبه ذلك بعض '''سنة''' العليق وثمره شبيه العنب الدليك وهو أحمر يشبه البسر إلا أن طرفه محدود وفي داخله شبه الصوف ونواره نوار الورد أبيض يشوبه حمرة|translation=In what concerns the bramble, it is well known, and in what concerns the mountain-rose and the dog-rose, it is well-known among the physicians as ''nisrīn''; Abū Ḥanīfa said the mountain-rose resembles the rose, and this resembles a bit the '''image''' of the bramble, and the fruit is similar to the dust-vine; it is red and resembles the unripe date, except that its tip is pointed, and in its inner there is a kind of wool, and its blossom is that of a white rose with an admixture of red.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In the sense of “religious traditions”, the term is almost always approving, as it also implies adherence to the said traditions, hence orthodoxy in general. As such, it has come to be a common self-designation for diverse groups, and so it is never used to refer to ideological and sectarian rivals in polemics.
* In the sense of “Sunnism”, the term is also usually an approving denomination, though it is also used neutrally to single out those sects that employ other terms as their self-designations, such as {{m|ar||شِيعَة}}. Compare the meanings of {{m|en|catholic}} and {{m|en|orthodox}} in English, the latter of which is found in the names of several Christian denominations and churches.
* While {{m|ar||سُنَّة}} is used to signify both “normative teachings recorded in hadiths” and “recommended practices”, this does not mean that hadiths are never interpreted as commandments and hard obligations. In fact, this is very often the case. In the same way, the fact that {{m-self|ar|سُنَّة}} also refers to the “Sunni sect” does not mean that other sects with different names do not have “a Sunnah” (that is, a religious canon).

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|سُنَّة|pl=سُنَن}}

