Page 10956 سير: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{ar-rootbox|س ي ر}}

The “strap” sense is apparently a {{bor|ar|ira-mid}} borrowing, compare {{cog|fa-cls|سیرم|tr=sīram, sīrum|t=raw deer-skins cut into thongs, used in sewing saddles; strap; the shoemaker's strap which binds the last to his knee; a hawk's leash}}, {{m|fa-cls|سیرمان|tr=sīrmān|t=ruby; embroidered silk}}. Compare a whole set of horse tack terminology clearly to potentially borrowed from Iranian at the same time: {{m|ar|رَسَن}}, {{m|ar|رَخْت}}, {{m|ar|جُلّ}}, {{m|ar|جُوَالَق}}, {{m|ar|نَمَط}}, {{m|ar|بَرْذَعَة}}, {{m|ar|شِكَال}}, {{m|ar|لِجَام}}, {{m|ar|شَكِيمَة}}, {{m|ar|إِبْزِيم}}, {{m|ar|سَرْج}}, {{m|ar|خُرْج}} and {{m|ar|كُرْز}}.

The immediate source of the Arabic term, as also of {{cog|cop|ⲥⲓⲣ|t=line, stripe}} if distinct from {{m|cop|ⲥⲓⲣ|t=hair of an animal or woman}} inherited from {{noncog|egy|sr|<hiero>s-r:D3</hiero>|tr=sr|t=hair of an animal or woman}}, is likely the widely used {{bor|ar|arc|סירא}} / {{m|syc|ܣܝܪܐ|tr=sīrā|t=a woven thread or mesh; chain; cord, rope; thong, shoe-lace}}. This term is commonly explained as a borrowing from {{der|ar|grc|σειρᾱ́|t=cord, rope}}, although it could also be interpreted as clipped from {{m|arc|סֵירוּג|tr=sērūg}}, {{m|arc|סֵרוּג|tr=sērūg|t=plaiting, strapping}} or a similar term of the same root as the source of {{m|ar|سَرْج}}, which is itself however connected to Iranian. It also appears as the source of {{doublet|ar|صِير|notext=1|id1=fishpaste|t1=a kind of small fish used in brine for a spicy paste}}.

This could mean that all or most of the root {{ar-root|س ي ر}} related to guidance is derived from this borrowing, as transferred senses of straps by which horses are guided. ''Dictionnaire du faisceau dialectal arabe andalou'' suggests that even {{m|ar|صَارَ}} and the whole {{ar-root|ص ي ر}}, sometimes a variant of {{m|ar|سَارَ|t=to go, to travel}}, is from this noun by differentiation. {{cog|arc|-}} and {{cog|he|סָר|tr=sār|t=to turn aside, to turn back}} which reach as far back as {{cog|arc-imp|-}} and the {{w|Torah}}, with frequent occurrence, may indicate a Semitic basis however.

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|سَيْر|m|pl=سُيُور}}

# {{ar-verbal noun of|سَارَ|form=I}}
# {{C|ar|Horse tack}} [[strap]], [[strip]] of [[hide]], [[skin]] [[thong]] (such as, and originally, of a [[horse]]-[[harness]] or a [[shoulder-strap]], and modernly a [[belt]] in [[machinery]])
#* {{RQ:In Darfur|II|page=184|section=3.3.31|passage=فيعملون اللحم قديدًا وهو المسمّى عندهم بالشراميط لأنّهم يشرمطونه أي يقطعونه '''سيورًا''' ويأكلون منه طريًّا.|t=They make the meat into jerky, which they call “shreds” because they shred it, meaning they cut it into '''strips'''. Some of it they also eat undried.}}
# {{C|ar|Animal body parts}} [[antler]] of a [[stag]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|سَيْر|pl=سُيُور}}

