Page 11365 شفلح: -------- begin text --------
{{wikipedia|lang=ar|قبار|شَفَلَّح}}

===Etymology===
While the shape suggests a {{der|ar|xhu|-}} plant name, the ending is rather part of an {{bor|ar|arc|-}} root {{m|arc|פ־ל־ח}} / {{syc-root-entry|ܦ|ܠ|ܚ}} (as in {{m|ar|فَلَّاح}}) or {{m|arc|פ־ר־ח}} / {{syc-root-entry|ܦ|ܪ|ܚ}}, which have been used for plant capsules bursting open such as the developing caperberry does; {{cog|arc|פׅרְחָא|tr=pirḥā}} / {{m|syc|ܦܲܪܚܵܐ|tr=parḥā}} is specifically attested as meaning caper. Compare parallel developments in {{m|ar|لَصَف|t=caper}}; and indeed {{m|ar|عِتْر|t=caper; {{taxlink|Glossonema varians|species}}}} is from a root used for slit offerings. The leading syllable in the present Arabic word is the {{der|ar|sem-pro|-}} causative prefix {{m|sem-pro|*ša-}}.

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|شَفَلَّح|m}}

# [[caper]] {{gloss|{{taxfmt|Capparis spinosa|species}} and its produce}}
#: {{syn|ar|كَبَر|id1=caper|عِتْر|لَصَف}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|شَفَلَّح}}

===References===
* {{R:arc:Löw-Flora|volume=1|pages=322–331}}
* {{R:arc:Löw-Pflanzen|pages=262–265, especially 264}}
* {{R:ar:Mandaville|B|page=277}}

{{C|ar|Brassicales order plants|Spices and herbs}}
-------- end text --------
