Page 11676 صابورة: -------- begin text --------
{{wp|lang=ar|صابورة}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|صَابُور|سَابُور||al-Andalus}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|arc|-}}, only attested in {{cog|sam|ࠑࠁࠓࠄ|t=ballast}}, but the original Aramaic pattern derives with [https://cal.huc.edu/oneentry.php?lemma=cbr+V&cits=all meanings] of “heaping up” otherwise attested for the root while a cognate Arabic root {{ar-root|ض ب ر|nocat=1}} also exists with formations such as {{m|ar|ضَبَرَ|t=to pile up; to bind firmly}}, {{m|ar|إِضْبَارَة}}, {{m|ar|ضِبَارَة|t=bundle}}, though {{cog|roa|-}} philologists offer supposed descendants of {{cog|la|saburra|t=sand}} meaning “ballast”.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|صَابُورَة}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|صَابُورَة|f|pl=صَوَابِير|pl2=صَابُورَات}}

# [[ballast]]
#* {{R:ar:FAH|6II|passage=ويقولون سَابُورُ المركب بالسّين. والصّواب صَابُورٌ بالصّاد لأنّه صُبِرَ به أي حُبِسَ. ومنه صُبْرَةُ الطّعام. فأمّا سَابُورُ اسم الرّجل فبالسّين ولا يعرف له اشتقاق لأنّه أعجميّ.|t=They say ''sābūr'', right is ''ṣābūr'', seen in ''ṣubira'' meaning ''ḥubisa'' ‘being tied’. No native derivation for this masculine noun with ''s'' is known since it is foreign.}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|صَابُورَة|pl=صَوَابِير|pl2=صَابُورَات}}

{{C|ar|Ship parts}}
