Page 12844 عامة: -------- begin text --------
{{ar-rootbox|ع م م}}

===Etymology===
Derived from the feminine form of {{ar-active participle|noderived=1}} of the verb {{m|ar|عَمَّ}}. See {{cog|he|עם|tr=ʕam|t=people, nation}} for comparison.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|عَامَّة}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|عَامَّة|f|pl=عَوَامّ}}

# {{lb|ar|uncountable|construct}} the [[major]] or most [[general]] [[part]] (of), the [[majority]] or [[generality]] (of); [[most]] (of), [[most]]
#: {{syn|ar|أَكْثَر|جُلّ|أَغْلَب|مُعْظَم|t1=the majority (of)|t2=most of|t3=the more overwhelming part (of)|t4=the greater part (of)}}
#: {{usex|ar|تِلْكَ إِرَادَةُ عَامَّةِ ٱلشَّعْبِ|This is the will of the majority of the people!}}
#: {{usex|ar|عَامَّةُ ٱلنَّاسِ يُرِيدُونَ هٰذَا|Most people want that.}}
## {{lb|ar|collective|definite|_|or construct|derogatory}} the [[people]] who have not [[go through|gone through]] [[moral]], [[theological]], and/or [[philosophical]] [[instruction]] and [[train]]ing or who are [[oblivious]] to [[mysterious]], [[esoteric]], or [[occult]] [[knowledge]]; the [[laity]]; the [[common]] [[unlearned]] [[people]], the [[populace]], the [[hoi polloi]], the [[multitude]]
##: {{ant|ar|خَاصَّة|فَلَاسِفَة|عُلَمَاء|مُثَقَّفُون|t1=the few; the clergy; the elite|t2=the philosophers|t3=the theologians; the clergy; the sages|t4=the educated and cultured; the intellegentsia}}
##: {{uxi|ar|دَعْكَ مِنَ اَلْعَامَّةِ|Ignore the many.}}
##: {{ux|ar|رَاَيْتُ عَامَّةَ ٱلنَّاسِ وَخَوَاصَّهُمْ|I saw the commoners and the intellectuals}}
##: {{coi|ar|عَوَامّ الشَّعْبِ|the [unlearned] laymen of the people}}
## {{lb|ar|collective|definite|_|or construct|derogatory}} the [[people]] or [[sects]] [[think|thought]] to be like the [[laity]] in their [[simple-minded]]ness, [[ignorance]], or [[numerousness]]; [[simpletons]]
##: {{usex|ar|اَلْعَامَّة مِنَ الْمُتَفَلْسِفَة|the simpletons among the would-be philosophers}}
# {{lb|ar|countable|rare}} a [[raft]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|عَامَّة|pl=عَوَامّ}}

====Derived terms====
{{col4|ar|عَامِّيّ}}

===Adverb===
{{ar-adv|عَامَّةً}}

# [[general]]ly
#: {{syn|ar|عُمُومًا}}
#: {{ant|ar|خَاصَّةً|t=especially}}
# [[altogether]], [[entirely]]
#: {{syn|ar|جَمِيعًا|t=together; all}}

====Usage notes====
In its numerating sense, the word simply denotes "the general, widespread majority" (as opposed to those who are simply numerically fewer) or, more specifically, "the public". However, the pejorative uses are so predominant that it is sometimes hard to distinguish whether the reference to the higher numbers implies disparagement or not. Blunter demeaning often pivots on ''lack of knowledge'', be it moral, religious, and theological, philosophical, or secular (that is, non-religious), hence their being visualized as a class of multitudes that is respectively contrasted with the {{m|ar|خاصة|اَلْخَاصَة|t=the theologically and/or philosophically erudite; the occultists}}; with {{m|ar|فلاسفة|اَلْفَلَاسِفَة|t=the philosophers}}; and, more recently, with {{m|ar|مثقفون|اَلْمُثَقَّفُون|t=the intelligentsia}}. By analogy, the word is very commonly found in sectarian polemics, where it centralizes the purported affectation of knowledge of the ideological rivals.

===Adjective===
{{ar-adj-fem|عَامَّة}}

# {{feminine singular of|ar|عَامّ}}

====References====
* {{R:ar:Lane}}
* {{R:ar:Steingass}}

{{cln|ar|adverbial accusatives|collective nouns}}
