{{wikipedia|lang=ar}}
{{ar-coll-noun|عَرَب|f|g2=m-p|sing=عَرَبِيّ|singg=m|pl=عُرُوب|pl2=أَعْرُب|pl3=عُرْبَان|pl4=عُرُب|pauc=عَرَب}}

# [[Arab]]s, true [[Arab]]s

=====Usage notes=====
According to Lane's ''Arabic Lexicon'' (entry {{lang|ar|عرب}}, book I, p. 2045), {{m|ar|عَرَب}} is of the feminine gender. However, since the word is a collective noun with human referents, it is possible for it to be treated as either a masculine plural or a feminine singular noun. For example, the {{w|عرب|Wikipedia entry|lang=ar}} has {{m|ar||الْعَرَب الْبَاقِيَة الَّذِين|tr=al-ʕarab al-bāqiya allaḏīn ...|gloss=the remaining Arabs who ...}} with {{m|ar|بَاقِيَة||remaining}}, a feminine singular, but {{m|ar|الَّذِين||[[who]]}}, a masculine plural. Semantically, such nouns often refer to races and ethnic groups. For similar collective nouns, see {{m|ar|عَجَم|t=Non-Arabs}}, {{m|ar|كُرْج|t=Georgians}}, {{m|ar|فُرْس|t=Persians}}, {{m|ar|تُرْك|t=Turks}}, {{m|ar|كُرْد|t=Kurds}}, {{m|ar|رُوم|t=Romans}}, and {{m|ar|رُوس|t=Rus Vikings}}.

