Page 13472 عود: -------- begin text --------
{{wikipedia|lang=ar}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ar-rootbox|ع و د}}From {{m|ar|عَادَ|t=to return, to turn back}} in the sense of a branch or other slender piece of wood being flexible.

The sense “[[oud]], [[lute]]” can be understood as a {{sl|ar|pal||tr=lwt'|ts=rōd|t=string; stringed instrument; [[barbat]]|notext=1}} (> {{cog|fa|رود|tr=rud}}), compare like liquid {{rebracketing|ar|nocap=1}} in {{m|ar|عَسْكَر}} and {{m|ar|أَنْجَر}}.

The instrument sense of the word then stands as an Arabic version of the Persian {{m|fa|بربط|tr=barbat}}, that featured a smaller more curved neck with greater tension, as well as a larger rounded belly created from steam-bent strips of wood. The [[w:Barbat_(lute)|Barbat]] was carved from one solid piece of wood, the larger size of the oud's belly was something not previously possible before the further development of using hot moisture to aid in bending thin strips. This characteristic technique became the traditional source of the instruments name;<ref>{{R:Iranica|author=Jean During|date=1988-12-15|url=http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/barbat|title=Barbaṭ}}</ref> for more see [[w:Oud|Oud]] and [[w:Barbat_(lute)|Barbat]].

However there is a cognate instrument {{cog|uga|𐎓𐎄|t=lute, an instrument}} 1000's of years prior to this,<ref>Matahisa Koitabashi: [https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jorient1962/36/2/36_2_1/_article ''Musical Instruments in the Ugaritic Texts.''] In: Bulletin of the Society for Near Eastern Studies in Japan Vol. 39 (1996) No. 2 P 16-32</ref> identified as an early {{l|en|chordophone}} with connection to the {{der|ar|sux|[[𒄑]][[𒅗𒌣]]|tr=<sup>GEŠ</sup>gu<sub>3</sub>.de<sub>2</sub>|t=instruments in general, a lute|lit=wood that has voice}}<ref name="PSD">{{R:sux:ePSD|e=1865|entry=gude}}; note: the ĝeš prefix is a determinative for wooden objects.</ref><ref>"194a" in Dominique Collon: ''Ancient Near Eastern Art.'' University of California Press: Berkeley, 1995 in conjunction with The Trustees of the British Museum, page 225.</ref> This precursor to ouds and guitars was constructed of strings laid on a wood-staff that ran through the whole body of the instrument attached to a simple drum-like resonating body; as strings of lyres and drums were used prior, the distinguishing feature was this wooden rod.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|عُود}}
* {{audio|ar|Ar-عود.ogg}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|عُود|m|pl=أَعْوَاد|pl2=عِيدَان}}

# [[wood]], [[timber]]
# [[stick]], [[rod]], [[pole]]
# [[branch]], [[twig]]
# [[stem]], [[stalk]]
# [[cane]], [[reed]]
# {{C|ar|Gums and resins|Malvales order plants}} [[aloe]], [[agarwood]], any [[plant]] with high [[moisture]] [[content]]
#* {{RQ:Qazwiniy:1849|1|page=260|passage=عود شجرة تنبت في جزائر بحر الهند عروقها تقلع وتدفن في الأرض حتى تتعفن  منها الحشية فيبقى العود الخالص قال شيخ الرئيس مضعة يطيب النكهة وينفع الدماغ جدَّا ويقوّي الحواسّ والقلب ويفرحه وتدخينه بالسكر طيب جدَّا والسكر يقوّي رائحته وشراب العود طارد للرياح المؤلمة.|t='''Agalloch''' is a tree that grows in islands of the Indian sea and its roots are extracted and buried in the earth until they rot, of it one makes a stuffing and it stays pure, Šayḵ ar-Raʾīs says that a bit of it already is of nice smell and assists the brain in a serious extent and strengthens the senses and the heart and rejoices it and when smoked with sugar it is also very nice, sugar strengthens its smell and the syrup of '''agalloch''' expectorates doloriferous winds.<ref>Commented by {{R:ar:Jacob-Geographen|volume=4|pages=159–160}}, who identifies this use with ''[[Aquilaria malaccensis|Aquilaria agallocha]]''.</ref>}}
# [[thin]] [[strip]] of [[wood]], [[veneer]]
# {{lb|ar|musical instruments}} [[oud]], [[lute]]
# [[body]], [[build]], [[physique]]
# [[strength]], specifically [[tensile strength]], [[force]], [[intensity]]

