Page 13624 غجر: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{unk|ar}}, in Modern Standard Arabic use from {{bor|ar|arz|-}} extended from the name one of the tribes of {{w|Doms in Egypt}}. Attested in {{w|Al-Jahiz|al-Jāḥiẓ}} (ᛦ 868) as the occupation of a {{w|Basra|Baṣran}}, in the form {{m|ar|كَجَار}} or {{m|ar|كَچَار}} (without [[إعجام|ʔiʕjām]]): possibly clipped from {{der|ar|pra-sau|𑀪𑀺𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀸𑀬𑀭}} from {{der|ar|sa|भिक्षाचर}} which gave {{cog|inc-hnd}} {{m|ur|بھکھاری}} / {{m|hi|भिखारी}}, all meaning “beggar”, and originally a designation for mendicants of mixed ethnic affiliation.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|غَجَر}}
* {{IPA|arz|/ɣa.ɡar/|a=Egyptian}}

===Noun===
{{ar-coll-noun|غَجَر|m|sing=غَجَرِيّ|singg=m}}

# [[Ghagar]], one of the tribes of {{w|Doms in Egypt}}
# [[Gypsies]] ([[Romani]])
# a [[rude]] or [[abusive]] person

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-coll-noun|غَجَر|sing=غَجَرِيّ}}

===References===
* {{R:ar:Richardson:2017|page=117}}
-------- end text --------
