Page 14053 فتى: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{der|ar|sem-wes-pro|*pataw-|t=to seduce, to beguile}}, the young not only so called because they are easily persuaded, as illustrated by {{cog|sem|-}}-borrowed {{cog|xcl|տղայ}} and {{m|xcl|երախայ}}, which refer to the child and the simpleton at the same time, but in particular referring to the sexual prurience of adult-state humans, as that verb primarily bore a literal as well as a saucy meaning “to penetrate, to jook”—hence {{cog|tig|ፈታ}} and {{cog|gez|ፈተወ}}, {{cog|ti|ፈተወ}} mean “to love”, as in being attracted by the sex.

For similar semantic direction and range, see also {{ar-root|م|ن|ي|nocat=1}} / {{ar-root|م|ن|و|nocat=1}} meaning in the base stem “to tempt; to afflict, to make suffer”, {{ar-form|II|nocap=1}} “to rouse desire or hope in”, {{ar-form|V|nocap=1}} “to desire”, well known, and finally specifically in {{noncog|akk|menûm|𒈨𒉡𒌝|tr=menûm|t=to love}}, but also in {{noncog|ar|مَنِيّ|t=what is discharged in ejaculation}}. A parallel case of a particular age of man having been named in accordance with such reflections is {{m|ar|غُلَام|t=boy}} from {{m|ar|غَلِمَ|t=to be in rut}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|فَتًى}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|فَتًى|m|d=فَتَيَان|d2=فَتَوَان|pl=فِتْيَان|pl2=فِتْيَة|pl3=فُتُوّ|pl4=فُتِيّ|f=فَتَاة}}

# [[male]] [[adolescent]], [[youth]], [[juvenile]]
# [[male]] [[servant]]
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|18|62
|passage=فَلَمَّا جَاوَزَا قَالَ '''لِفَتَاهُ''' آتِنَا غَدَاءَنَا لَقَدْ لَقِينَا مِن سَفَرِنَا هٰذَا نَصَبًا
|translation=So when they had passed beyond it, he said to '''his boy''', "Bring us our morning meal. We have certainly suffered in this, our journey, much fatigue."
|subst=فَلَمَّا/فَ-لَمَّا,لِفَتَاهُ/لِ-فَتَاهُ}}

====Declension====
