{{ar-decl-noun|فَرْفَخِين}}
-------- end text --------
Page 14161 فرفير: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Disputed etymology:
* Deriving from {{der|ar|grc|πορφύρα|t=murex; Tyrian purple color and dye}} which is expected to ultimately have a Semitic root likely from {{der|ar|phn|-}}. The medical sense derives from {{sl|ar|en|purpura|notext=1}}, from Latin, from the Greek.
* Natively from the root {{ar-root|ف|ر|ف|ر}} or {{ar-root|ف|و|ر}} meaning “to shake or shake about”, “to agitate”, “to mangle or molest”, “to break or crush”, theoretically a cognate with the {{cog|phn|-}} term that {{m|grc|πορφύρα}} derives from.
**All in reference to the process in which the [[w:Tyrian purple|Tyrian dye]] is extracted from the [[w:Murex|Murex]]; the more time-consuming poking or shaking the snail into agitation or the faster, but depleting, method of crushing the shell.
**Deriving from either root in the sense of "becoming agitated", as the changing of color and complexion is used idiomatically to denote changes in states or moods in Semitic Languages.

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|فِرْفِير|m}}

# [[violet]] [[color]]
# [[dark]] [[purple]]
# {{lb|ar|medical}} [[purpura]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|فِرْفِير}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ar|فِرْفِيرِيّ|t=violet-colored}}

