-------- end text --------
Page 14174 فرملة: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
{{unknown|ar}}. It is a word only from the late 19<sup>th</sup> century (then also with a variant {{alter|ar|فَرْمِيلَة}}), originating and still spread in the dialects of Egypt, the Sudan and Chad. The east uses {{m|ar|بْرَِيك|tr=brēk}}, the lands west and {{w|Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon|Mandate Syria}} the {{noncog|fr|frein}}, so it is perhaps from its {{bor|ar|ota|-}} derivative {{m|tr|frenlemek|t=to brake}}, the verb coming first, but {{bor|ar|it|fermare}} fits too.

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|فَرْمَلَة|f|pl=فَرَامِل|pl2=فَرْمَلَات}}

# {{ar-verbal noun of|فَرْمَلَ|form=Iq}}
# [[brake]]
#: {{syn|ar|كَابِحَة|مِكْبَح|ضَابِطَة}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|فَرْمَلَة|pl=فَرَامِل|pl2=فَرْمَلَات}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|ar|فَرْمَل|t=to brake}}
* {{l|ar|فَرْمَلْجِيّ|t=brakesman}}

====References====
* {{R:ar:BehnstedtWoidich|WA2|page=445}}

===Etymology 2===
Introduced in the 18<sup>th</sup> century after Turkish cuts, dissimilated from {{bor|ar|ota|فرمنه|tr=fermene|t=a kind of ornamented jacket worn in front of the waist}}, from the term surfacing in Arabic as {{m|ar|فَرْمَان|t=a written decree}}, understood as lending dignity carried in front for passthrough.

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|فَرْمَلَة|f|pl=فَرَامِل}}

# {{tlb|ar|Libya|Tunisia}} a [[kind]] of [[traditional]] [[vest]] with [[gold]] [[braid]] [[ornament]]s resembling a {{m|ar|صَدْرِيَّة}}, [[kirtle]], [[gilet]], [[plastron]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|فَرْمَلَة|pl=فَرَامِل}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|ar|فُرَيْمِلَة|pos=diminutive}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|bid|parmalà|bor=1}}
* {{desc|ha|farmàlàn|bor=1}}
* {{desc|kr|fármala|bor=1}}
* → Kotoko: {{l|und|fármàlà}}<!-- ex Sergio Baldi: Dictionnaire des emprunts arabes dans les langues de l'Afrique de l'Ouest (2008) -->

====References====
* {{R:sem:Růžička:1909|pages=105–106}}
* {{R:ar:Gifts worthy of kings|page=69, footnote 42|instead=1}}
* {{R:ar:Stuhlmann:1912|page=168b}}

