Page 14366 فلك: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
Belonging to the root {{ar-root|ف ل ك}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|فَلَك}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|فَلَك|m|pl=أَفْلَاك}}

# [[orbit]], [[rounded]] [[course]]
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|21|33|passage=وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِي خَلَقَ ٱللَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ وَٱلشَّمْسَ وَٱلْقَمَرَ كُلٌّ فِي '''فَلَكٍ''' يَسْبَحُونَ|t=And it is He who created the night and the day and the sun and the moon; all [heavenly bodies] in an '''orbit''' are swimming.}}
# [[sphere]]
# [[sky]], [[outer space]], [[universe]], (figurative) [[heaven]]
# [[orb]]
# {{ellipsis of|ar|عِلْم الْفَلَك|t=[[astronomy]]}}
# {{C|ar|Mythological creatures}} [[falak]], [[ouroboros]]

=====Usage notes=====
This word is the ancient version of [[كون]] (which has been used in Arabic since the sixth century, unlike فلك which has been used since ever), it means roughly [[Heaven]], but unlike English, the it isn’t limited to positive and divine worlds, as it can also describe the dwellings of demons and spirits. See also [[ذر]] (a spiritual universe in Islamic cosmogony, named as such because the offspring of Adam had come out his back as if they were small ants).

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|فَلَك|pl=أَفْلَاك}}

