Page 14652 قارب: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
{{ar-rootbox|ق ر ب}}
{{ar-active participle}} of {{m|ar|قَرِبَ|t=to approach, to come near or be near, to be connecting or adjacent}}. Originally referring to a small boat used by a seaman as a convenient means to approach shore rather than bringing a larger vessel to port.

Alternatively a loan from {{bor|ar|la|carabus}} or {{bor|ar|grc|κάραβος}} – also found as {{bor|ar|syc|ܩܪܒܝܢ}} – with an uncertain etymology related to beetles and crabs, perhaps originally Semitic; for more see [[caravel]], {{m|ar|عَقْرَب}} and its cognates.<ref>{{cite-journal|first=André W.|last=Sleeswyk|year=1998|url=http://www.persee.fr/web/revues/home/prescript/article/nauti_0154-1854_1998_act_14_1_1208|title=Carvel-planking and Carvel Ships in the North οf Europe|work=Archaeonautica|volume=14|issue=1|pages=223–228}}; {{R:ar:Vollers-Beitr.|part=III|page=300}}; {{R:ar:Fraenkel|page=218}}; {{R:ar:Dozy|volume=2|page=323}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|قَارِب}}
