Page 14657 قارورة: -------- begin text --------
{{wp|lang=ar}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|قَازُوزَة|قَاقُوزَة|قَاقُزَة|قُزَازَة||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
It could be believed from the root {{ar-root|ق ر ر}} from the idea of the glass “standing still” or “chilling” after being shaped. However, apart from the circumstance that the material of a bottle, in general, in the ancient Near East, relatively rarely was glass as opposed to pottery or animal hide, this measure {{m|ar||فَاعُولَة}} does not follow regular derivation rules of the Arabic language, in its state during [[Antiquity]], and points instead to a loan from an [[Appendix:Aramaic terms only attested in borrowings|unattested Aramaic]] word, possibly paralleling {{m|ar|جَرَّة|t=jar, amphora}}: for this observation take into consideration the otherwise unrecorded [[Nabataean]] ''gərōrā'' noted by al-Ḵwarizmī for the scorpion-name {{m|ar|جَرَّار}}, {{m|ar|جَرَّارَة}} (quoted in the linked place) and the particular meanings of “drawing” and “flowing away” ''applied to liquids'' documented for the Judeo-Aramaic [https://cal.huc.edu/oneentry.php?lemma=grr+V&cits=all base verb] {{m|arc|גְּרַר|tr=gərar|t=to drag, to pull}} akin to the Arabic {{m|ar|جَرَّ}}.

The {{cog|jpa|-}} pot-name {{m|jpa|קררה}}, {{m|jpa|קרירה}}, although primarily used to boil liquids, is probably too remote in form.

Alternative forms, in so far as not {{glossary|tashif|mispointed}}, are contaminated from the source of {{m|ar|زُجَاجَة|t=glass bottle}} in any case, which has the form {{m|ar|قُِزَاز|tr=guzāz, gizāz}} in many dialects, and long already fulfils the function of a word for a “glass vessel”; though influence from the source of {{m|ar|كُوز|t=tankard}} also be imaginable.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|قَارُورَة}}
* {{audio|ar|Ar-قارورة.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|قَارُورَة|f|pl=قَوَارِير}}

# [[flask]], [[vial]]; [[bottle]]

