Page 14741 قبعة: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|syc|ܩܘܒܥܐ|tr=qūḇᵊʿā|t=hood}}, from Western {{der|ar|arc|קובעא|tr=kōḇᵊʿā}}, cognate with {{cog|he|כּוֹבַע|tr=kóḇaʿ}}, {{m|he|קּוֹבַע|tr=kóḇaʿ|t=helmet; turban}}, while {{cog|gez|ቆብዕ|t=hood, cap}} is also from Aramaic. As first proposed by {{w|Theodor Gaster}}, the source is {{der|ar|hit||tr=kupaḫi-|t=headgear}} which is cognate with {{cog|grc|κεφαλή}}, {{cog|la|caput}}, {{cog|gem-pro|*haubudą}} etc. For the semantic rationale, the helmet as a headgear has spread from Asia Minor, thus the borrowing, but then the word was in Western Aramaic vulgarized to mean other kinds of headwear still relatable to helmets in shape, as turbans in Hebrew, whereas now Arabic has deviated from the original meaning completely by using the word for the Western-style {{m|en|hat}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|قُبَّعَة}}
* {{audio|ar|Ar-قبعة.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|قُبَّعَة|f|pl=قُبَّعَات}}

# [[cap]], [[hood]], [[cowl]]
# [[hat]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|قُبَّعَة|pl=sfp}}

====References====
* {{R:ar:Fraenkel|page=54}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=3|page=392}}
* {{cite-journal|last=Gaster|first=Theodor|authorlink=Theodor Gaster|title=A Hittite Word in Hebrew|journal=The Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society of Great Britain and Ireland|doi=10.2307/25194871|issue=4|year=1933|page=909}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=2|page=664}}
* {{R:Nöldeke:Neue Beitr.|page=37}}
* {{R:Payne Smith 1903|page=492}}
* {{R:he:Rabin-Hit.|pages=124–125}}
* {{cite-journal|last=Sapir|first=Edward|authorlink=Edward Sapir|title=Hebrew "Helmet," a Loanword, and Its Bearing on Indo-European Phonology|journal=Journal of the American Oriental Society|doi=10.2307/594782|issue=1|volume=57|year=1937|pages=73–77}}
* {{R:ar:Steingass|page=817}}
* {{R:ar:Wehr-5-de|page=997}}

{{C|ar|Headwear}}
-------- end text --------
Page 14742 قبقاب: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Objectes de la Sala Secà i Muntanya (27094121802).jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|قَبْقَاب}}|upright=1.25]]
{{swp|lang=ar}}

===Etymology===
From the root {{ar-root|ق|ب|ب}}, meaning “the {{l|en|side}} of an {{l|en|animal}}”, “the {{l|en|belly}} or {{l|en|abdomen}}” suggested by [[w:Edward_William_Lane|Lane]] to ultimately be {{onomatopoeic|ar|nocap=1}} in origin from the sound of a {{l|en|rumbling}} {{l|en|stomach}}. It is in this location where a {{l|en|saddle}} {{l|en|rests}}, hence derives the sense of a {{l|en|wood}} suitable for {{l|en|carving}} and {{l|en|shaping}}. Related to {{m|ar|قَيْقَب}} and {{ar-root|ق|و|ب|nocat=1}}, meaning “to {{l|en|carve}}, {{l|en|peel}}, or {{l|en|hollow out}} wood”. Compare {{cog|gez|ቆጶን|tr=ḳop̣p̣on}} – a Cushitic borrowing –, {{cog|om||kopē, kopʿē}}, {{cog|har|ኩብካብ|tr=kubkab}}, {{cog|tig|ቅረቃብ}}, {{w|Gurage languages|Gurage}} {{m|sgw|ቈቤ|tr=ḳʷäbe}}, {{cog|bej||kobā}}. {{w|Reinhart Dozy|Dozy}} suggests an ultimately {{bor|ar|sem-srb|-}} origin, a regional {{l|en|wanderword|wanderwort}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|قَبْقَاب|قُبْقَاب}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|قَبْقَاب|m|head2=قُبْقَاب|pl=قَبَاقِيب}}

# [[patten]], [[clog]], [[chopine]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|قَبْقَاب|m|head2=قُبْقَاب|pl=قَبَاقِيب}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ar|قَبَاقِيبِيّ|t=he who crafts such shoes; a family name}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|xaa|قبقاب|tr=qabqāb|قوقب|tr2=qawqab|قرقب|tr3=qirqab}}
* {{desc|ary|قرقابة|tr=qarqāba}}
* {{desc|ka|bor=1|ყაყაბი|t=a kind of footwear (for women)|lb=Imereti|qq=obsolete}}
** {{desc|xmf|bor=1|ყაყაბი|ყაყაფი|t=a kind of high-heeled footwear|qq=obsolete}}
*** {{desc|ab|bor=1|аҟаԥҟаԥ|alt1=а-ҟаԥҟаԥ|аҟаҟаԥ|alt2=а-ҟаҟаԥ}}
* {{desc|ota|قبقاب|tr=kabkab|bor=1}}
** {{desc|tr|kabkab|kapkap}}
