===Etymology 2===
{{clipping|ar|قِسِّيس}}, from {{der|ar|syc|ܩܫܝܫܐ|t=priest|tr=qašīšā}}.

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|قَسّ|head2=قِسّ|m|pl=قُسُوس|pl2=قُسُس}}

# {{lb|ar|Christianity}} [[clergyman]]; [[priest]]; [[pastor]], [[minister]]
#: {{ux|ar|دَرَجَاتُ الْقِسِّيسِيَّةِ عِنْدَ الْمَلْكَانِيَّةِ وَالْيَعَاقِبَةِ ثَلَاثَةٌ: أُسْقُفٌ، وَ'''قِسٌّ'''، وَشَمَّاسٌ.|t=The orders of the priesthood in the Eastern and Oriental Churches are three: bishop, '''priest''', and deacon.}}
#: {{ux|ar|شَاهَدْتُ خُطْبَةً لِ'''قِسٍّ''' أَمْرِيكِيٍّ.|t=I watched a sermon by an American '''pastor'''.}}

===Usage notes===
The word is a synonym of {{m|ar|كَاهِن|t=priest}} in Arabic {{w|Episcopal polity|episcopal}} Christianity. In {{w|Congregationalist polity|congregationalist}} Christianity, {{m|ar|قِسّ}} corresponds to several Protestant titles, like "pastor" or "minister," while {{m|ar|كَاهِن|t=priest}} is not used, since {{w|Priesthood of all believers|these denominations have no priesthood}}.

