Page 14963 قصب: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
{{ar-rootbox|ق ص ب}}
{{back-formation|ar|قَصَّاب|t={{l|en|butcher}}}} borrowed from Aramaic {{m|arc|קצבא}} / {{m|syc|ܩܰܨܳܒܳܐ|tr=qaṣṣābā}}, therewith forming a root {{ar-root|ق|ص|ب}}. Doublet of indigenous Arabic {{doublet|ar|قَضَبَ|t1=to cut off, to [[trim]]|notext=1}}.

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|I/a~i.pass.vn:قَصْب}}

# to [[cut up]] (a slaughtered animal)

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|I/a~i.pass.vn:قَصْب}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|قَصْب|m}}

# {{ar-verbal noun of|قَصَبَ|form=I}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|قَصْب}}

===Etymology 2===
{{ar-rootbox|ق ص ب}}
[[File:Harvested sugar cane stalks, Alexandria, LA IMG 4237.JPG|thumbnail|right|{{l|ar|سكر|السُّكَّر|tr=-}} {{lang|ar|قَصَب}}]]
From the root {{ar-root|ق|ص|ب}} of the aforementioned verb meaning “to be segment”, “to be cut into sections”, “to be severed”; stemming from a reed's divisions or from being collected as cut pieces; compare {{m|ar|قَضْب}}.

====Noun====
{{ar-coll-noun|قَصَب|m|sing=قَصَبَة}}

# [[stalk]]
# [[reed]], [[cane]] {{qualifier|especially [[sugarcane]]}}
# [[thread]], [[filament]]
#* {{RQ:Rashid-Dhakhayir|page=61 line 3 a fine–62 line 2|passage=وفلسقيتان أُخريان من بِلّوْر مربّط ففضة، مذهّبة الناحية الواحدة، مشبكة بجواهر، وفي وسطها درات، وفي الناحية الأخُرَى أَربع '''قصبات''' فضة مطلية بذهب، وكرنيب وكوز فضة مطليّان بذهب مجرى فيهما الجوهر.|t=And two other flasks are of crystals twined with silver, gildened in one corner, netted with jewels, and in their mids pearls, and in the other corner four '''threads''' of silver thatched with gold, and a bowl and a mug of silver layered both with gold and woven with gemstones.}}
# [[pipe]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-coll-noun|قَصَب}}

