===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|arc|קְסְטִירָא|tr=qəsṭīrā}}, {{m|arc|קַסִּיטְרָא|tr=qassīṭrā|t=tin}}, from {{der|ar|grc|κασσίτερον}}, {{m|grc|κασσίτερος|t=tin|lit=the metal or earth from the land of the Kassi}}; ultimately of obscure origin, considered of pre-Greek substrate. Identification and the meaning of {{m|grc|κασσί}} has been disputed; see {{w|Kassites}}, a historically significant bronze age people from the {{w|Zagros Mountains}}, the nearest source of Mesopotamian tin – which could relate us to {{doublet|ar|مَرْقَشِيتَا|notext=1|t1=marcasite}} –, and the {{w|Cassiterides}} and {{w|Cassiterite|cassiterite}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|قَصْدِير}}
* {{audio|ar|Ar-قصدير.ogg}}
** {{IPA|acw|/ɡasˤ.diːr/|a=Hijazi}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|قَصْدِير|m}}

# [[tin]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|قَصْدِير}}

===References===
* {{R:CAL|qsyTr|N}}
* {{R:ar:Almaany}}
* {{R:ar:Fraenkel|page=153}}
* {{R:ar:Vollers-Beitr.|part=III|page=301}}

{{C|ar|Chemical elements|Metals}}
{{cln|ar|terms derived from toponyms}}

