Page 15055 قفيز: -------- begin text --------
{{swp|lang=ar}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|arc|קְפִיזָא}} / {{m|syc|ܩܦܝܙܐ|tr=qəp̄īzā}}. It is a dry measure the oldest attestation of which is {{der|ar|egx-dem|kpḏ|t=a standard measure of 100 ml, applicable to dry and liquid content}}, later {{cog|cop|ϭⲁⲡⲉⲓϫⲉ}}, and it is to be identified with {{cog|pal||tr=kpyc|sc=Phlv|ts=kabīz}}, {{cog|fa-cls|کویز|tr=kawīz}}, {{m|fa|کویژ|tr=kawîž}}, {{cog|ckb|کەویژ}}, {{m|ckb|کێوِژ}}, {{cog|grc|καπίθη}} (in {{w|Anabasis (Xenophon)|Xenophon’s ''Anabasis''}}), dialectal Modern {{cog|el|καπίθιον}}, {{cog|xpr||tr=kpyš}}, {{cog|sog|tr=kpc}}, {{cog|xbc|καβσο}}, {{m|xbc|καβιζο}}, {{cog|xco||tr=kpcyk}}, {{cog|xcl|կապիճ}}, {{w|Mush dialect|Mush}} {{cog|hy|կապեճ}}, {{w|Homshetsi dialect|Hamshen}} {{cog|hy|կապճակ}}, {{m|hy|կապիծ}}, {{cog|oge|კაპიჭი}}, {{m|oge|კაბიწი}}, {{cog|lzz|კაპიწი}}, {{cog|os|къабиц}}, {{m|os|къӕбиц}}, {{cog|myz|tr=qabīṣā}}, {{cog|tr|gebiç}}, {{m|tr|kapis}}, {{m|tr|kebiç}}, {{m|tr|kepiç}}, {{m|tr|kepinç}}, {{m|tr|kepis}}, {{m|tr|gabiz}}, {{m|tr|kepiz}}.

For speculated origin, compare {{noncog|akk|𒆏𒁍|ts=kappu|t=originally meaning “hand” from {{noncog|sem-pro|*kapp-}}, cupped hands; a bowl, a small serving container}}, possibly with a later {{noncog|ira|-}} language suffix or from a yet to be attested addition of {{m|akk|𒅖|ts=-iš|t=to be -like}}, rendering a literal meaning “to be hand-like, to be like cupped hands”. Alternatively related to {{noncog|xur|𒅗𒉿|tr=ka-pi|ts=kapi|t=a grain measure}}, both it and the {{noncog|akk|-}} being of no little frequency, or even {{noncog|ine-pro|*keh₂p-|t={{l|de|fassen}}}} or {{m|ine-pro|*(s)kep-||to work with a sharp instrument}}.

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|قَفِيز|m|pl=أَقْفِزَة|pl2=قُفْزَان}}

# {{tlb|ar|obsolete}} a [[dry measure]]
## {{lb|ar|medieval Iraq}}
### {{lb|ar|{{w|Kufa|al-Kūfa}}, {{w|Baghdad|Bağdād}}}} about 60 litres
### {{lb|ar|{{w|Basra|al-Baṣra}}, {{w|Wasit, Iraq|Wāṣiṭ}}}} about 30 litres
## {{lb|ar|medieval Palestine}} about 152 litres
## {{lb|ar|medieval Tunisia}} varying between about 170–200 litres
## {{lb|ar|medieval Cordoba}} 44.16 litres
## {{lb|ar|Morocco|_|until 1923|Algeria until 1843|Tunisia until 1895}} 528.4 [[litre]]s

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|قَفِيز|pl=أَقْفِزَة|pl2=قُفْزَان}}

