{{ar-conj|II}}

===Etymology 3===
Borrowed from {{bor|ar|syc|ܩܰܠܶܣ|tr=qalles|t=to praise}}, in Jewish {{cog|arc|קַלֵּס}}, according to Rabin ultimately a bifurcated cultic word that is reflected in the opposite sense as {{cog|uga|𐎖𐎍𐎕|t=to scorn}}, {{cog|he|קִלֵּס|tr=qilles|t=to mock}} and {{cog|gez|ተሳለቀ|t=to scoff at}} and is from {{der|ar|hit||ts=kalleš-|t=to call, to invite}} from cultic contexts.

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|II}} {{tlb|ar|rare|obsolete}}

# to receive with songs or dance performances {{+obj|ar|acc}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|II}}

===Etymology 4===
{{rfe|ar|Not sure if the same as 3 – 4 is included in Wehr while the former is from the time of ʿumar.}}

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|II}}

# to [[bow]] [[before]] {{+obj|ar|:لِ}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|II}}

===Etymology 5===
Apparently {{onomatopoeic|ar|nocap=1}}.

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|I/a~i.pass.vn:قَلْس,قَلَس}}

# to [[belch]], to [[regurgitate]], to [[vomit]]
#* {{RQ:Sunna|bulugh|1|89
|passage=مَنْ أَصَابَهُ قَيْءٌ أَوْ رُعَافٌ, أَوْ قَلَسٌ, أَوْ مَذْيٌ فَلْيَنْصَرِفْ فَلْيَتَوَضَّأْ, ثُمَّ لِيَبْنِ عَلَى صَلَاتِهِ, وَهُوَ فِي ذَلِكَ لَا يَتَكَلَّمُ
|translation=Whoever vomits, bleeds through the nose, or released seminal discharge should go, perform ablution and then go over to his prayer, and he may not speak in the process.}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|I/a~i.pass.vn:قَلْس,قَلَس}}

