Page 15081 قلقاس: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Bambouseraie de Prafrance 20100904 006.jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|قُلْقَاس}}|upright=1.15]]
{{slim-wikipedia|lang=ar}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|قُلْقَاص}}
* {{alter|ar|قُرْقَاص}} dubiously read and mistranslated in {{RQ:Ibn al-ʿawwām|24|9|volume=2|page=212|instead=1}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|syc|ܩܠܩܐܣ|tr=qōlqās}}, {{m|syc|ܩܠܘܩܣ|tr=qōlqōs}}, {{m|syc|ܩܠܘܩܘܣ|tr=qōlōqōs}}, {{m|syc|ܩܠܩܐ|tr=qalqā, qelqā|t=taro}}, still attested in {{cog|tru|ܩܰܠܩܳܐ|t=a kind of cardoon or artichoke}}, {{cog|sem-nna|-}} {{m|arc|ܩܰܠܩܳܐ|tr=qalqa}} and in some dialects {{m|arc|ܩܰܩܢܳܐ|tr=qaqna}}, which also explains {{cog|fa|کنگر|tr=kangar|t=thistle; acanthus}}, and also in {{cog|he-mis|קולקס}}, {{m|he|קרקס|t=taro}}.

This is said to be from {{der|ar|egy|-}}, as the {{cog|grc|κολοκασία}} and {{cog|la|colocasia}} meaning taro is known to be transferred only from the 4<sup>th</sup> century AD from the meaning of [[sacred lotus]] which also has an edible rhizome, but {{cog|cop|ⲕⲟⲗⲧⲁϧ}}, {{m|cop|ⲕⲟⲗⲅⲁⲥ}}, {{m|cop|ⲕⲟⲣⲕⲁⲥⲓ}} is reckoned as from Hebrew or Arabic.

The origin is then {{cog|sem|-}} at least ultimately in view of {{cog|akk|qulqulliānu}}, {{m|akk|qulqullânu|t=a type of cassia?}}, {{cog|uga|𐎖𐎍𐎖𐎍|pos=''some medicinal plant also fed to horses''}}, {{cog|he|קְלוֹקֵל|tr=qəlōqēl|t=inferior, poor; miserable food}}, {{cog|ar|قِلْقِل|t=some kind of hard-grained plant}}, {{m|ar|قُلَاقِل|t={{vern|devil-bean}}, {{taxlink|Crotalaria retusa|species}}}}, the latter and {{m|ar|قُلْقُل}} also meaning “agile, exuberant”, evidently all just reduplications of the root {{ar-root|ق ل ل|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|قُلْقَاس}}
** {{IPA|acw|/ɡul.ɡaːs/|[ɡʊl.ɡaːs]|a=Hijazi}}

