Page 15163 قنطر: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{m|ar|قِنْطَار|t={{l|en|hundredweight}}}}. Together the words form a root {{ar-root|ق ن ط ر}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|قَنْطَرَ}}

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|Iq}}

# to [[heap up]] great possessions; to own great wealth
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|3:14
|passage=زُيِّنَ لِلنَّاسِ حُبُّ ٱلشَّهَوَاتِ مِنَ ٱلنِّسَاءِ وَالْبَنِينَ وَٱلْقَنَاطِيرِ ٱلْمُقَنْطَرَةِ مِنَ ٱلذَّهَبِ وَٱلْفِضَّةِ وَٱلْخَيْلِ ٱلْمُسَوَّمَةِ وَٱلْأَنْعَامِ وَٱلْحَرْثِ ذٰلِكَ مَتَاعُ ٱلْحَيَاةِ ٱلدُّنْيَا وَٱللّٰهُ عِنْدَهُ حُسْنُ ٱلْمَآبِ
|translation=Beautified for men is the love of things they covet, women, children, heaped-up wealth of gold and silver, branded beautiful horses, cattle and cropland. This is the pleasure of the present world’s life, but Allah has the finest return with Him.
|transliteration=zuyyina li-n-nāsi ḥubbu š-šahawāti mina n-nisāʾi wālbanīna wal-qanāṭīri l-muqanṭarati mina ḏ-ḏahabi wal-fiḍḍati wal-ḵayli l-musawwamati wal-ʾanʿāmi wal-ḥarṯi ḏālika matāʿu l-ḥayāti d-dunyā wal-lāhu ʿindahu ḥusnu l-maʾābi}}

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|Iq}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{m|ar|قَنْطَرَة|t={{l|en|arch}}; {{l|en|vault}}}}. Together the words form a root {{ar-root|ق ن ط ر}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|قَنْطَرَ}}

====Verb====
{{ar-verb|Iq}}

# to [[vault]], to [[arch]] over

=====Conjugation=====
{{ar-conj|Iq}}

-------- end text --------
Page 15164 قنطرة: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Faculty of Science (University of Khartoum) 003.jpg|thumb|right|{{l-self|ar|قنطرة|قَنَاطِر|tr=-|t={{l|en|arcade}}}}]]

===Etymology===
Multiple confounded etymologies:
* Via {{bor|ar|syc|ܩܶܢܛܪܳܘܢ|tr=qenṭrōn}} from {{der|ar|grc|κέντρον|t=center}}{{ar-root|ق ن ط ر|notext=1}}, from the idea of an arch vaulting a central point.
* From {{bor|ar|syc|ܩܛܰܪܬܴܐ|tr=qəṭartā|t=binding, node, conjunction; ''specifically also'' bridge}} from the root {{syc-root|ܩ|ܛ|ܪ}} related to binding.
* From {{bor|ar|la|cintrum}}, its plural {{m|la|cintra}}, a vulgar form of {{m|la|cinctūra|t=girthing}} employed as such in Medieval Latin and the source of {{cog|fr|cintre|t=curve, round arch}} – which would ascribe to Arabic the first attestation of this Romance form, the 6<sup>th</sup>-century poet {{w|Tarafa|Ṭarafa}}’s vers {{lang|ar|كقنطرة الروميّ أقسم رَبّها لتْكتَنَفَنَ حتى تشاد بِقَرْمَد}}.
* From {{bor|ar|la|canthērius}} – {{der|ar|grc|κανθήλιος}} –, meaning a “[[gelding]]” and because of this a “beast of burden” and then “[[trellis]]; [[chevron]]s, [[spar]]s, [[gantry]]” ({{m|de|Gebälk}}, {{m|de|Sparrwerk}}, {{m|de|Gerüst}}), compare for such development the etymologies of {{m|en|chevron}}, {{m|en|chevalet}}, {{m|en|gantry}}, {{m|en|easel}}.
* A genuine formation from the root {{ar-root|ق ط ر}} from which {{m|ar|قَطَرَ|t=to put (camels) into a file}}, {{m|ar|قِطَار|t=train}}, the {{m|ar|ن}} being not a rare augment.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|قَنْطَرَة}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|قَنْطَرَة|f|pl=قَنَاطِر}}

# [[arch]], [[vault]]
# [[bridge]]
## [[viaduct]]
## [[aqueduct]]
## [[barrage]], [[dam]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|قَنْطَرَة|pl=قَنَاطِر}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ar|قَنْطَرَ|t=to {{l|en|vault}}, to {{l|en|arch}} over}}

====Descendants====
