* {{desc|bej|kunfed|bor=1}}
* {{desc|gez|ቍንፍዝ|tr=ḳʷənfəz|bor=1|alts=1}}
* {{desc|ssy|qunfid|bor=1}}
* {{desc|tig|ቅንፍዝ|tr=ḳənfəz|bor=1}}
* {{desc|ti|ቅንፍዝ|tr=ḳənfəz|bor=1}}
* {{desc|ota|قنفذ|tr=kunfuz|bor=1}}

===References===
* {{R:CAL|qwpd%232|N}}
* {{R:CAL|qwpd)|N}}, an owl
* {{R:CAL|qpwp|N}}, an owl
* {{R:CAL|qpd|V}}
* {{R:sem-pro:SED|volume=2|pages=181–183, Nr. 133}}, where they do not dare to gloss {{cog|xeb|𒄀𒁀𒋧|tr=qi₃-pa₂-šum₂}} unlike the other terms. Page LXIV they own that with that the reconstruction of a proto-form becomes impossible.
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=3|page=507}}
* {{R:Hommel-Säugethiere|page=339}}
* {{R:ar:Lane|page=2569}}
* {{R:tig:Leslau:1982|page=60}}, where Geʿez, Tigrinya, Tigre, Amharic are declared loanwords from Arabic.
* {{R:gez:Leslau-Comp.|page=434b}}, where Geʿez, Tigrinya, Tigre, are declared loanwords from Arabic, and the roll-up derivation is recognized.
* {{R:sem:Růžička:1909|page=133}}, where the roll-up derivation is recognized.
* {{R:he:Wagner|page=102}}, recognizes the roll-up derivation and deems, with reservation because of it being a native animal, the Hebrew word, which also means a bird, borrowed from Aramaic, noting that the correspondence ''ḏ'' in Arabic to ''d'' in Hebrew and ''d'' in Aramaic is only possible by borrowing as inherited ''*ḏ'' gives ''ḏ'' in Arabic, ''z'' in Hebrew, ''d'' in Aramaic (''ḏ'' in begedkefet), and indeed {{cog|he|קִפּוֹז|tr=qippṓz}} occurs {{w|Book of Isaiah|Isaiah}} 34,15 as the name of a snake, assumed {{vern|European whipsnake}}, {{taxlink|Dolichophis jugularis|species}}, or alternatively the [[Eurasian scops owl]], {{taxfmt|Otus scops|species}}, or the [[tawny owl]], {{taxfmt|Strix aluco|species}}, derived from {{m|he|קָפַץ|tr=qāfáṣ}}, {{m|ar|قَفَزَ|t=to jump up}}.
* {{R:ar:Wahrmund|volume=2|page=532a}}

