Page 15344 كاشم: -------- begin text --------
{{wp|lang=ar||''Levisticum'' gen.|mul=طرديلن|mullabel=''Tordylium'' gen.}}

===Etymology===
The pattern {{m|ar||فَاعَل}}, which is further expounded at {{m|ar|عَالَم}}, especially in variation with {{m|ar||فَاعِل}}, makes the term liable to being deemed a borrowing from {{bor|ar|arc|-}}. So probably a metathesis of the [[active participle]] of {{m|arc|כמש}} / {{m|syc|ܟܡܫ|tr=kmaš|t=to be wrinkled}}, which else derives {{m|syc|ܟܡܫܐ|tr=kamšā|t=grape}}, {{m|syc|ܟܡܫܘܢܐ|tr=kamšōnē|t=the dried skins of grapes, raisins}}. The sequence {{m|ar|مِش}} in unattested intermediate {{m|ar||*كَامِش}} was probably inacceptable in Arabic.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|كَاشِم|كَاشَم}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|كَاشِم|head2=كَاشَم|m}}

# {{vern|mountain stone-parsley}} ({{taxlink|Seseli libanotis|species}})
#: {{syn|ar|أَنْجُدَان رُومِيّ|كَرَفْس جَبَلِيّ}}
# [[lovage]] ({{taxfmt|Levisticum officinale|species}})
#: {{syn|ar|زَوْفَرَا}}
# {{vern|officinal hartwort}}, {{taxlink|Tordylium officinale|species}}, or {{vern|Syrian hartwort}}, {{taxlink|Tordylium syriacum|species}}

====Usage notes====
The plants referred to by this word are confusable and practically indistinguishable for premodern people.

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|head2=كَاشَم|كَاشِم}}

===References===
* {{R:CAL|kmš|V}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=4|page=39}}
* {{R:arc:Löw-Flora|volume=3|pages=470–473}}

{{C|ar|Celery family plants}}
-------- end text --------
