===Etymology 2===
The shape ''KāLūM'' and the variant form vocalized in three ways especially as ending with ''ā'' suggests an {{bor|ar|arc|-}} origin; attested in a broader meaning in {{cog|syc|ܟܘܦܪܐ|tr=kuppārā|t=sindon; cover of a calyx}} and in Qumranic and {{cog|tmr|כופרא|tr=kuppārā|t=palm spadix or spathe}}, and {{cog|tmr|גופרא|tr=guppārā|t=inflorescence of a palm}}, {{cog|myz|ࡂࡅࡐࡀࡓࡀ|t=inflorescence of a palm}}, {{cog|syc|ܓܘܦܪܐ|tr=guppārā|t=inflorescence also of a palm}}. But still these are deemed foreign by {{w|Theodor Nöldeke|Nöldeke}} following [https://gedsh.bethmardutho.org/entry/Bar-Ali-Isho Bar ʿAlī] and are probably formed in yet another {{cog|sem|-}} language, connecting to the Arabic root {{ar-root|ك ف ر}} and {{ar-root|غ ف ر|nocat=1}} related to “covering”.
{{etymid|ar|bract}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|كَافُور|m|pl=كَوافِير}}

# [[bract]] of the [[inflorescence]] of the [[date palm]]
#: {{alti|ar|كُفَرَّى|جُفَرَّى}}

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|كَافُور|pl=كَوافِير}}

===References===
{{rel-top|click to show literature concerning either term}}
* {{R:CAL|k)pwr|N}}
* {{R:CAL|kwpr|N}}
* {{R:CAL|kwpr)%232|N}} (CAL misses the Syriac at {{R:syc:Brockelmann 1995|page=341a}}, giving only the “dregs” meaning above it)
* {{R:CAL|gwpr|N}} (and {{R:syc:Brockelmann 1995|page=129a}} already proposed Aramaic origin of the Arabic “bract” word. Another Syriac meaning he gives as {{m|la|spūma maris}} = {{m|la|pumice}} would be an ancient equation for ''coral'' which is an animal however comparable to a palm inflorescence and not “a plant name” as in CAL)
* {{RQ:Janah:2020|page=398|lang=en|section=211 (fol. 19v,15–17)|instead=1}}
* {{R:akk:Fonahn:1907|column=640}}
* {{R:ar:Fraenkel|page=147}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=4|page=47b|entry=قافور}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=3|page=479b}}
* {{R:arc:Löw-Flora|volume=2|page=335}}
* {{R:myz:Nöldeke|page=112 § 95 footnote 1}}, apparently the first to claim its Aramaic origin.
* {{R:ar:Vollers-Beitr.|part=II|page=616}}
{{rel-bottom}}

