Page 15406 كبش: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
A term of cultic significance, borrowed into Arabic from {{bor|ar|arc|-}}, as shown by the consonantism. The late occurrence of {{cog|akk|kabsum|𒆏𒋢|tr=kabsu|t=young male sheep}} also implies Aramaic borrowing. Compare {{cog|sqt|كبڛ|tr=kobŝ|t=ram}}.

{{rel-top|Relation with {{cog|syc|ܟܒܫܐ|tr=keḇšā|t=wether}}}}
Nöldeke and Fraenkel assumed that {{cog|syc|ܟܒܫܐ|tr=keḇšā|t=wether}} represented a borrowing from Arabic. This hypothesis was put forth because the /š/ would have been irregular if both the Syriac and Arabic term descended from a shared form with {{noncog|sem-wes-pro||*ś|ts=ɬ}}. This idea was thence repeated in Syriac and Mandaic reference works until the 21st century. However, this cannot be correct, since we would have expected a vocalization {{m|syc||*ܟܰܒ݂ܫܳܐ|tr=kaḇšā}} - rather than {{m|syc||ܟܶܒ݂ܫܳܐ|tr=keḇšā}} - if it had been borrowed from Arabic. Additionally, the Syriac term was already borrowed as {{cog|xcl|քօշ|քաւշ}} in the 5th century.

Instead, the Syriac form is closer to {{cog|he|כֶּבֶשׂ|tr=kéḇeś|t=sheep}} and {{cog|phn|𐤊𐤁𐤔|t=sheep}}.

A potential cognate outside of Semitic is {{cog|xmf|ქაბლა|t=calf}}. Interestingly, {{m+|xmf|ლ}} (corresponding to {{m+|sva|შ}}) can reflect {{cog|ccs-pro||*lʿ|ts=ɬ}}, close in pronunciation to {{noncog|sem-wes-pro||*ś|ts=ɬ}}.
{{rel-bottom}}
{{rfv-etym|ar}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|كَبْش}}

