Page 16125 لصف: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|أَصَف|نَصَف}}

===Etymology===
A metathesized borrowing from {{bor|ar|arc|-}}, since, while in {{cog|he|-}}, in which the standard term for the caper is {{m|he|צָלָף|tr=ṣālā́p̄}}, the root is unused, the {{m+|arc|-}} root {{m|arc|צ־ל־ף}} / {{syc-root-entry|ܨ|ܠ|ܦ}} forms meanings related to splittings and cracks, which refers to the bursting ripening [[caperberry]]; though the pertinent noun meaning a caper is only scarcely attested in {{cog|arc|-}} as {{m|arc|נִצְפָא|tr=niṣp̄ā}} and {{m|syc|ܢܹܝܨܦܵܐ|tr=nēṣp̄ā}} – a distortion of the consonants least common for this etymon in Arabic – because this native Aramaic term had been ousted by the {{m|ar|كَبَر|id=caper}} type word for the caper. The {{m+|ar|شَفَلَّح|t=capper}} can also plausibly be derived from a root for splittings and cracks.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|لَصَف}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|لَصَف|m}}

# {{C|ar|Brassicales order plants|Spices and herbs}} [[caper]] {{gloss|{{taxfmt|Capparis|genus}} gen. and especially {{taxfmt|Capparis spinosa|species}}, as well as the edible buds from it}}
#: {{syn|ar|كَبَر|شَفَلَّح|عِتْر|id1=caper}}
#* {{RQ:Qazwiniy:1849|1|pages=296–297}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|لَصَف}}

===References===
* {{R:CAL|clp|V}}
* {{R:xaa:ELA|II|page=1151}}, knows also {{m|ar|لَصِيف|t=[[artichoke thistle]] (''[[Cynara cardunculus]]''), {{taxlink|Cynara baetica|species}}, {{vern|common golden thistle}} (''[[Scolymus hispanicus]]'')}} and derives from {{ar-root|ص ف ف}} without giving a reason or what would be exactly in rows.
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=4|page=104}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=3|page=993}}
* {{R:arc:Löw-Flora|volume=1|pages=322–331}}
* {{R:arc:Löw-Pflanzen|pages=262–265}}
-------- end text --------
