===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|مَالْطَة|مَالِطَة}}<ref>{{cite-journal|year=2016|author=Martin R. Zammit|title=''Ḍuriba bi-Mālṭa'' ‘Minted in Malta’: deciphering the Kufic legend on the Fāṭimid quarter dinar|journal=Melita Classica|volume=3|page=210|publisher=Malta Classics Association|url=https://classicsmalta.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/12.-Duriba-bi-Malta.-Minted-in-Malta.-deciphering-the-Kufic-legend-on-the-Fatimid-quarter-dinar.-M.R.-Zammit.-MC-3-2016-209-216.pdf|passage=In Arabic, the word ''Māliṭa'' can be written, or inscribed, either with two ''’alifs'',one after the letter {{IPAchar|/m/}} and a final ''’alif'' (مـالطـا), or with an ''’alif'' after {{IPAchar|/m/}} and a final ''tā’ marbūṭa'' (مـالطـة).}}</ref><ref>{{R:ar:Wehr-4}}</ref>

===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|la|Melita}}, from {{der|ar|grc|Μελίτη}}, of disputed origin. The modern form without medial {{IPAchar|/i/}} is apparently due to the influence of {{cog|en|Malta}} or {{cog|mt|Malta}}, although {{cog|la|Malta}} is attested in medieval manuscripts of the ''{{w|Antonine Itineraries}}''.

