Page 17273 مرطبان: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Tursucu Sahne sokak beyoglu Istanbul 0644.jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|مَرْطَبَانَات}}|upright=1.5]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|بَرْطَمَان}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|ar|mnw|}}, a small port town in [[Burma]] today called {{w|Mottama}}, the name of which, under the reign of the {{w|Kingdom of Pegu}}, in the fifteenth to seventeenth century was used to denote glazed earthenware jars sent to influential persons, [[Martaban jar]]s. The term came to denote more dissimilar types of jars so that it now means merely mundane canning jars across languages.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|مَرْطَبَان}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|مَرْطَبَان|m|pl=مَرْطَبَانَات}}

# [[jar]], [[kitchen]] [[receptacle]], [[spice]] [[box]], or a [[casket]] for keeping medicines {{gloss|of [[glass]]}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|مَرْطَبَان|pl=sfp}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:ar:Dozy|volume=4|page=582}}
* {{cite-book|editor=Sila Tripati|last=Simões|first=Sara|chapter=A brief study concerning martaban jars|url=https://www.academia.edu/7089498/A_brief_study_concerning_martaban_jars|year=2010|title=Maritime Contacts of the Past: Deciphering Connections amongst Communities|publisher=Delta Book World|location=New Delhi|isbn=978-819262443-3}}

{{C|ar|Vessels}}
{{cln|ar|terms derived from toponyms}}
