Page 17284 مرقاق: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{ar-tool noun}} from the root {{ar-root|ر ق ق}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|مِرْقَاق}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|مِرْقَاق|m}}

# [[rolling pin]]
#: {{syn|ar|شَوْبَك|فَرَّادَة}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|مِرْقَاق}}

{{C|ar|Kitchenware}}
-------- end text --------
Page 17285 مرقة: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
{{wp|lang=ar}}
Compare {{cog|jpa|מרקחתה|tr=mrqḥtā|t=a spiced dish}}, in the absolute state {{m|arc|מרקחה|tr=mrqḥā}}, and {{cog|he|מִרְקַחַה|tr=mirqaḥā|t=spice seasoning, ointment, jam}}, but also {{cog|he|מָרָק|tr=mārāq|t=soup, broth}}, and finally the {{cog|phn|-}} loanword {{cog|grc|ἀμόργη|t=amurca, dregs of oil}}.

The forms mentioned at {{m|ar|مَرَخَ|t=to rub, to anoint}} and some meanings of the root formula {{ar-root|م ر ق|nocat=1}} related to maceration—though they have to be cautioned against as potentially denominal—vaguely seem to relate to the meaning of a broth or stock, however it is formally difficult to etiologize the term with these or any other root beginning with ''m'', when accounting for the fuller forms in Aramaic and Hebrew, so that there rather has been a ''mi-'' prefixation in {{cog|sem-nwe}}, although ''r-q-ḥ'' would appear to be an obscure root, which would require to postulate a deformation from another root (perhaps one relating to {{m|ar|مَرَخَ}}). A further marker is the variation {{m|ar|مَرَقَة}}, {{m|ar|مَرَق}}, also {{m|ar|مَرْقَة}}, {{m|ar|مَرْق}}, which seems to mirror the above expressed one of the {{bor|ar|arc|-}} states. A viable root is then {{m|he|ר־ו־ק|tr=r-w-q}}, {{m|he|ר־י־ק|tr=r-y-q}} related to “pouring out”, present in Arabic as {{ar-root|ر و ق|nocat=1}}, {{ar-root|ر ي ق|nocat=1}} and irregular {{m|ar|هَرَقَ}}.

For borrowings in the lexical field of kinds of stock regard {{m|ar|صِير|t=brine}}, {{m|ar|بُنّ|t=brine|id=backfrombynni}}, {{m|ar|مُرِّيّ|t=a salty sauce created by rotting cereals}}, or {{m|ar|إِطْرِيَة|t=thin noodles cooked in broth}}, {{m|ar|شُورْبَة}} ~ {{m|ar|شُرْبَة|t=soup}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|ar|مَرَق}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{ar-IPA|مَرَقَة}}

====Noun====
{{ar-noun|مَرَقَة|f}}

# [[broth]], [[stock]], [[gravy]], [[sauce]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-noun|مَرَقَة}}

===Etymology 2===
{{ar-rootbox|م ر ق}}

====Noun====
{{ar-sing-noun|مِرْقَة|coll=مِرْق}}

# piece of [[fetid]] [[wool]]

=====Declension=====
{{ar-decl-sing-noun|مِرْقَة}}

===References===
* {{R:CAL|mrqxh|N}}
* {{R:xaa:ELA|II|page=1194}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=4|pages=171b–172a}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=2|pages=1094b–1095b}}
* {{R:CEDHL|page=387a}}
* {{R:ar:Steingass|page=988a–b}}
* {{R:ar:Wehr-5-de|pages=1200a–b}}
* {{cite-book|last=Welch|year=2017|first=Eric Lee|location=Leiden|publisher=Brill|chapter=ἀμόργη/Amurca: A Semitic Loanword?|doi=10.1163/9789004194557_009|title=Cultural Contact and Appropriation in the Axial-Age Mediterranean World. A Periplos|series=Culture and History of the Ancient Near East|seriesvolume=86|editors=Baruch Halpern, Kenneth S. Sacks|pages=125–128}}

