Page 18253 ملاط: -------- begin text --------
[[File:Lisbon Portugal 381 Castle of São Jorge (5108592242).jpg|thumb|right|{{lang|ar|مِلَاط}}|upright=1.15]]
{{wp|lang=ar}}

===Etymology===
{{bor|ar|arc|-}} borrowing, found as {{cog|tmr|הַמְלָטָא|tr=hamlāṭā|t=''disputed meaning'': mortar; row of bricks; beam}}, {{cog|syc|ܡܠܴܛܳܐ|tr=mlāṭā|t=mortar}}. Also found as {{cog|he|מֶלֶט|tr=meleṭ|t=mortar}} in the {{w|Book of Jeremiah}}, 43:9, {{cog|axm|մաղթ|t=a designation of various natural gums and resins}}, as well as {{cog|grc|[[μάλθα]], [[μάλθη]]|t=a kind of mixture of tar and wax for caulking ships and coating wax tablets}}, whence {{cog|la|maltha|t=a kind of natural tar; a kind of varnish or putty for coating containers}}. {{w|Pliny the Elder}} describes the natural tar ''maltha'' in his {{w|Natural History (Pliny)|''Natural History''}} 2:235 as a substance oozed up from pools at [[Samosata]] and used by the [[Commagene]]s to defend Samosata’s walls by pouring it on opponents, sticking on them and kindled by water; so its origin must be sought in a {{der|ar|qfa-sub}} there.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|مِلَاط}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|مِلَاط|m|pl=مُلُط}}

# the [[binding agent]] used for [[construct]]ing [[building]]s, [[mortar]], [[plaster]]

