Page 18937 ناسك: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Likely borrowed from a base stem active participle of the matching {{bor|ar|arc|-}} root meaning in the widespread base stem “to pour out”, “to cast” – of which sense in the passive participle form {{m|ar|نَسِيك|t=ingot of gold or silver}} derives as a separate borrowing from Aramaic – and in a technical [[cultic]] sense “to [[libate]]”, a root related to the Arabic {{ar-root|ن ز ع|nocat=1}}; considering also that the other terms within the root formula {{m|ar|نَسَكَ|t=to lead a devout or virtuous or ascetic life}}, {{m|ar|تَنَسَّكَ|t=to be devout or virtuous or ascetic}},  {{m|ar|نَُِسك|tr=nask, nusk, nisk|t=devotion to a god}}, {{m|ar|مَنْسَِك|tr=mansak, mansik|t=place of ritual, stead frequented for ceremony}} have most probably been contrived on the model of the present term.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|نَاسِك}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|نَاسِك|m|pl=نُسَّاك}}

# [[ascetic]]

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|نَاسِك|pl=نُسَّاك}}

===References===
* {{R:CAL|nsk%232|V}}
* {{R:ar:Fraenkel|page=152}}
* {{R:ar:Baalbaki}}
* {{R:ar:Baranov}}
* {{R:xaa:ELA|II|page=1260}}, imagining it instead cognate
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=4|page=275}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=2|page=1251}}
* {{R:ar:Lane}}
* {{R:ar:Wehr-3}}
* {{R:ar:Wehr-4|page=1129}}

{{C|ar|Occupations}}
-------- end text --------
