Page 18955 ناطف: -------- begin text --------
{{wikipedia|lang=ar}}

===Etymology===
Either from the root {{ar-root|ن ط ف}} related to dribbling, spilling, because of the manner cream is created, or an {{bor|ar|arc|-}} borrowing, since [[soapwort]] has been used to make a foam sugared and used as a surrogate for [[egg white]] [[foam]] ([[meringue]]) or [[whipped cream]],<ref>{{R:arc:Löw-Flora|volume=1|page=649}}</ref> and the Aramaic root cognate to {{ar-root|nocat=1|ن ظ ف}} would be {{m|arc|נ־ט־ף|tr=n-ṭ-p̄}} which however just means “to drip”, perhaps the source of the uncommon Arabic root. The Iberian {{m|es|turrón}} type of candy is claimed to be a material borrowing from Arabic practice.<ref>{{cite-book|title=Sweet Delights from a Thousand and One Nights: The Story of Traditional Arab Sweets|year=2013|location=London and New York|publisher=I. B. Tauris|page=157|last=Salloum|authorlink=Habeeb Salloum|first=Habeeb|last2=Salloum|first2=Muna|last3=Salloum Elias|first3=Leila|isbn=9781780764641|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=qcPESEWrDF0C&pg=PA157}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|نَاطِف}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|نَاطِف|m|pl=-}} {{tlb|ar|Syria|Iraq|Palestine|Egypt}}

# a kind of [[nougat]] or [[hard candy]] from [[cream]] or [[foam]] hardened with [[sugar]]
#: {{syn|ar|قُبَّيْط}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-noun|نَاطِف}}

===References===
<references/>

{{C|ar|Sweets}}
-------- end text --------
