Page 19317 نعل: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
{{ar-rootbox|ن ع ل}}
{{rfc|ar|etymology}}
* Compare for the “shoe” noun {{cog|syc|ܢܥܠܐ|tr=naʿlā}}, {{cog|he|נַעַל|tr=náʿal}}
* Perhaps related to {{cog|gez|ንዑልት}}, {{m|gez|ኑዑልት|t=widow married by {{l|en|levirate marriage}}}} because, according to Dillmann, the ceremony of the levirate marriage seems to involve the bridegroom throwing a shoe in the biblical passages where it is found – but this Ethiopic word is perhaps from {{noncog|cus}}; Cushitic was unknown in Dillmann’s time, which forced Semitic explanations of Ethiopic.
* Likely related to {{cog|akk|𒈿|tr=nâlu, niālu|t=to lie down}} and its derivations related to “spreading”
* In the view of this general “spreading” meaning in Akkadian, likely related to {{cog|uga|𐎐𐎓𐎍}} which is variously glossed: “palanquin”, “sandal”, “robe”
* ➜ Apparently the later Semitic languages have specialised a “spreading” meaning to a “shoeing” meaning

====Pronunciation 1====
* {{ar-IPA|نَعَلَ}}

=====Verb=====
{{ar-verb|I/a~a.vn:نَعْل}}

# to [[sole]], to [[shoe]]

======Conjugation======
{{ar-conj|I/a~a.vn:نَعْل}}

====Pronunciation 2====
* {{ar-IPA|نَعِلَ}}

=====Verb=====
{{ar-verb|I/i~a.nopass.vn:نَعَل}}

# to be [[shoe]]d

======Conjugation======
{{ar-conj|I/i~a.nopass.vn:نَعَل}}

====Pronunciation 3====
* {{ar-IPA|نَعَّلَ}}

=====Verb=====
{{ar-verb|II}}

# to [[sole]], to [[shoe]]

======Conjugation======
{{ar-conj|II}}

====Pronunciation 4====
* {{ar-IPA|نَعْل}}

=====Noun=====
{{ar-noun|نَعْل|m|pl=نِعَال|pl2=أَنْعُل}}

