Page 19524 نياحة: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{bor|ar|syc|ܢܝܚܬܐ|tr=nəyāḥtā|t=quiet; rest}}, where the root {{syc-root-entry|ܢ|ܘ|ܚ}} relating to “rest”, as Arabic {{ar-root|ن و خ|nocat=1}}, was figuratively extended for becoming quiet and sad minutes of silence.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|نِياحة}}

===Noun===
{{ar-noun|نِياحة|f}}

# [[lamentation]], [[wailing]], loud [[beweep]]ing of a [[deceased]]
# {{lb|ar|Egypt|Coptic|_|Christianity|euphemistic}} [[death]]
#: {{syn|ar|وَفاة|مَوْت}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ar|نَاحَ|t=to wail}}
* {{l|ar|نُوَاح|t=a wailing; wailings}}
* {{l|ar|نَائِحَة|t=a wailing woman}}
* {{l|ar|نَوَّاحَة}}, {{l|ar|نَوَّاح|t=wailer, mourner}}
* {{l|ar|مَناح}}, {{l|ar|مَنَاحَة|t=wailing, mourning}}
* {{l|ar|نَاوَحَ|t=to be faced to (seemingly while wailing together)}}
* {{l|ar|تَنَيَّحَ|t=}}
* {{l|ar|تَنَوَّحَ|t=to dangle}}
* {{l|ar|نَنَاوَحَ|t=to warble; to howl}}
* {{l|ar|اِسْتَنَاحَ|t=to howl, to wail as to induce others to wail}}

====See also====
* {{l|ar|مَأْتَم|t=staying, biding; assembly for rejoicing or mourning}}
