Page 19895 هيه: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ar|هي}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ar|sem-pro|*šiʔa}}.<br>Although the [[third person|Third person]] [[feminine|Feminine]] [[Singular]] [[personal pronoun|Personal pronoun]] {{m|ar|هِيَ}} occurs 63 times in [[Qur'an]], this [[ه|hāʔ]]-suffixed form occurs only once sentence-finally, making it a [[hapax legomenon|Hapax Legomenon]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|هِيَهْ}}

===Pronoun===
{{ar-pron|هِيَهْ|f}}

# {{lb|ar|[[Qur'anic]]|_|hapax legomenon}} [[it]] {{gloss|subject pronoun, referring to inanimate nouns of feminine gender, fire in this instance}}
#: {{also|هي}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|101|10-11
|passage=[[وَ]][[ما|مَآ]] [[أدرى|أَدْرَىٰ]][[كَ]] [[مَا]] '''هِيَهْ''' ۝ [[نَارٌ]] [[حام|حَامِيَةٌۢ]] ۝|tr=wamā ʔadrāka mā '''hiyah'''. nārun ḥāmiyatun.|translation=And what can make you know what '''that''' is? (It is) a fire, intensely hot.}}

====Note====
* Modern translations and interpretations almost universally translate this word as “that”.

====See also====
{{ar-personal pronouns}}
-------- end text --------
Page 19896 هيهات: -------- begin text --------

===Verb===
{{head|ar|verb|head=هَيْهَاتَ}}

# it is far; it is [[impossible]]; [[no way]]
#: {{ux|ar|t='''It is far from possible''' for us to be in the condition of humiliating dependence. (A Refusal to Surrender)|'''هَيْهَاتَ''' مِنَّا الذِّلَّةُ}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|23:36
|passage='''هَيْهَاتَ هَيْهَاتَ''' لِمَا تُوعَدُونَ
|translation=Far beyond far it is (far-fetched), for what you are set to expect.}}
#* {{quote-text|ar|passage='''فَهَيْهَاتَ''' '''هَيْهَاتَ''' الْعَقِيقُ وَمَنْ بِهِ / وَ'''هَيْهَاتَ''' خِلٌّ بِالْعَقِيقِ نُوَاصِلُهْ}}

====Usage notes====
This word belongs to a special group of words known in Arabic as {{l|ar|أَسْمَاء أَفْعَال}}, plural of {{l|ar|اِسْم فِعْل}}, literally ''noun verbs''. They act grammatically as nouns more than verbs, but carry the function of a verb. They do not conjugate, but each word signify a grammatical time on its own, in this case the past/perfective. However, they are different from nouns in that they do not receive declensions and inflections (are in the {{l|ar|مَبْنِي}} state), and some (but not all) do not allow possessive enclitics.

[[Category:Arabic defective verbs]]
-------- end text --------
