Page 20326 ي: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===

====Pronunciation====
* {{sense|letter name}} {{ar-IPA|ياء}}
* {{sense|phoneme}} {{IPA|ar|/j/|/iː/}} {{q|in Literary Arabic}}
* {{sense|phoneme}} {{IPA|ar|/j/|/i(ː)/|/e(ː)/}} {{q|in Arabic dialects and loanwords}}

====Letter====
{{head|ar|letter|head='''ي '''/''' ي‍ـ‍ '''/''' ‍ـي‍ـ '''/''' ـي'''|tr=yāʔ}}

# {{n-g|The twenty-eighth and final letter of the [[Arabic]] alphabet. It is preceded by {{l|ar|و}}.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* After ''kasra'' it is pronounced as a long ''ī'' {{IPAchar|/iː/}} or ''y'' {{IPAchar|/j/}} after ''fatḥa'', ''ḍamma'' and before other vowels.
* In the final position ''alif maqṣūra'' ألف مقصورَة "restricted alif" is always written without dots ('''{{l|ar|ـى}}'''). ''alif maqṣūra'' always follows a fatḥa and is transliterated as ''ā''; see {{l|ar|ى}} for its pronunciation. It is also called ''alif layyina'' (ألف لينَة) "flexible alif"
* The traditional usage remains in Egypt and Sudan, for which the final ''yāʾ'' is written without dots and is visually identical to ''alif maqṣūra''.

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|fa|ی}}

====Symbol====
{{head|ar|numeral symbol|head='''ي '''/''' ي‍ـ‍ '''/''' ‍ـي‍ـ '''/''' ـي'''|tr=yāʔ}}

# {{n-g|The tenth letter in traditional [[abjad]] order, which is used in place of numerals for list numbering ([[abjad numerals]]). It is preceded by {{l|ar|ط}} and followed by {{l|ar|ك}}.}}

===Etymology 2===

====Suffix====
{{head|ar|suffix|tr=-ī|head=ـِي|head2=ـِيَ|head3=ـيَ}}

# {{n-g|bound object pronoun:}} [[me]], [[my]]
#: {{suffixusex|ar|لِـ|gloss1=to|ـِي|لِي|gloss3=to me}}
#* {{RQ:Qur'an|7|105|passage=قَدْ جِئْتُكُم بِبَيِّنَةٍ مِنْ رَبِّكُمْ فَأَرْسِلْ '''مَعِيَ''' بَنِي إِسْرَائِيلَ}}

=====Usage notes=====
{{m|ar|أَنَا}} has four enclitic forms which are employed in different contexts and are generally not interchangeable. The enclitic forms {{m|ar|ـنِي}} and {{m|ar|ـنِيَ}} are attached to prepositions ending in {{m|ar|نْ}} with no final vowel (''e.g.'', {{m|ar|مِنْ}} and {{m|ar|عَنْ}}) and to verbs. They may also be added to the class of particles traditionally called "the sisters of {{m|ar|إِنَّ}}" (except {{m|ar|لَعَلَّ}}).

The forms {{m|ar||ـِي}} and {{m|ar||ـيَ}} are used elsewhere mostly interchangeably, though restrictions in metrical poetry often determine which variant is used. All short case endings before the suffix are elided (that is, those of the singular, the broken plural, and the so-called sound feminine plural), as in {{m|ar||قَوْلِي|t=my speech (nominative, accusative, or genitive)}}, {{m|ar||أَصَابِعِي|t=my fingers (nominative, accusative, or genitive)}}, and {{m|ar||مُعَلِّمَاتِي|t=my female teachers (nominative, accusative, or genitive)}}.

In cases where {{m|ar||ـِي}} would be preceded by a long vowel, as when suffixing sound masculine plural nouns, only {{m|ar||ـيَ}} is used. If the word ends in a long close vowel (that is, ''-ū'' or ''-ī''), the long close vowel assimilates to /i/ and the suffix is geminated, thus producing {{m|ar||ـِيَّ}}, as in {{m|ar||مُعَلِّمِيَّ|t=my teachers (nominative, accusative, or genitive)}} (from {{m|ar||مُعَلِّمُو|t=(the) teachers of (nominative)}} or {{m|ar||مُعَلِّمِي|t=teachers of (accusative or genitive)}}) and {{m|ar||قَاضِيَّ|t=my judge (nominative, accusative, or genitive); my judges (nominative, accusative, or genitive)}} (from {{m|ar||قَاضِي|t=(the) judge of (nominative)}} or from {{m|ar||قَاضُو|t=(the) judges of (nominative)}} or {{m|ar||قَاضِي|t=(the) judges of (accusative or genitive)}}). Therefore, when suffixed, such nouns, like nouns with short endings, are described as indeclinable in traditional Arabic grammar. However, the last long vowel is retained if it is open (that is, ''-ā''), as in {{m|ar||إِصْبَعَايَ|t=my two fingers (nominative)}} (from {{m|ar||إِصْبَعَا|t=(the) two fingers of (nominative)}}. If the word ends in the diphthong ''-aj'', {{m|ar||ـيَ}} is used, /j/ is elided, and the suffix is geminated, as in {{m|ar||إِصْبَعَيَّ|t=my two fingers (accusative or genitive)}} (from {{m|ar||إِصْبَعَيْ|t=(the) two fingers of (accusative or genitive)}}. Thus, suffixed dual nouns are distinguishable in case, unlike all the other forms.

