Page 20418 يلب: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
Apparently borrowed from {{bor|ar|arc|חלבא}} / {{m|syc|ܚܠܒܐ|tr=ḥelbā|t=fatty tissue, omentum, membrane, diaphragm}}, native Semitic cognate to the Arabic {{ar-root|خ ل ب|nocat=1}} and {{ar-root|خ ل ف|nocat=1}}, compare {{ar-root|س ل ب|nocat=1}} and {{ar-root|س ل ف|nocat=1}}, in a regiolect and chronolect where the distinction of {{IPAchar|/ʔ/}}, {{IPAchar|/ʕ/}}, {{IPAchar|/ħ/}} and {{IPAchar|/j/}} was already degenerated and the word bore the latter sound, as is the case for {{m+|ar|يَافُوخ}} and {{cog|xcl|յիմար}}. Some more general clothing names have been derived from these two root formulas in Semitic, and in particular the same Aramaic source lays claim to {{cog|xcl|հալաւ|t=garment}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ar-IPA|يَلَب}}

===Noun===
{{ar-coll-noun|يَلَب|sing=يَلَبَة}} {{tlb|ar|obsolete}}

# [[skin]] of the [[back]] of a [[wild]] [[beast]]
# [[leather]] or [[felt]] [[war]]-[[gear]], [[soft]] [[field]] [[accoutrement]]s ([[armour]] as well as [[shield]]s, and possibly [[helmet]]s)
#* {{quote-book|ar|year=a. 584|title=his {{w|Mu'allaqat|Muʿallaqa}}, a [[w:ar:معلقة عمرو بن كلثوم|well-known work]]|author={{w|Amr ibn Kulthum|ʿAmr ibn Kult̠ūm}}|url=https://poetsgate.com/poem.php?pm=11411|passage=عَلَيْنَا البَيْضُ وَٱل'''يَلَبُ''' ٱليَمَانِي<br/>وَأَسْيَافٌ يَقُمْنَ وَيَنْحَنِيْنَا<br/>عَلَيْنَا كُلُّ سَابِغَةٍ دِلَاصٍ<br/>تَرَى فَوْقَ ٱلنِّطَاقِ لَهَا غُضُوْنَا|t=We are armed with bright sabres, and clad in '''habergeons'''<br/>Our scimitars are part straight, part bent.<br/>We have coats of mail that glitter like lightning,<br/>the plaits of which are seen in wrinkles above our belts.}}

====Declension====
{{ar-decl-coll-noun|يَلَب}}

===References===
* {{R:CAL|xlb%232|N}}
* {{R:ar:Almaany}}
* {{R:ar:Dozy|volume=2|page=353b}}
* {{R:ar:Freytag|volume=4|page=522b}}
* {{R:ar:Kazimirski|volume=2|page=1633b}}
* {{R:ar:Rehatsek-SOA|pages=233–234}}

{{C|ar|Skin|Armor}}
