Page 20484 ً: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=ـً}}

# '''fatḥa tanwīn''' ({{lang|ar|فتحَة تنوين}}) or '''fatḥatān''' ({{lang|ar|فتحتان}}), a [[nunation]] (''[[tanwīn]]'' ({{lang|ar|تنوين}})) of the vowel ''a'' ({{lang|ar|ـَ}} - fatḥa), indicating the [[accusative case]] in Classical Arabic, usually used in combination with {{l|ar|ا|tr=ʔalif}} - ({{lang|ar|ـًا}}), unless it's {{l|ar|ة|tr=tāʔ marbūṭa}} {{lb|ar|{{lang|ar|ةً}}}} or {{l|ar|ء|tr=hamza}} {{lb|ar|{{lang|ar|ءً}}}}. In a non-pausal formal situation it's pronounced as "-an".

===See also===
{{ar-diacritics}}
-------- end text --------
Page 20485 ٌ: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=ـٌ}}

# '''ḍamma tanwīn''' ({{lang|ar|ضَمَّة تَنْوِين}}) or '''ḍammatān''' ({{lang|ar|ضَمَّتَان}}), a [[nunation]] (''[[tanwīn]]'' ({{lang|ar|تَنْوِين}})) of the vowel ''u'' ({{lang|ar|ـُ}} - ḍamma), indicating the [[nominative case]] in Classical Arabic. In a non-pausal formal situation it's pronounced as "-un". In dialects, relaxed (less formal) Arabic, or in [[pausa]], the pronunciation is either shortened to "-u" or ignored altogether, even when written.

===See also===
* {{l|ar|&#2289;|ـࣱ}}
{{ar-diacritics}}
-------- end text --------
Page 20486 ٍ: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=ـٍ}}

# '''kasra tanwīn''' ({{lang|ar|كَسْرَة تَنْوِين}}) or '''kasratān''' ({{lang|ar|كَسْرَتَان}}), a [[nunation]] (''[[tanwīn]]'' ({{lang|ar|تَنْوِين}})) of the vowel ''i'' ({{lang|ar|ـِ}} - kasra), indicating the [[genitive case]] in Classical Arabic. In a non-pausal formal situation it's pronounced as "-in". In dialects, relaxed (less formal) Arabic or in [[pausa]], the pronunciation is either shorted to "-i" or ignored altogether, even when written.

===See also===
{{ar-diacritics}}
-------- end text --------
Page 20487 َ: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=ـَ}}

# [[fatḥa]] ({{l|ar|فَتْحَة|tr=-}}), indicates a short {{IPAchar|/a/}} or its cognates, indicates a long {{IPAchar|/aː/}} if followed by an {{l|ar|ا|tr=ʔalif}}.

===See also===
{{ar-diacritics}}

-------- end text --------
Page 20488 ُ: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=◌ُ|tr=u}}

# [[damma|ḍamma]] &ndash; {{l|ar|ضَمَّة}} &ndash; indicates a short {{IPAchar|/u/}} or its cognates, indicates a long {{IPAchar|/uː/}} if followed by a {{l|ar|و|tr=wāw}}.

===See also===
{{ar-diacritics}}

-------- end text --------
Page 20489 ِ: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=ـِ}}

# [[kasra]] ({{l|ar|كسرَة}}), indicates a short {{IPAchar|/i/}} or its cognates, indicates a long {{IPAchar|/iː/}} if followed by a {{l|ar|ي|tr=yāʔ}}.

===See also===
{{ar-diacritics}}

-------- end text --------
Page 20490 ّ: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=◌ّ|tr=-}}

# [[shadda]] ({{l|ar|شَدَّة}}), indicates a [[geminate]] (long, double) consonant.

===See also===
{{ar-diacritics}}
-------- end text --------
Page 20491 ْ: -------- begin text --------

===Letter===
{{head|ar|letter|head=ـْ|tr=-}}

# [[sukun|sukūn]] ({{l|ar|سُكُون|tr=-}}), indicates the absence of a vowel after a consonant or the presence of a diphthong.

====Usage notes====
The word final sukūn in a vocalised Arabic text is routinely omitted even in texts designed for learners as the actual pronunciation of the final consonant may depend on the position of the word in a sentence, position of the clausal pause, level of formality and may vary from speaker to speaker. The noun inflection (and of some other parts of speech) is almost absent in all modern Arabic dialects and more relaxed forms of [[MSA]], so case usage is considered too complicated even for native Arabic speakers and not consistently taught. The texts of [[Qur'an|Qurʾān]] are a notable exception where the exact pronunciation is considered very important.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|ar|[[ـۡ]]|tr=-}} (Eastern sukun)

===See also===
{{ar-diacritics}}
-------- end text --------
